const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Windsor. Before Page's house.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Justice Shallow, Slender and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir Hugh, persuade me not. I will make a Star Chamber matter of it. If","he were twenty Sir John Falstaffs, he shall not abuse Robert Shallow,","esquire."],"id":2,"m":"Sir Hugh, don't try to talk me out of it. I will take this to the Star Chamber. Even if he were twenty Sir John Falstaffs, he will not insult Robert Shallow, esquire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["In the county of Gloucester, Justice of Peace and Coram."],"id":3,"m":"In the county of Gloucester, Justice of the Peace and Coram."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ay, cousin Slender, and Custalorum."],"id":4,"m":"Yes, cousin Slender, and Custalorum too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, and Ratolorum too; and a gentleman born, Master Parson, who writes","himself \"Armigero\" in any bill, warrant, quittance, or","obligation—\"Armigero.\""],"id":5,"m":"Yes, and Ratolorum as well; and a gentleman by birth, Master Parson, who signs himself \"Armigero\", meaning entitled to a coat of arms, on any bill, warrant, receipt or bond. \"Armigero.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ay, that I do, and have done any time these three hundred years."],"id":6,"m":"Yes, I do, and have done for the last three hundred years."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["All his successors, gone before him hath done't; and all his ancestors","that come after him may. They may give the dozen white luces in their","coat."],"id":7,"m":"All his successors who went before him have done it, and all his ancestors who come after him may. They can show the dozen white luces, the pike fish, on their coat of arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It is an old coat."],"id":8,"m":"It is an old coat."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["The dozen white louses do become an old coat well. It agrees well,","passant. It is a familiar beast to man, and signifies love."],"id":9,"m":"The dozen white lice suit an old coat well. It fits nicely, as they say in heraldry. The louse is a beast very familiar to man, and it stands for love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["The luce is the fresh fish. The salt fish is an old coat."],"id":10,"m":"The luce is the fresh fish. The salt fish is an old coat."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I may quarter, coz."],"id":11,"m":"I can add it to my own arms, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["You may, by marrying."],"id":12,"m":"You can, by marrying into it."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is marring indeed, if he quarter it."],"id":13,"m":"It will be marring indeed, if he quarters it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Not a whit."],"id":14,"m":"Not at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Yes, py'r Lady. If he has a quarter of your coat, there is but three","skirts for yourself, in my simple conjectures. But that is all one. If","Sir John Falstaff have committed disparagements unto you, I am of the","Church, and will be glad to do my benevolence to make atonements and","compremises between you."],"id":15,"m":"Yes, by our Lady. If he takes a quarter of your coat, there are only three skirts left for you, in my simple opinion. But never mind that. If Sir John Falstaff has wronged you, I am a man of the Church, and I will gladly do my good office to make peace and a settlement between you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["The Council shall hear it; it is a riot."],"id":16,"m":"The King's Council shall hear about it; it is a riot."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is not meet the Council hear a riot. There is no fear of Got in a","riot. The Council, look you, shall desire to hear the fear of Got, and","not to hear a riot. Take your vizaments in that."],"id":17,"m":"It is not fitting for the Council to hear a riot. There is no fear of God in a riot. The Council, you see, will want to hear the fear of God, not a riot. Take that into your considerations."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ha! O' my life, if I were young again, the sword should end it."],"id":18,"m":"Ha! On my life, if I were young again, the sword would settle it."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is petter that friends is the sword, and end it; and there is also","another device in my prain, which peradventure prings goot discretions","with it. There is Anne Page, which is daughter to Master George Page,","which is pretty virginity."],"id":19,"m":"It is better to let friends be the sword and settle it. And I have another plan in my head that may bring some good sense with it. There is Anne Page, daughter of Master George Page, who is a pretty young maiden."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Mistress Anne Page? She has brown hair, and speaks small like a woman?"],"id":20,"m":"Mistress Anne Page? She has brown hair and speaks softly, like a woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is that fery person for all the 'orld, as just as you will desire,","and seven hundred pounds of moneys, and gold, and silver, is her","grandsire upon his death's-bed—Got deliver to a joyful","resurrections!—give, when she is able to overtake seventeen years old.","It were a goot motion if we leave our pribbles and prabbles, and desire","a marriage between Master Abraham and Mistress Anne Page."],"id":21,"m":"That is the very person, exactly as you could wish. And her grandfather on his deathbed (God bring him to a joyful resurrection!) left her seven hundred pounds in money, gold and silver, to be hers when she reaches seventeen. It would be a good idea if we gave up our petty squabbles and arranged a marriage between Master Abraham and Mistress Anne Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Did her grandsire leave her seven hundred pound?"],"id":22,"m":"Did her grandfather leave her seven hundred pounds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Ay, and her father is make her a petter penny."],"id":23,"m":"Yes, and her father will give her a better sum still."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I know the young gentlewoman; she has good gifts."],"id":24,"m":"I know the young lady; she has good qualities."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Seven hundred pounds, and possibilities, is goot gifts."],"id":25,"m":"Seven hundred pounds, and more to come, are good qualities."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Well, let us see honest Master Page. Is Falstaff there?"],"id":26,"m":"Well, let's go and see honest Master Page. Is Falstaff there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Shall I tell you a lie? I do despise a liar as I do despise one that is","false, or as I despise one that is not true. The knight Sir John is","there, and I beseech you be ruled by your well-willers. I will peat the","door for Master Page."],"id":27,"m":"Shall I tell you a lie? I hate a liar as much as I hate someone who is false, or someone who is not true. The knight Sir John is there, and I beg you to listen to those who wish you well. I will knock at Master Page's door."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocks.","id":28},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","cont":1,"lines":["What, ho! Got pless your house here!"],"id":29,"m":"Hello there! God bless this house!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[Within] Who's there?"],"id":30,"m":"Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Here is Got's plessing, and your friend, and Justice Shallow, and here","young Master Slender, that peradventures shall tell you another tale,","if matters grow to your likings."],"id":31,"m":"God's blessing is here, and your friend, and Justice Shallow, and young Master Slender, who may have another story to tell you, if things go the way you like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page.","id":32},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["I am glad to see your worships well. I thank you for my venison, Master","Shallow."],"id":33,"m":"I am glad to see your worships well. Thank you for the venison, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Master Page, I am glad to see you, much good do it your good heart! I","wished your venison better; it was ill killed. How doth good Mistress","Page? And I thank you always with my heart, la, with my heart."],"id":34,"m":"Master Page, I am glad to see you; may it do your kind heart good! I wish the venison had been better; it was badly killed. How is good Mistress Page? I always thank you with all my heart, truly, with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Sir, I thank you."],"id":35,"m":"Thank you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir, I thank you; by yea and no, I do."],"id":36,"m":"Thank you, sir; yes indeed, I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["I am glad to see you, good Master Slender."],"id":37,"m":"I am glad to see you, good Master Slender."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["How does your fallow greyhound, sir? I heard say he was outrun on","Cotsall."],"id":38,"m":"How is your fawn-coloured greyhound, sir? I heard he was beaten in the race on the Cotswolds."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["It could not be judged, sir."],"id":39,"m":"The result could not be settled, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["You'll not confess, you'll not confess."],"id":40,"m":"You won't admit it, you won't admit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["That he will not. 'Tis your fault; 'tis your fault. 'Tis a good dog."],"id":41,"m":"No, he won't. It's your dog's fault, your dog's fault. It's a good dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["A cur, sir."],"id":42,"m":"A mongrel, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir, he's a good dog, and a fair dog, can there be more said? He is","good, and fair. Is Sir John Falstaff here?"],"id":43,"m":"Sir, he's a good dog and a handsome dog; what more can you say? He's good and handsome. Is Sir John Falstaff here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Sir, he is within; and I would I could do a good office between you."],"id":44,"m":"Sir, he is inside, and I wish I could make peace between you."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is spoke as a Christians ought to speak."],"id":45,"m":"That is spoken as a Christian ought to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He hath wronged me, Master Page."],"id":46,"m":"He has wronged me, Master Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Sir, he doth in some sort confess it."],"id":47,"m":"Sir, he more or less admits it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["If it be confessed, it is not redressed. Is not that so, Master Page?","He hath wronged me, indeed he hath, at a word, he hath. Believe me.","Robert Shallow, esquire, saith he is wronged."],"id":48,"m":"Admitting it is not making amends. Isn't that right, Master Page? He has wronged me, he really has, in a word, he has. Believe me. Robert Shallow, esquire, says he has been wronged."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Here comes Sir John."],"id":49,"m":"Here comes Sir John."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir John Falstaff, Bardolph, Nym and Pistol.","id":50},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now, Master Shallow, you'll complain of me to the King?"],"id":51,"m":"Well, Master Shallow, are you going to complain about me to the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Knight, you have beaten my men, killed my deer, and broke open my","lodge."],"id":52,"m":"Knight, you have beaten my servants, killed my deer and broken into my hunting lodge."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But not kissed your keeper's daughter!"],"id":53,"m":"But I didn't kiss your gamekeeper's daughter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Tut, a pin! This shall be answered."],"id":54,"m":"Nonsense! You will answer for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I will answer it straight: I have done all this. That is now answered."],"id":55,"m":"I will answer right now: I did all of it. There, that's answered."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["The Council shall know this."],"id":56,"m":"The Council will hear of this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Twere better for you if it were known in counsel: you'll be laughed","at."],"id":57,"m":"It would be better for you if it were kept quiet: you'll be laughed at."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Pauca verba, Sir John; goot worts."],"id":58,"m":"Few words, Sir John; good words."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good worts? Good cabbage!—Slender, I broke your head. What matter have","you against me?"],"id":59,"m":"Good worts? Good cabbages! Slender, I cracked your head. What complaint do you have against me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Marry, sir, I have matter in my head against you, and against your","cony-catching rascals, Bardolph, Nym, and Pistol. They carried me to","the tavern and made me drunk, and afterwards picked my pocket."],"id":60,"m":"Well, sir, I have a complaint in my head against you, and against your swindling rogues, Bardolph, Nym and Pistol. They took me to the tavern, got me drunk and then picked my pocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["You Banbury cheese!"],"id":61,"m":"You skinny Banbury cheese!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, it is no matter."],"id":62,"m":"Yes, never mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["How now, Mephostophilus?"],"id":63,"m":"What's this, Mephistopheles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, it is no matter."],"id":64,"m":"Yes, never mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Slice, I say! Pauca, pauca, slice, that's my humour."],"id":65,"m":"Cut him up, I say! Few words, few words; cut him, that's my mood."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Where's Simple, my man? Can you tell, cousin?"],"id":66,"m":"Where's Simple, my servant? Do you know, cousin?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Peace, I pray you. Now let us understand; there is three umpires in","this matter, as I understand: that is, Master Page, fidelicet Master","Page; and there is myself, fidelicet myself; and the three party is,","lastly and finally, mine host of the Garter."],"id":67,"m":"Quiet, please. Now let us understand this. There are three umpires in this matter, as I understand it: that is, Master Page, namely Master Page; and myself, namely myself; and the third party is, lastly and finally, the landlord of the Garter Inn."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["We three to hear it and end it between them."],"id":68,"m":"We three will hear the case and settle it between them."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Fery goot. I will make a prief of it in my notebook, and we will","afterwards 'ork upon the cause with as great discreetly as we can."],"id":69,"m":"Very good. I will make a note of it in my notebook, and afterwards we will work on the case as carefully as we can."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Pistol!"],"id":70,"m":"Pistol!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["He hears with ears."],"id":71,"m":"He listens with his ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["The tevil and his tam! What phrase is this, \"He hears with ear\"? Why,","it is affectations."],"id":72,"m":"The devil and his mother! What way of speaking is that, \"He hears with ear\"? Why, it is pure affectation."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Pistol, did you pick Master Slender's purse?"],"id":73,"m":"Pistol, did you pick Master Slender's purse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, by these gloves, did he, or I would I might never come in mine own","great chamber again else! Of seven groats in mill-sixpences, and two","Edward shovel-boards that cost me two shilling and two pence a-piece of","Yed Miller, by these gloves."],"id":74,"m":"Yes, by these gloves he did, or may I never set foot in my own great hall again! Seven groats in milled sixpences, and two Edward shovel-board shillings that cost me two shillings and twopence each from Ed Miller, by these gloves."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Is this true, Pistol?"],"id":75,"m":"Is this true, Pistol?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["No, it is false, if it is a pick-purse."],"id":76,"m":"No, it is false, if he is a pickpocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Ha, thou mountain-foreigner!—Sir John and master mine,","I combat challenge of this latten bilbo.—","Word of denial in thy labras here!","Word of denial! Froth and scum, thou liest."],"id":77,"m":"Ha, you foreigner from the mountains! Sir John, my master, I challenge this tin-sword weakling to a fight. I throw the denial back in your lips! I deny it! You scum and froth, you lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["[Points at Nym] By these gloves, then, 'twas he."],"id":78,"m":"By these gloves, then, it was him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["Be avised, sir, and pass good humours. I will say \"marry trap with","you\", if you run the nuthook's humour on me. That is the very note of","it."],"id":79,"m":"Be careful, sir, and keep your temper. I will say \"go hang yourself\" if you try to play the constable with me. That's how it stands."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["By this hat, then, he in the red face had it. For though I cannot","remember what I did when you made me drunk, yet I am not altogether an","ass."],"id":80,"m":"By this hat, then, the one with the red face took it. Though I can't remember what I did when you got me drunk, I am not a complete fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What say you, Scarlet and John?"],"id":81,"m":"What do you say, Scarlet and John?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Why, sir, for my part, I say the gentleman had drunk himself out of his","five sentences."],"id":82,"m":"Well, sir, for my part, I say the gentleman had drunk himself out of his five sentences."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is his \"five senses\". Fie, what the ignorance is!"],"id":83,"m":"It is \"five senses\". Shame, what ignorance!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["And being fap, sir, was, as they say, cashiered; and so conclusions","passed the careers."],"id":84,"m":"And being drunk, sir, he was, as they say, robbed of his cash; and so things went past their limit."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, you spake in Latin then too; but 'tis no matter. I'll ne'er be","drunk whilst I live again, but in honest, civil, godly company, for","this trick. If I be drunk, I'll be drunk with those that have the fear","of God, and not with drunken knaves."],"id":85,"m":"Yes, and you talked Latin then too; but never mind. I'll never get drunk again as long as I live, except in honest, civil, godly company, after this trick. If I get drunk, I'll get drunk with people who fear God, not with drunken rogues."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["So Got 'udge me, that is a virtuous mind."],"id":86,"m":"God judge me, that is a virtuous attitude."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You hear all these matters denied, gentlemen; you hear it."],"id":87,"m":"You hear all these charges denied, gentlemen; you hear it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford, Mistress Page and her daughter Anne Page with wine.","id":88},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Nay, daughter, carry the wine in, we'll drink within."],"id":89,"m":"No, daughter, take the wine inside; we'll drink in there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Anne Page.","id":90},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["O heaven, this is Mistress Anne Page."],"id":91,"m":"Oh heaven, this is Mistress Anne Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["How now, Mistress Ford?"],"id":92,"m":"How are you, Mistress Ford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mistress Ford, by my troth, you are very well met. By your leave, good","mistress."],"id":93,"m":"Mistress Ford, on my word, I am very glad to meet you. With your permission, good lady."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kisses her.","id":94},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Wife, bid these gentlemen welcome. Come, we have a hot venison pasty to","dinner. Come, gentlemen, I hope we shall drink down all unkindness."],"id":95,"m":"Wife, welcome these gentlemen. Come, we have a hot venison pie for dinner. Come, gentlemen, I hope we will drink away all bad feeling."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Slender.","id":96},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I had rather than forty shillings I had my book of Songs and Sonnets","here."],"id":97,"m":"I would give forty shillings to have my book of Songs and Sonnets here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Simple.","id":98},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Simple, where have you been? I must wait on myself, must I?","You have not the Book of Riddles about you, have you?"],"id":99,"m":"Well, Simple, where have you been? I have to wait on myself, do I? You haven't got the Book of Riddles with you, have you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Book of Riddles? Why, did you not lend it to Alice Shortcake upon","Allhallowmas last, a fortnight afore Michaelmas?"],"id":100,"m":"The Book of Riddles? Why, didn't you lend it to Alice Shortcake last All Saints' Day, two weeks before Michaelmas?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shallow and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":101},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Come, coz; come, coz, we stay for you. A word with you, coz. Marry,","this, coz: there is, as 'twere, a tender, a kind of tender, made afar","off by Sir Hugh here. Do you understand me?"],"id":102,"m":"Come, cousin, come, cousin, we're waiting for you. A word with you, cousin. It's this, cousin: there is, so to speak, an offer, a kind of offer, made indirectly by Sir Hugh here. Do you understand me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, sir, you shall find me reasonable. If it be so, I shall do that","that is reason."],"id":103,"m":"Yes, sir, you will find me reasonable. If that is so, I will do what is reasonable."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Nay, but understand me."],"id":104,"m":"No, but understand me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["So I do, sir."],"id":105,"m":"I do, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Give ear to his motions, Master Slender. I will description the matter","to you, if you be capacity of it."],"id":106,"m":"Listen to his proposal, Master Slender. I will describe the matter to you, if you are capable of understanding it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Nay, I will do as my cousin Shallow says. I pray you pardon me, he's a","Justice of Peace in his country, simple though I stand here."],"id":107,"m":"No, I will do what my cousin Shallow says. Excuse me, he's a Justice of the Peace in his county, humble as I may look standing here."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["But that is not the question. The question is concerning your marriage."],"id":108,"m":"But that is not the question. The question concerns your marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ay, there's the point, sir."],"id":109,"m":"Yes, that's the point, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Marry, is it; the very point of it—to Mistress Anne Page."],"id":110,"m":"Indeed it is, the very point of it: to Mistress Anne Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Why, if it be so, I will marry her upon any reasonable demands."],"id":111,"m":"Well, if that's it, I will marry her on any reasonable terms."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["But can you affection the 'oman? Let us command to know that of your","mouth, or of your lips; for divers philosophers hold that the lips is","parcel of the mouth. Therefore, precisely, can you carry your good will","to the maid?"],"id":112,"m":"But can you love the woman? Let us hear that from your mouth, or from your lips; for many philosophers hold that the lips are part of the mouth. So, exactly, can you give your affection to the girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Cousin Abraham Slender, can you love her?"],"id":113,"m":"Cousin Abraham Slender, can you love her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I hope, sir, I will do as it shall become one that would do reason."],"id":114,"m":"I hope, sir, I will behave as someone who wants to be reasonable should."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Nay, Got's lords and his ladies! You must speak possitable, if you can","carry her your desires towards her."],"id":115,"m":"No, by God's lords and ladies! You must say positively whether you can give her your desire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["That you must. Will you, upon good dowry, marry her?"],"id":116,"m":"You must. Will you marry her, with a good dowry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I will do a greater thing than that, upon your request, cousin, in any","reason."],"id":117,"m":"I will do a greater thing than that, if you ask me, cousin, within reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Nay, conceive me, conceive me, sweet coz. What I do is to pleasure you,","coz. Can you love the maid?"],"id":118,"m":"No, understand me, understand me, dear cousin. What I do is to please you, cousin. Can you love the girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I will marry her, sir, at your request. But if there be no great love","in the beginning, yet heaven may decrease it upon better acquaintance,","when we are married and have more occasion to know one another. I hope","upon familiarity will grow more contempt. But if you say \"Marry her,\" I","will marry her. That I am freely dissolved, and dissolutely."],"id":119,"m":"I will marry her, sir, since you ask. And if there isn't much love at the beginning, heaven may decrease it when we know each other better, once we are married and have more chance to get acquainted. I hope that familiarity will breed more contempt. But if you say \"Marry her,\" I will marry her. On that I am freely dissolved, and dissolutely."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is a fery discretion answer, save the fall is in the 'ord","\"dissolutely.\" The 'ort is, according to our meaning, \"resolutely.\" His","meaning is good."],"id":120,"m":"It is a very sensible answer, except for the slip in the word \"dissolutely\". The word, as we mean it, is \"resolutely\". His meaning is good."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ay, I think my cousin meant well."],"id":121,"m":"Yes, I think my cousin meant well."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, or else I would I might be hanged, la!"],"id":122,"m":"Yes, or else may I be hanged, I swear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Here comes fair Mistress Anne."],"id":123,"m":"Here comes lovely Mistress Anne."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Anne Page.","id":124},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Here comes fair Mistress Anne.—Would I were young for your sake,","Mistress Anne."],"id":125,"m":"Here comes lovely Mistress Anne. I wish I were young for your sake, Mistress Anne."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["The dinner is on the table, my father desires your worships' company."],"id":126,"m":"Dinner is on the table, and my father would like your worships to join him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I will wait on him, fair Mistress Anne."],"id":127,"m":"I will join him, lovely Mistress Anne."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["'Od's plessed will! I will not be absence at the grace."],"id":128,"m":"By God's blessed will! I won't miss grace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Shallow and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":129},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Will't please your worship to come in, sir?"],"id":130,"m":"Will you please come in, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["No, I thank you, forsooth, heartily; I am very well."],"id":131,"m":"No, thank you, truly, with all my heart; I am very comfortable here."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["The dinner attends you, sir."],"id":132,"m":"Dinner is waiting for you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I am not a-hungry, I thank you, forsooth. [To Simple] Go, sirrah,","for all you are my man, go wait upon my cousin Shallow."],"id":133,"m":"I'm not hungry, thank you, truly. Go, fellow, even if you are my servant, go and wait on my cousin Shallow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Simple.","id":134},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","cont":1,"lines":["A Justice of Peace sometime may be beholding to his friend for a man. I","keep but three men and a boy yet, till my mother be dead. But what","though? Yet I live like a poor gentleman born."],"id":135,"m":"A Justice of the Peace may sometimes have to borrow a servant from his friend. I keep only three men and a boy for now, until my mother dies. But what of it? I still live like a poor gentleman born."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I may not go in without your worship. They will not sit till you come."],"id":136,"m":"I'm not allowed to go in without your worship. They won't sit down until you come."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I' faith, I'll eat nothing. I thank you as much as though I did."],"id":137,"m":"Honestly, I'll eat nothing. I thank you as much as if I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I pray you, sir, walk in."],"id":138,"m":"Please, sir, come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I had rather walk here, I thank you. I bruised my shin th' other day","with playing at sword and dagger with a master of fence—three veneys","for a dish of stewed prunes—and, by my troth, I cannot abide the smell","of hot meat since. Why do your dogs bark so? Be there bears i' the","town?"],"id":139,"m":"I'd rather walk out here, thank you. I bruised my shin the other day fencing with sword and dagger against a fencing master, three bouts for a dish of stewed prunes, and, on my word, I haven't been able to stand the smell of hot meat since. Why are your dogs barking like that? Are there bears in town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I think there are, sir; I heard them talked of."],"id":140,"m":"I think there are, sir; I heard people talking about them."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I love the sport well, but I shall as soon quarrel at it as any man in","England. You are afraid, if you see the bear loose, are you not?"],"id":141,"m":"I love the sport, but I'm as quick to object to it as any man in England. You are afraid when you see the bear loose, aren't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Ay, indeed, sir."],"id":142,"m":"Yes, I am, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["That's meat and drink to me now. I have seen Sackerson loose twenty","times, and have taken him by the chain. But, I warrant you, the women","have so cried and shrieked at it that it passed. But women, indeed,","cannot abide 'em; they are very ill-favoured rough things."],"id":143,"m":"That's meat and drink to me. I've seen Sackerson, the famous bear, loose twenty times and grabbed him by the chain. But I promise you, the women screamed and shrieked so much it was beyond belief. Women really can't stand them; they're very ugly, rough things."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page.","id":144},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Come, gentle Master Slender, come. We stay for you."],"id":145,"m":"Come, gentle Master Slender, come. We're waiting for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I'll eat nothing, I thank you, sir."],"id":146,"m":"I'll eat nothing, thank you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["By cock and pie, you shall not choose, sir! Come, come."],"id":147,"m":"By God and the holy book, you have no choice, sir! Come, come."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Nay, pray you lead the way."],"id":148,"m":"Well, please lead the way."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Come on, sir."],"id":149,"m":"Come on, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Mistress Anne, yourself shall go first."],"id":150,"m":"Mistress Anne, you go first."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Not I, sir; pray you keep on."],"id":151,"m":"Not me, sir; please, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Truly, I will not go first; truly, la! I will not do you that wrong."],"id":152,"m":"Honestly, I won't go first; honestly I won't! I won't do you that wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I pray you, sir."],"id":153,"m":"Please, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I'll rather be unmannerly than troublesome. You do yourself wrong,","indeed, la!"],"id":154,"m":"I'd rather be rude than a nuisance. You do yourself wrong, really, you do!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":155},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Windsor. Before Page's house.","id":156},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Hugh Evans and Simple.","id":157},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Go your ways, and ask of Doctor Caius' house which is the way. And","there dwells one Mistress Quickly, which is in the manner of his nurse,","or his dry nurse, or his cook, or his laundry, his washer and his","wringer."],"id":158,"m":"Off you go, and ask the way to Doctor Caius's house. A Mistress Quickly lives there, who is a sort of nurse to him, or his dry nurse, or his cook, or his laundress, his washer and his wringer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Well, sir."],"id":159,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Nay, it is petter yet. Give her this letter. For it is a 'oman that","altogether's acquaintance with Mistress Anne Page; and the letter is to","desire and require her to solicit your master's desires to Mistress","Anne Page. I pray you be gone. I will make an end of my dinner; there's","pippins and cheese to come."],"id":160,"m":"No, there's something better yet. Give her this letter. She is a woman who is very well acquainted with Mistress Anne Page, and the letter asks and requires her to press your master's suit with Mistress Anne Page. Please go. I will finish my dinner; there are apples and cheese still to come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":161},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":162},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, Host, Bardolph, Nym, Pistol and Robin.","id":163},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mine host of the Garter!"],"id":164,"m":"Landlord of the Garter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What says my bully rook? Speak scholarly and wisely."],"id":165,"m":"What does my fine fellow say? Speak learnedly and wisely."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Truly, mine host, I must turn away some of my followers."],"id":166,"m":"Honestly, landlord, I have to dismiss some of my followers."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Discard, bully Hercules; cashier. Let them wag; trot, trot."],"id":167,"m":"Get rid of them, my mighty Hercules; sack them. Let them go; off they trot."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I sit at ten pounds a week."],"id":168,"m":"I'm paying ten pounds a week here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Thou'rt an emperor—Caesar, Keiser, and Pheazar. I will entertain","Bardolph. He shall draw, he shall tap. Said I well, bully Hector?"],"id":169,"m":"You're an emperor, a Caesar, a Kaiser and a Vizier. I will take on Bardolph. He can draw beer and tap the barrels. Well said, my brave Hector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Do so, good mine host."],"id":170,"m":"Do that, good landlord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["I have spoke, let him follow.—Let me see thee froth and lime. I am at a","word, follow."],"id":171,"m":"I have spoken; let him follow me. (To Bardolph) Let me see you froth the beer and doctor the wine. I am a man of my word; follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Host.","id":172},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Bardolph, follow him. A tapster is a good trade. An old cloak makes a","new jerkin; a withered servingman a fresh tapster. Go, adieu."],"id":173,"m":"Bardolph, follow him. Tapster is a good trade. An old cloak makes a new jacket, and a worn-out servant makes a fresh tapster. Go, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["It is a life that I have desired. I will thrive."],"id":174,"m":"It is the life I have always wanted. I will do well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["O base Hungarian wight, wilt thou the spigot wield?"],"id":175,"m":"You low beggarly fellow, will you work the tap?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":176},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["He was gotten in drink. Is not the humour conceited?"],"id":177,"m":"He was conceived while his parents were drunk. Isn't that a clever idea?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am glad I am so acquit of this tinderbox. His thefts were too open.","His filching was like an unskilful singer, he kept not time."],"id":178,"m":"I am glad to be rid of this tinderbox. His thefts were too obvious. His stealing was like a clumsy singer: he never kept time."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["The good humour is to steal at a minute's rest."],"id":179,"m":"The best way is to steal in the space of a moment's rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["\"Convey,\" the wise it call. \"Steal?\" Foh! A fico for the phrase!"],"id":180,"m":"\"Convey\" is what wise men call it. \"Steal\"? Pah! A fig for the word!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, sirs, I am almost out at heels."],"id":181,"m":"Well, gentlemen, I have almost worn through my shoes."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Why, then, let kibes ensue."],"id":182,"m":"Well then, let chilblains follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["There is no remedy, I must cony-catch, I must shift."],"id":183,"m":"There's no help for it; I must swindle, I must live by my wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Young ravens must have food."],"id":184,"m":"Young ravens must be fed."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Which of you know Ford of this town?"],"id":185,"m":"Which of you knows Ford of this town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I ken the wight, he is of substance good."],"id":186,"m":"I know the man; he is well off."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My honest lads, I will tell you what I am about."],"id":187,"m":"My honest lads, I will tell you what I am about."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Two yards, and more."],"id":188,"m":"About two yards, and more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No quips now, Pistol. Indeed, I am in the waist two yards about, but I","am now about no waste; I am about thrift. Briefly, I do mean to make","love to Ford's wife. I spy entertainment in her. She discourses, she","carves, she gives the leer of invitation. I can construe the action of","her familiar style; and the hardest voice of her behaviour, to be","Englished rightly, is \"I am Sir John Falstaff's.\""],"id":189,"m":"No jokes now, Pistol. Yes, I am two yards about the waist, but I am not about waste now; I am about thrift. In short, I intend to make love to Ford's wife. I see a welcome in her. She chats, she is gracious, she gives me inviting looks. I can interpret the meaning of her friendly manner, and the strictest reading of her behaviour, put into plain English, is \"I belong to Sir John Falstaff.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["He hath studied her will and translated her will—out of honesty into","English."],"id":190,"m":"He has studied what she wants and translated it, out of honesty into English."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["The anchor is deep. Will that humour pass?"],"id":191,"m":"The anchor is deep. Will that idea work?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now, the report goes she has all the rule of her husband's purse. He","hath a legion of angels."],"id":192,"m":"Now, the rumour is that she has full control of her husband's money. He has a legion of gold angels."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["As many devils entertain, and \"To her, boy,\" say I."],"id":193,"m":"Then take on as many devils, and \"Go for her, boy,\" I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["The humour rises; it is good. Humour me the angels."],"id":194,"m":"The mood rises; it's good. Get me those angels."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I have writ me here a letter to her; and here another to Page's wife,","who even now gave me good eyes too, examined my parts with most","judicious oeillades. Sometimes the beam of her view gilded my foot,","sometimes my portly belly."],"id":195,"m":"I have written a letter to her here, and here is another to Page's wife, who gave me the eye just now as well and looked me over with very knowing glances. Sometimes the beam of her look gilded my foot, sometimes my stately belly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Then did the sun on dunghill shine."],"id":196,"m":"Then the sun shone on a dunghill."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I thank thee for that humour."],"id":197,"m":"Thanks for that joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O, she did so course o'er my exteriors with such a greedy intention","that the appetite of her eye did seem to scorch me up like a","burning-glass. Here's another letter to her. She bears the purse too;","she is a region in Guiana, all gold and bounty. I will be cheaters to","them both, and they shall be exchequers to me; they shall be my East","and West Indies, and I will trade to them both. Go, bear thou this","letter to Mistress Page;—and thou this to Mistress Ford. We will","thrive, lads, we will thrive."],"id":198,"m":"Oh, she ran her eyes over my outside with such greedy attention that the hunger in her look seemed to burn me up like a magnifying glass. Here's another letter to her. She controls the purse too; she is like a region of Guiana, all gold and riches. I will be the swindler of both of them, and they will be my treasuries; they will be my East and West Indies, and I will trade with both. Go, take this letter to Mistress Page, and you take this one to Mistress Ford. We will do well, lads, we will do well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Shall I Sir Pandarus of Troy become,","And by my side wear steel? Then Lucifer take all!"],"id":199,"m":"Shall I become a pimp like Pandarus of Troy, with a sword at my side? Then the devil take everything!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I will run no base humour. Here, take the humour-letter. I will keep","the 'haviour of reputation."],"id":200,"m":"I won't do anything so low. Here, take back your letter. I will keep my good name."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[To Robin] Hold, sirrah, bear you these letters tightly;","Sail like my pinnace to these golden shores.—","Rogues, hence, avaunt! Vanish like hailstones, go!","Trudge, plod away o' th' hoof, seek shelter, pack!","Falstaff will learn the humour of this age:","French thrift, you rogues—myself and skirted page."],"id":201,"m":"(To Robin) Here, boy, carry these letters carefully. Sail like my little ship to these golden shores. Rogues, away, be gone! Vanish like hailstones, go! Trudge off on foot, find your own shelter, clear out! Falstaff will follow the fashion of the times: French thrift, you rogues, myself and one page in a short coat."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Falstaff and Robin.","id":202},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Let vultures gripe thy guts! For gourd and fullam holds,","And high and low beguile the rich and poor.","Tester I'll have in pouch when thou shalt lack,","Base Phrygian Turk!"],"id":203,"m":"May vultures tear your guts! Loaded dice will serve us, and cheating will fool both rich and poor. I'll have sixpence in my pocket when you have nothing, you low heathen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["I have operations in my head which be humours of revenge."],"id":204,"m":"I have plans in my head that are schemes of revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Wilt thou revenge?"],"id":205,"m":"Will you take revenge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["By welkin and her star!"],"id":206,"m":"By the sky and its stars!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["With wit or steel?"],"id":207,"m":"With brains or with swords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["With both the humours, I.","I will discuss the humour of this love to Ford."],"id":208,"m":"With both. I will reveal this love affair to Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["And I to Page shall eke unfold","How Falstaff, varlet vile,","His dove will prove, his gold will hold,","And his soft couch defile."],"id":209,"m":"And I will also tell Page how Falstaff, the vile rascal, means to test his wife, take his gold and dirty his marriage bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["My humour shall not cool. I will incense Ford to deal with poison, I","will possess him with yellowness, for the revolt of mine is dangerous.","That is my true humour."],"id":210,"m":"My anger won't cool. I will stir Ford up to use poison; I will fill him with jealousy, because my rebellion is dangerous. That is my true mood."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Thou art the Mars of malcontents. I second thee. Troop on."],"id":211,"m":"You are the god of war among malcontents. I'm with you. March on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":212},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"A room in Doctor Caius's house.","id":213},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly and Simple.","id":214},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["What, John Rugby!"],"id":215,"m":"John Rugby, come here!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Rugby.","id":216},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["I pray thee go to the casement, and see if you can see my master,","Master Doctor Caius, coming. If he do, i' faith, and find anybody in","the house, here will be an old abusing of God's patience and the King's","English."],"id":217,"m":"Please go to the window and see if you can see my master, Doctor Caius, coming. If he does come and finds anyone in the house, there will be a fine old abuse of God's patience and the King's English."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["I'll go watch."],"id":218,"m":"I'll keep watch."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Go; and we'll have a posset for't soon at night, in faith, at the","latter end of a sea-coal fire."],"id":219,"m":"Go, and we'll have a hot spiced drink for it later tonight, honestly, when the coal fire is dying down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Rugby.","id":220},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["An honest, willing, kind fellow, as ever servant shall come in house","withal; and, I warrant you, no tell-tale nor no breed-bate. His worst","fault is that he is given to prayer; he is something peevish that way,","but nobody but has his fault. But let that pass. Peter Simple you say","your name is?"],"id":221,"m":"An honest, willing, kind fellow, as good a servant as ever came into a house, and I promise you, no tell-tale and no troublemaker. His worst fault is that he is too fond of prayer; he's a bit silly that way, but everyone has a fault. But never mind that. Your name is Peter Simple, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Ay, for fault of a better."],"id":222,"m":"Yes, for lack of a better one."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["And Master Slender's your master?"],"id":223,"m":"And Master Slender is your master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Ay, forsooth."],"id":224,"m":"Yes, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Does he not wear a great round beard, like a glover's paring-knife?"],"id":225,"m":"Doesn't he wear a big round beard, like a glove-maker's curved knife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["No, forsooth, he hath but a little wee face, with a little yellow","beard, a Cain-coloured beard."],"id":226,"m":"No, truly, he has just a tiny little face, with a little yellow beard, a reddish-yellow beard like Cain's."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["A softly-sprighted man, is he not?"],"id":227,"m":"A gentle-spirited man, isn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Ay, forsooth. But he is as tall a man of his hands as any is between","this and his head. He hath fought with a warrener."],"id":228,"m":"Yes, truly. But he is as brave a fighter as anyone between here and there. He once fought with a gamekeeper."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["How say you? O, I should remember him. Does he not hold up his head, as","it were, and strut in his gait?"],"id":229,"m":"What's that? Oh, I should remember him. Doesn't he hold his head up high, so to speak, and strut when he walks?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Yes, indeed, does he."],"id":230,"m":"Yes, indeed he does."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Well, heaven send Anne Page no worse fortune! Tell Master Parson Evans","I will do what I can for your master. Anne is a good girl, and I wish—"],"id":231,"m":"Well, heaven send Anne Page no worse luck! Tell Parson Evans I will do what I can for your master. Anne is a good girl, and I wish—"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Rugby.","id":232},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["Out, alas! Here comes my master."],"id":233,"m":"Oh no! Here comes my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["We shall all be shent. Run in here, good young man, go into this","closet. He will not stay long."],"id":234,"m":"We'll all be in trouble. Run in here, good young man, go into this cupboard. He won't stay long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Simple steps into the closet.","id":235},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["What, John Rugby! John! What, John, I say! Go, John, go inquire for my","master. I doubt he be not well, that he comes not home."],"id":236,"m":"John Rugby! John! John, I say! Go, John, go and look for my master. I'm afraid he isn't well, since he hasn't come home."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Rugby.","id":237},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":238},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["~And down, down, adown-a, etc."],"id":239,"m":"And down, down, adown-a, and so on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Doctor Caius.","id":240},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vat is you sing? I do not like dese toys. Pray you, go and vetch me in","my closet une boîtine verte, a box, a green-a box. Do intend vat I","speak? A green-a box."],"id":241,"m":"What is it you are singing? I don't like these trifles. Please go and fetch me from my cupboard a little green box, a box, a green box. Do you understand what I am saying? A green box."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Ay, forsooth, I'll fetch it you.","[Aside] I am glad he went not in himself. If he had found the young","man, he would have been horn-mad."],"id":242,"m":"Yes, truly, I'll fetch it for you. (Aside) I'm glad he didn't go in himself. If he had found the young man, he would have gone mad with rage."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Fe, fe, fe fe! Ma foi, il fait fort chaud. Je m'en vais à la cour—la","grande affaire."],"id":243,"m":"Phew, phew, phew! My word, it's very hot. I am going to the court, on the great business."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Is it this, sir?"],"id":244,"m":"Is it this, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Oui, mette-le au mon pocket. Dépêche, quickly—Vere is dat knave","Rugby?"],"id":245,"m":"Yes, put it in my pocket. Hurry, quickly. Where is that rogue Rugby?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["What, John Rugby, John!"],"id":246,"m":"John Rugby, John!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Rugby.","id":247},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":248,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["You are John Rugby, and you are Jack Rugby. Come, take-a your rapier,","and come after my heel to the court."],"id":249,"m":"You are John Rugby, and you are Jack Rugby. Come, take your rapier and follow at my heels to the court."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["'Tis ready, sir, here in the porch."],"id":250,"m":"It's ready, sir, here in the porch."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By my trot, I tarry too long. 'Od's me! Qu'ay j'oublié? Dere is some","simples in my closet dat I vill not for the varld I shall leave behind."],"id":251,"m":"On my word, I'm taking too long. God save me! What have I forgotten? There are some herbs in my cupboard that I wouldn't leave behind for the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Ay me, he'll find the young man there, and be mad!"],"id":252,"m":"Oh dear, he'll find the young man there and go mad!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["O diable, diable! Vat is in my closet? Villainy! Larron! [Pulling","Simple out.] Rugby, my rapier!"],"id":253,"m":"Oh devil, devil! What is in my cupboard? Villainy! Thief! Rugby, my rapier!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Good master, be content."],"id":254,"m":"Good master, calm down."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Wherefore shall I be content-a?"],"id":255,"m":"Why should I calm down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["The young man is an honest man."],"id":256,"m":"The young man is an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["What shall de honest man do in my closet? Dere is no honest man dat","shall come in my closet."],"id":257,"m":"What is an honest man doing in my cupboard? No honest man comes into my cupboard."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["I beseech you, be not so phlegmatic. Hear the truth of it. He came of","an errand to me from Parson Hugh."],"id":258,"m":"Please don't be so hot-tempered. Hear the truth of it. He came to me on an errand from Parson Hugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vell?"],"id":259,"m":"Well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Ay, forsooth, to desire her to—"],"id":260,"m":"Yes, truly, to ask her to—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Peace, I pray you."],"id":261,"m":"Quiet, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Peace-a your tongue!—Speak-a your tale."],"id":262,"m":"You hold your tongue! You, tell your story."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["To desire this honest gentlewoman, your maid, to speak a good word to","Mistress Anne Page for my master in the way of marriage."],"id":263,"m":"To ask this honest woman, your servant, to put in a good word for my master with Mistress Anne Page, with a view to marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["This is all, indeed, la! But I'll ne'er put my finger in the fire, and","need not."],"id":264,"m":"That's all it is, truly! But I'll never put my finger in the fire when I don't have to."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Sir Hugh send-a you?—Rugby, baille me some paper.—Tarry you a","little-a while."],"id":265,"m":"Sir Hugh sent you? Rugby, give me some paper. You wait here a little while."},{"t":"sd","text":"Writes.","id":266},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["[Aside to Simple] I am glad he is so quiet. If he had been throughly","moved, you should have heard him so loud and so melancholy. But","notwithstanding, man, I'll do you your master what good I can; and the","very yea and the no is, the French doctor, my master—I may call him my","master, look you, for I keep his house, and I wash, wring, brew, bake,","scour, dress meat and drink, make the beds, and do all myself—"],"id":267,"m":"(Aside to Simple) I'm glad he's so quiet. If he had been thoroughly roused, you would have heard him shout and rage. But all the same, man, I'll do your master what good I can. And the long and short of it is that the French doctor, my master (I can call him my master, you see, because I keep his house, and I wash, wring, brew, bake, scrub, cook and make the drink, make the beds and do it all myself)—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["[Aside to Mistress Quickly] 'Tis a great charge to come under one","body's hand."],"id":268,"m":"(Aside to Mistress Quickly) That's a lot of work for one person to handle."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["[Aside to Simple] Are you avised o' that? You shall find it a great","charge, and to be up early and down late; but notwithstanding—to tell","you in your ear, I would have no words of it—my master himself is in","love with Mistress Anne Page; but notwithstanding that, I know Anne's","mind. That's neither here nor there."],"id":269,"m":"(Aside to Simple) Have you noticed that? You'd find it's a lot of work, up early and to bed late. But all the same (to whisper it in your ear, and I don't want this talked about) my master himself is in love with Mistress Anne Page. But all the same, I know Anne's mind. That's neither here nor there."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["You jack'nape, give-a dis letter to Sir Hugh. By gar, it is a","shallenge. I will cut his troat in de park, and I will teach a scurvy","jackanape priest to meddle or make. You may be gone, it is not good you","tarry here.—By gar, I will cut all his two stones. By gar, he shall not","have a stone to throw at his dog."],"id":270,"m":"You monkey, give this letter to Sir Hugh. By God, it is a challenge. I will cut his throat in the park, and I will teach a lousy monkey of a priest to meddle in my affairs. You can go; it's not good for you to stay here. By God, I will cut off both his balls. By God, he won't have a stone left to throw at his dog."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Simple.","id":271},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Alas, he speaks but for his friend."],"id":272,"m":"Oh dear, he's only speaking for his friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["It is no matter-a ver dat. Do not you tell-a me dat I shall have Anne","Page for myself? By gar, I vill kill de Jack priest; and I have","appointed mine host of de Jarteer to measure our weapon. By gar, I will","myself have Anne Page."],"id":273,"m":"That doesn't matter. Didn't you tell me I would have Anne Page for myself? By God, I will kill that rascal priest; and I have asked the landlord of the Garter to referee our duel. By God, I will have Anne Page myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Sir, the maid loves you, and all shall be well. We must give folks","leave to prate. What, the good-year!"],"id":274,"m":"Sir, the girl loves you, and all will be well. We have to let people chatter. What the devil!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Rugby, come to the court with me. [To Mistress Quickly] By gar, if I","have not Anne Page, I shall turn your head out of my door.—Follow my","heels, Rugby."],"id":275,"m":"Rugby, come to the court with me. (To Mistress Quickly) By God, if I don't get Anne Page, I will throw you out of my house. Follow at my heels, Rugby."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Caius and Rugby.","id":276},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["You shall have An—fool's head of your own. No, I know Anne's mind for","that. Never a woman in Windsor knows more of Anne's mind than I do, nor","can do more than I do with her, I thank heaven."],"id":277,"m":"You'll have Anne—a fool's head of your own, that's what. No, I know Anne's mind on that. No woman in Windsor knows Anne's mind better than I do, or can do more with her than I can, thank heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["[Within] Who's within there, ho?"],"id":278,"m":"Is anyone in there? Hello?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Who's there, I trow? Come near the house, I pray you."],"id":279,"m":"Who's there, I wonder? Come into the house, please."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fenton.","id":280},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["How now, good woman? How dost thou?"],"id":281,"m":"Hello, good woman! How are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["The better, that it pleases your good worship to ask."],"id":282,"m":"All the better for your kind worship asking."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["What news? How does pretty Mistress Anne?"],"id":283,"m":"What news? How is pretty Mistress Anne?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["In truth, sir, and she is pretty, and honest, and gentle; and one that","is your friend, I can tell you that by the way, I praise heaven for it."],"id":284,"m":"Truly, sir, she is pretty, and virtuous, and gentle; and she is a friend of yours, I can tell you that in passing, thank heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Shall I do any good, think'st thou? Shall I not lose my suit?"],"id":285,"m":"Do you think I'll have any success? Won't I lose my suit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Troth, sir, all is in His hands above. But notwithstanding, Master","Fenton, I'll be sworn on a book she loves you. Have not your worship a","wart above your eye?"],"id":286,"m":"Honestly, sir, it's all in the hands of God above. But all the same, Master Fenton, I'd swear on the Bible she loves you. Doesn't your worship have a wart above your eye?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Yes, marry, have I; what of that?"],"id":287,"m":"Yes, I do indeed; what about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Well, thereby hangs a tale. Good faith, it is such another Nan! But, I","detest, an honest maid as ever broke bread. We had an hour's talk of","that wart. I shall never laugh but in that maid's company. But, indeed,","she is given too much to allicholy and musing. But for you—well, go to."],"id":288,"m":"Well, there's a story about that. Truly, she's such a girl, our Nan! But, I swear (I mean protest), as virtuous a girl as ever broke bread. We talked about that wart for an hour. I never laugh except when I'm with that girl. But she is really too given to melancholy and daydreaming. But as for you, well, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Well, I shall see her today. Hold, there's money for thee. Let me have","thy voice in my behalf. If thou seest her before me, commend me."],"id":289,"m":"Well, I will see her today. Here, there's some money for you. Speak up for me. If you see her before I do, give her my regards."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Will I? I' faith, that we will! And I will tell your worship more of","the wart the next time we have confidence, and of other wooers."],"id":290,"m":"Will I? Of course I will! And I'll tell your worship more about the wart the next time we have a confidential talk, and about other suitors."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Well, farewell, I am in great haste now."],"id":291,"m":"Well, goodbye; I'm in a great hurry now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Farewell to your worship."],"id":292,"m":"Goodbye, your worship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Fenton.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["Truly, an honest gentleman—but Anne loves him not, for I know Anne's","mind as well as another does. Out upon 't, what have I forgot?"],"id":294,"m":"Truly, an honest gentleman. But Anne doesn't love him, because I know Anne's mind as well as anyone does. Oh dear, what have I forgotten?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":295},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Page's house.","id":296},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page reading a letter.","id":297},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What, have I scaped love-letters in the holiday-time of my beauty, and","am I now a subject for them? Let me see.","[Reads] Ask me no reason why I love you, for though Love use Reason","for his precisian, he admits him not for his counsellor. You are not","young, no more am I. Go to, then, there's sympathy. You are merry, so","am I. Ha, ha, then there's more sympathy. You love sack, and so do I.","Would you desire better sympathy? Let it suffice thee, Mistress Page,","at the least, if the love of soldier can suffice, that I love thee. I","will not say, pity me—'tis not a soldier-like phrase—but I say love me.","By me,","Thine own true knight,","By day or night,","Or any kind of light,","With all his might,","For thee to fight,","John Falstaff.","What a Herod of Jewry is this! O wicked, wicked world! One that is","well-nigh worn to pieces with age, to show himself a young gallant!","What an unweighed behaviour hath this Flemish drunkard picked—with the","devil's name!—out of my conversation, that he dares in this manner","assay me? Why, he hath not been thrice in my company! What should I say","to him? I was then frugal of my mirth. Heaven forgive me! Why, I'll","exhibit a bill in the parliament for the putting down of men. How shall","I be revenged on him? For revenged I will be, as sure as his guts are","made of puddings."],"id":298,"m":"What, did I escape love letters in the springtime of my beauty, only to become a target for them now? Let me see. (Reads) \"Don't ask me the reason why I love you, for though Love may use Reason as his strict adviser, he never takes him as his counsellor. You are not young, and neither am I. Well then, there's a bond between us. You are merry, and so am I. Ha, ha, there's more of a bond. You love sack, and so do I. Could you want a better bond? Let it be enough for you, Mistress Page, at least if a soldier's love is enough, that I love you. I won't say 'pity me', that is not a soldierly phrase, but I say love me. Signed, your own true knight, by day or night, or any kind of light, with all his might, for you to fight, John Falstaff.\" What a ranting tyrant this is! Oh wicked, wicked world! A man almost worn to pieces with age, showing off as a young lover! What careless behaviour has this Dutch drunkard picked out of my conversation, in the devil's name, that he dares try me like this? Why, he's barely been in my company three times! What could I have said to him? I was sparing with my jokes then. Heaven forgive me! Why, I'll bring a bill before Parliament to have men abolished. How shall I get my revenge on him? Because revenge I will have, as surely as his guts are made of sausages."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford.","id":299},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Mistress Page! Trust me, I was going to your house."],"id":300,"m":"Mistress Page! Honestly, I was on my way to your house."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["And, trust me, I was coming to you. You look very ill."],"id":301,"m":"And honestly, I was coming to you. You look very unwell."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, I'll ne'er believe that. I have to show to the contrary."],"id":302,"m":"No, I'll never believe that. I have something to show that proves the opposite."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Faith, but you do, in my mind."],"id":303,"m":"Well, you do look it, to my mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Well, I do, then. Yet I say I could show you to the contrary. O,","Mistress Page, give me some counsel."],"id":304,"m":"All right, I do, then. But I tell you I could show you the opposite. Oh, Mistress Page, give me some advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What's the matter, woman?"],"id":305,"m":"What's the matter, woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["O woman, if it were not for one trifling respect, I could come to such","honour!"],"id":306,"m":"Oh woman, if it weren't for one little consideration, I could rise to such honour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Hang the trifle, woman; take the honour. What is it? Dispense with","trifles. What is it?"],"id":307,"m":"Hang the little consideration, woman; take the honour. What is it? Forget the trifles. What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["If I would but go to hell for an eternal moment or so, I could be","knighted."],"id":308,"m":"If I would just go to hell for an eternal moment or so, I could be made a knight."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What? Thou liest! Sir Alice Ford! These knights will hack, and so thou","shouldst not alter the article of thy gentry."],"id":309,"m":"What? You're lying! Sir Alice Ford! These knights are ten a penny, so you shouldn't change your rank as a gentlewoman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["We burn daylight. Here, read, read. Perceive how I might be knighted. I","shall think the worse of fat men as long as I have an eye to make","difference of men's liking. And yet he would not swear; praised women's","modesty; and gave such orderly and well-behaved reproof to all","uncomeliness that I would have sworn his disposition would have gone to","the truth of his words. But they do no more adhere and keep place","together than the Hundredth Psalm to the tune of \"Greensleeves.\" What","tempest, I trow, threw this whale, with so many tuns of oil in his","belly, ashore at Windsor? How shall I be revenged on him? I think the","best way were to entertain him with hope, till the wicked fire of lust","have melted him in his own grease. Did you ever hear the like?"],"id":310,"m":"We're wasting time. Here, read it, read it. See how I could be made a knight. I'll think worse of fat men as long as I have eyes to tell men apart. And yet he never swore; he praised women's modesty, and gave such proper, well-mannered criticism of all bad behaviour that I would have sworn his nature matched his words. But they go together no better than the Hundredth Psalm sung to the tune of \"Greensleeves\". What storm, I wonder, washed this whale, with so many barrels of oil in his belly, ashore at Windsor? How shall I get my revenge on him? I think the best way would be to keep him hoping until the wicked fire of lust has melted him in his own fat. Have you ever heard anything like it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Letter for letter, but that the name of Page and Ford differs! To thy","great comfort in this mystery of ill opinions, here's the twin brother","of thy letter. But let thine inherit first, for I protest mine never","shall. I warrant he hath a thousand of these letters, writ with blank","space for different names—sure, more, and these are of the second","edition. He will print them, out of doubt; for he cares not what he","puts into the press, when he would put us two. I had rather be a","giantess and lie under Mount Pelion. Well, I will find you twenty","lascivious turtles ere one chaste man."],"id":311,"m":"Letter for letter, except that the names Page and Ford are different! To comfort you in this mystery of our bad reputations, here is the twin brother of your letter. But let yours be the heir, because I swear mine never will. I bet he has a thousand of these letters, written with a blank space for different names; more, surely, and these are the second edition. No doubt he will print them, since he doesn't care what he puts in the press, when he would put the two of us there. I'd rather be a giantess buried under Mount Pelion. Well, I could find you twenty lustful turtledoves before one chaste man."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, this is the very same—the very hand, the very words. What doth he","think of us?"],"id":312,"m":"Why, this is exactly the same: the same handwriting, the same words. What does he think of us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Nay, I know not. It makes me almost ready to wrangle with mine own","honesty. I'll entertain myself like one that I am not acquainted","withal; for, sure, unless he know some strain in me that I know not","myself, he would never have boarded me in this fury."],"id":313,"m":"I don't know. It almost makes me ready to argue with my own virtue. I'll treat myself like someone I don't know; because surely, unless he knows some streak in me I don't know myself, he would never have attacked me so boldly."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["\"Boarding\" call you it? I'll be sure to keep him above deck."],"id":314,"m":"\"Boarding\", you call it? I'll make sure to keep him above deck."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["So will I. If he come under my hatches, I'll never to sea again. Let's","be revenged on him. Let's appoint him a meeting, give him a show of","comfort in his suit, and lead him on with a fine-baited delay, till he","hath pawned his horses to mine host of the Garter."],"id":315,"m":"So will I. If he gets below my hatches, I'll never go to sea again. Let's get our revenge on him. Let's arrange a meeting with him, give him a show of encouragement in his courtship, and lead him on with well-baited delays until he has pawned his horses to the landlord of the Garter."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, I will consent to act any villainy against him that may not sully","the chariness of our honesty. O, that my husband saw this letter! It","would give eternal food to his jealousy."],"id":316,"m":"Yes, I will agree to do any mischief against him that won't stain our careful good name. Oh, if my husband saw this letter! It would feed his jealousy for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why, look where he comes; and my good man too. He's as far from","jealousy as I am from giving him cause, and that, I hope, is an","unmeasurable distance."],"id":317,"m":"Why, look, here he comes, and my good husband too. He is as far from jealousy as I am from giving him cause, and that, I hope, is an immeasurable distance."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["You are the happier woman."],"id":318,"m":"You are the luckier woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Let's consult together against this greasy knight. Come hither."],"id":319,"m":"Let's plan together against this greasy knight. Come over here."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":320},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford with Pistol, and Page with Nym.","id":321},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well, I hope it be not so."],"id":322,"m":"Well, I hope it isn't true."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Hope is a curtal dog in some affairs.","Sir John affects thy wife."],"id":323,"m":"Hope is a dog with its tail cut short in some matters. Sir John is after your wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Why, sir, my wife is not young."],"id":324,"m":"Why, sir, my wife is not young."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["He woos both high and low, both rich and poor,","Both young and old, one with another, Ford.","He loves the gallimaufry. Ford, perpend."],"id":325,"m":"He woos high and low, rich and poor, young and old, all alike, Ford. He loves a mixed dish. Ford, think about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Love my wife?"],"id":326,"m":"Loves my wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["With liver burning hot.","Prevent, or go thou like Sir Actaeon he,","With Ringwood at thy heels.","O, odious is the name!"],"id":327,"m":"With a burning hot liver. Stop him, or you'll go like Actaeon, with the hound Ringwood at your heels. Oh, the name is hateful!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["What name, sir?"],"id":328,"m":"What name, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The horn, I say. Farewell.","Take heed, have open eye, for thieves do foot by night.","Take heed, ere summer comes, or cuckoo birds do sing.—","Away, Sir Corporal Nym.—Believe it, Page, he speaks sense."],"id":329,"m":"The horn, I say. Goodbye. Be careful, keep your eyes open, for thieves walk at night. Be careful before summer comes and the cuckoo sings. Come away, Corporal Nym. Believe him, Page; he talks sense."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pistol.","id":330},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["[Aside] I will be patient. I will find out this."],"id":331,"m":"(Aside) I will be patient. I will find out about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"NYM","lines":["[To Page] And this is true, I like not the humour of lying. He hath","wronged me in some humours. I should have borne the humoured letter to","her; but I have a sword, and it shall bite upon my necessity. He loves","your wife; there's the short and the long. My name is Corporal Nym. I","speak, and I avouch 'tis true. My name is Nym, and Falstaff loves your","wife. Adieu. I love not the humour of bread and cheese. Adieu."],"id":332,"m":"(To Page) And this is true; I don't like lying. He has wronged me in certain ways. I was supposed to carry his fancy letter to her; but I have a sword, and it will bite when I need it. He loves your wife; that's the long and short of it. My name is Corporal Nym. I say it and I swear it's true. My name is Nym, and Falstaff loves your wife. Goodbye. I don't care for a diet of bread and cheese. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Nym.","id":333},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[Aside] \"The humour of it,\" quoth 'a! Here's a fellow frights","English out of his wits."],"id":334,"m":"(Aside) \"The humour of it,\" he says! Here's a fellow who frightens English out of its wits."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["[Aside] I will seek out Falstaff."],"id":335,"m":"(Aside) I will look for Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[Aside] I never heard such a drawling, affecting rogue."],"id":336,"m":"(Aside) I never heard such a drawling, affected rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["[Aside] If I do find it—well."],"id":337,"m":"(Aside) If I do find it's true, well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[Aside] I will not believe such a Cataian, though the priest o' the","town commended him for a true man."],"id":338,"m":"(Aside) I won't believe such a scoundrel, even if the town priest vouched for him as an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["[Aside] 'Twas a good sensible fellow—well."],"id":339,"m":"(Aside) He was a good, sensible fellow. Well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Mistress Page and Mistress Ford come forward.","id":340},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["How now, Meg?"],"id":341,"m":"Hello, Meg!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Whither go you, George? Hark you."],"id":342,"m":"Where are you going, George? Listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["How now, sweet Frank, why art thou melancholy?"],"id":343,"m":"What's the matter, dear Frank? Why are you gloomy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I melancholy? I am not melancholy. Get you home, go."],"id":344,"m":"Me, gloomy? I'm not gloomy. Go home, go."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Faith, thou hast some crotchets in thy head now.—Will you go, Mistress","Page?"],"id":345,"m":"Really, you have some strange notion in your head now. Shall we go, Mistress Page?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Have with you. You'll come to dinner, George?","[Aside to Mistress Ford] Look who comes yonder. She shall be our","messenger to this paltry knight."],"id":346,"m":"I'm with you. You'll come home to dinner, George? (Aside to Mistress Ford) Look who's coming over there. She will be our messenger to this worthless knight."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["[Aside to Mistress Page] Trust me, I thought on her. She'll fit it."],"id":347,"m":"(Aside to Mistress Page) Honestly, I was thinking of her. She'll be just right for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly.","id":348},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["You are come to see my daughter Anne?"],"id":349,"m":"Have you come to see my daughter Anne?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Ay, forsooth. And, I pray, how does good Mistress Anne?"],"id":350,"m":"Yes, truly. And please, how is good Mistress Anne?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Go in with us and see. We'd have an hour's talk with you."],"id":351,"m":"Come in with us and see. We'd like an hour's talk with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Mistress Page, Mistress Ford and Mistress Quickly.","id":352},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["How now, Master Ford?"],"id":353,"m":"Well, Master Ford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["You heard what this knave told me, did you not?"],"id":354,"m":"You heard what this rogue told me, didn't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Yes, and you heard what the other told me?"],"id":355,"m":"Yes, and you heard what the other one told me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Do you think there is truth in them?"],"id":356,"m":"Do you think there is any truth in them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Hang 'em, slaves! I do not think the knight would offer it, but these","that accuse him in his intent towards our wives are a yoke of his","discarded men, very rogues, now they be out of service."],"id":357,"m":"Hang them, the wretches! I don't think the knight would try it; and these men who accuse him of designs on our wives are a pair of his sacked servants, real rogues, now that they're out of work."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Were they his men?"],"id":358,"m":"Were they his men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Marry, were they."],"id":359,"m":"Yes, they were."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I like it never the better for that. Does he lie at the Garter?"],"id":360,"m":"I don't like it any better for that. Is he staying at the Garter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Ay, marry, does he. If he should intend this voyage toward my wife, I","would turn her loose to him; and what he gets more of her than sharp","words, let it lie on my head."],"id":361,"m":"Yes, he is. If he were planning this voyage towards my wife, I would let her loose on him; and if he gets anything more from her than sharp words, let the blame fall on my head."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I do not misdoubt my wife, but I would be loath to turn them together.","A man may be too confident. I would have nothing lie on my head. I","cannot be thus satisfied."],"id":362,"m":"I don't doubt my wife, but I wouldn't like to leave them together. A man can be too confident. I wouldn't want anything to fall on my head. I can't be satisfied like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Host.","id":363},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Look where my ranting host of the Garter comes. There is either liquor","in his pate or money in his purse when he looks so merrily.—How now,","mine host?"],"id":364,"m":"Look, here comes my loud landlord of the Garter. Either there's drink in his head or money in his purse when he looks so cheerful. Hello, landlord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["How now, bully rook? Thou'rt a gentleman.—Cavaliero Justice, I say!"],"id":365,"m":"Hello, my fine fellow! You're a gentleman. Sir Justice, come on, I say!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shallow.","id":366},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I follow, mine host, I follow.—Good even and twenty, good Master Page.","Master Page, will you go with us? We have sport in hand."],"id":367,"m":"I'm coming, landlord, I'm coming. Good evening twenty times over, good Master Page. Master Page, will you come with us? We have some fun on hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Tell him, Cavaliero Justice; tell him, bully rook."],"id":368,"m":"Tell him, Sir Justice; tell him, my fine fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir, there is a fray to be fought between Sir Hugh the Welsh priest and","Caius the French doctor."],"id":369,"m":"Sir, there is a duel to be fought between Sir Hugh, the Welsh priest, and Caius, the French doctor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Good mine host o' the Garter, a word with you."],"id":370,"m":"Good landlord of the Garter, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What say'st thou, my bully rook?"],"id":371,"m":"What do you say, my fine fellow?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Ford and the Host talk apart.","id":372},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["[To Page] Will you go with us to behold it? My merry host hath had","the measuring of their weapons, and, I think, hath appointed them","contrary places; for, believe me, I hear the parson is no jester. Hark,","I will tell you what our sport shall be."],"id":373,"m":"(To Page) Will you come with us to watch it? My merry landlord has been put in charge of their weapons and, I think, has sent them to different places; because, believe me, I hear the parson is no joker. Listen, I'll tell you what our fun will be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shallow and Page talk apart. Ford and the Host come forward.","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Hast thou no suit against my knight, my guest cavaliero?"],"id":375,"m":"You don't have a quarrel with my knight, my noble guest, do you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["None, I protest. But I'll give you a pottle of burnt sack to give me","recourse to him, and tell him my name is Brook, only for a jest."],"id":376,"m":"None, I swear. But I'll give you a half-gallon of hot sack if you get me in to see him and tell him my name is Brook, just as a joke."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["My hand, bully. Thou shalt have egress and regress—said I well?—and thy","name shall be Brook. It is a merry knight. Will you go, myn-heers?"],"id":377,"m":"Shake on it, my fine fellow. You shall have the run of the place (well said, wasn't it?) and your name shall be Brook. He's a merry knight. Shall we go, gentlemen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Have with you, mine host."],"id":378,"m":"I'm with you, landlord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["I have heard the Frenchman hath good skill in his rapier."],"id":379,"m":"I've heard the Frenchman is very skilled with the rapier."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Tut, sir, I could have told you more. In these times you stand on","distance—your passes, stoccadoes, and I know not what. 'Tis the heart,","Master Page; 'tis here, 'tis here. I have seen the time, with my long","sword I would have made you four tall fellows skip like rats."],"id":380,"m":"Pooh, sir, I could have told you more. These days you fuss about distance, your passes, your thrusts and I don't know what. It's courage, Master Page; it's here, it's here. There was a time when with my long sword I'd have made four big fellows skip like rats."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Here, boys, here, here! Shall we wag?"],"id":381,"m":"Come on, boys, come on! Shall we go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Have with you. I had rather hear them scold than fight."],"id":382,"m":"I'm with you. I'd rather hear them argue than fight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Host, Shallow and Page.","id":383},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Though Page be a secure fool, and stands so firmly on his wife's","frailty, yet I cannot put off my opinion so easily. She was in his","company at Page's house, and what they made there I know not. Well, I","will look further into 't, and I have a disguise to sound Falstaff. If","I find her honest, I lose not my labour. If she be otherwise, 'tis","labour well bestowed."],"id":384,"m":"Page may be an overconfident fool who relies so firmly on his weak wife, but I can't dismiss my suspicion so easily. She was with Falstaff at Page's house, and I don't know what they did there. Well, I will look into it further, and I have a disguise to sound out Falstaff. If I find she is faithful, my effort is not wasted. If she isn't, it's effort well spent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":385},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":386},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff and Pistol.","id":387},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I will not lend thee a penny."],"id":388,"m":"I won't lend you a penny."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Why then, the world's mine oyster,","Which I with sword will open."],"id":389,"m":"Then the world is my oyster, which I will open with my sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Not a penny. I have been content, sir, you should lay my countenance to","pawn; I have grated upon my good friends for three reprieves for you","and your coach-fellow Nym, or else you had looked through the grate","like a gemini of baboons. I am damned in hell for swearing to gentlemen","my friends you were good soldiers and tall fellows. And when Mistress","Bridget lost the handle of her fan, I took 't upon mine honour thou","hadst it not."],"id":390,"m":"Not a penny. I've allowed you to pawn my good name, sir; I've pestered my good friends for three pardons for you and your partner Nym, or else you'd have been staring through prison bars like a pair of baboons. I am damned to hell for swearing to gentlemen, my friends, that you were good soldiers and brave fellows. And when Mistress Bridget lost the handle of her fan, I swore on my honour that you didn't have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Didst not thou share? Hadst thou not fifteen pence?"],"id":391,"m":"Didn't you get a share? Didn't you get fifteen pence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Reason, you rogue, reason. Think'st thou I'll endanger my soul gratis?","At a word, hang no more about me, I am no gibbet for you. Go—a short","knife and a throng—to your manor of Pickt-hatch, go. You'll not bear a","letter for me, you rogue? You stand upon your honour! Why, thou","unconfinable baseness, it is as much as I can do to keep the terms of","my honour precise. Ay, ay, I myself sometimes, leaving the fear of God","on the left hand, and hiding mine honour in my necessity, am fain to","shuffle, to hedge, and to lurch; and yet you, rogue, will ensconce your","rags, your cat-a-mountain looks, your red-lattice phrases, and your","bold beating oaths, under the shelter of your honour! You will not do","it! You!"],"id":392,"m":"That was only fair, you rogue, only fair. Do you think I'll risk my soul for nothing? In a word, stop hanging around me; I am not your gallows. Go off, with a short knife for cutting purses in a crowd, to your manor in the brothel district, go. You won't carry a letter for me, you rogue? You stand on your honour! Why, you limitless lowlife, it is as much as I can do to keep my own honour exact. Yes, yes, even I, leaving the fear of God on one side and hiding my honour behind my needs, sometimes have to cheat, to dodge and to steal; and yet you, rogue, want to shelter your rags, your wildcat looks, your tavern talk and your loud bullying oaths behind your honour! You won't do it! You!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I do relent. What wouldst thou more of man?"],"id":393,"m":"I give in. What more can you ask of a man?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Robin.","id":394},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["Sir, here's a woman would speak with you."],"id":395,"m":"Sir, there's a woman here who wants to speak to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let her approach."],"id":396,"m":"Let her come in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly.","id":397},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Give your worship good morrow."],"id":398,"m":"Good morning to your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good morrow, goodwife."],"id":399,"m":"Good morning, good wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Not so, an't please your worship."],"id":400,"m":"Not a wife, if it please your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good maid, then."],"id":401,"m":"Good maid, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["I'll be sworn, as my mother was, the first hour I was born."],"id":402,"m":"I swear I'm as much a maid as my mother was the hour I was born."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I do believe the swearer. What with me?"],"id":403,"m":"I believe the swearer. What do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Shall I vouchsafe your worship a word or two?"],"id":404,"m":"May I grant your worship a word or two?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Two thousand, fair woman; and I'll vouchsafe thee the hearing."],"id":405,"m":"Two thousand, fair woman, and I'll grant you a hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["There is one Mistress Ford, sir—I pray, come a little nearer this ways.","I myself dwell with Master Doctor Caius."],"id":406,"m":"There is a Mistress Ford, sir (please come a little closer, this way). I myself live with Doctor Caius."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, on; Mistress Ford, you say—"],"id":407,"m":"Well, go on. Mistress Ford, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Your worship says very true. I pray your worship come a little nearer","this ways."],"id":408,"m":"Your worship is quite right. Please, your worship, come a little closer this way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I warrant thee, nobody hears. Mine own people, mine own people."],"id":409,"m":"I promise you, nobody can hear. They're my own people, my own people."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Are they so? God bless them, and make them His servants!"],"id":410,"m":"Are they? God bless them and make them His servants!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, Mistress Ford, what of her?"],"id":411,"m":"Well, Mistress Ford, what about her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Why, sir, she's a good creature. Lord, Lord, your worship's a wanton!","Well, heaven forgive you, and all of us, I pray!"],"id":412,"m":"Why, sir, she's a good creature. Lord, Lord, your worship is a naughty one! Well, heaven forgive you, and all of us, I pray!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mistress Ford, come, Mistress Ford."],"id":413,"m":"Mistress Ford, come on, Mistress Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Marry, this is the short and the long of it: you have brought her into","such a canaries as 'tis wonderful. The best courtier of them all, when","the court lay at Windsor, could never have brought her to such a","canary. Yet there has been knights, and lords, and gentlemen, with","their coaches, I warrant you, coach after coach, letter after letter,","gift after gift, smelling so sweetly, all musk, and so rushling, I","warrant you, in silk and gold, and in such alligant terms, and in such","wine and sugar of the best and the fairest, that would have won any","woman's heart; and I warrant you, they could never get an eye-wink of","her. I had myself twenty angels given me this morning, but I defy all","angels in any such sort, as they say, but in the way of honesty. And, I","warrant you, they could never get her so much as sip on a cup with the","proudest of them all. And yet there has been earls—nay, which is more,","pensioners—but, I warrant you, all is one with her."],"id":414,"m":"Well, this is the long and short of it: you have put her in such a flutter, it's amazing. The best courtier of them all, when the court was at Windsor, could never have got her in such a flutter. And yet there have been knights and lords and gentlemen, with their coaches, I promise you, coach after coach, letter after letter, gift after gift, smelling so sweet, all musk, and rustling, I promise you, in silk and gold, with such elegant words, and such wine and sugar of the finest and best, that would have won any woman's heart; and I promise you, they could never get so much as a wink out of her. I had twenty gold angels given me myself this morning, but I scorn angels of that kind, as they say, unless it's honestly. And I promise you, they could never get her to so much as sip from a cup with the proudest of them. And there have been earls; no, what's more, royal guards; but, I promise you, it's all the same to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But what says she to me? Be brief, my good she-Mercury."],"id":415,"m":"But what does she say to me? Keep it short, my good female Mercury."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Marry, she hath received your letter, for the which she thanks you a","thousand times; and she gives you to notify that her husband will be","absence from his house between ten and eleven."],"id":416,"m":"Well, she has received your letter, for which she thanks you a thousand times; and she wants you to know that her husband will be away from his house between ten and eleven."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ten and eleven?"],"id":417,"m":"Ten and eleven?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Ay, forsooth; and then you may come and see the picture, she says, that","you wot of. Master Ford, her husband, will be from home. Alas, the","sweet woman leads an ill life with him. He's a very jealousy man; she","leads a very frampold life with him, good heart."],"id":418,"m":"Yes, truly; and then you can come and see the picture, she says, that you know about. Master Ford, her husband, will be away from home. Alas, the sweet woman has a hard life with him. He's a very jealous man; she has a very troubled life with him, poor heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ten and eleven. Woman, commend me to her; I will not fail her."],"id":419,"m":"Ten and eleven. Woman, give her my regards; I won't let her down."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Why, you say well. But I have another messenger to your worship.","Mistress Page hath her hearty commendations to you too; and let me tell","you in your ear, she's as fartuous a civil modest wife, and one, I tell","you, that will not miss you morning nor evening prayer, as any is in","Windsor, whoe'er be the other; and she bade me tell your worship that","her husband is seldom from home, but she hopes there will come a time.","I never knew a woman so dote upon a man. Surely I think you have","charms, la! Yes, in truth."],"id":420,"m":"Well said. But I have another message for your worship. Mistress Page sends her warm regards to you too; and let me tell you in your ear, she's as virtuous, decent, modest a wife, and one, I tell you, who never misses morning or evening prayer, as any in Windsor, whoever the other may be; and she told me to tell your worship that her husband is seldom away from home, but she hopes a time will come. I never knew a woman so besotted with a man. Surely you must have charms, I'm sure! Yes, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Not I, I assure thee. Setting the attraction of my good parts aside, I","have no other charms."],"id":421,"m":"Not I, I assure you. Apart from the attraction of my good qualities, I have no other charms."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Blessing on your heart for 't!"],"id":422,"m":"Bless your heart for it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But, I pray thee, tell me this: has Ford's wife and Page's wife","acquainted each other how they love me?"],"id":423,"m":"But please tell me this: have Ford's wife and Page's wife told each other that they love me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["That were a jest indeed! They have not so little grace, I hope. That","were a trick indeed! But Mistress Page would desire you to send her","your little page, of all loves. Her husband has a marvellous infection","to the little page; and, truly, Master Page is an honest man. Never a","wife in Windsor leads a better life than she does. Do what she will,","say what she will, take all, pay all, go to bed when she list, rise","when she list, all is as she will, and truly she deserves it, for if","there be a kind woman in Windsor, she is one. You must send her your","page, no remedy."],"id":424,"m":"That would be a joke indeed! I hope they have more sense than that. That would be a fine trick! But Mistress Page begs you, for love's sake, to send her your little page. Her husband has a wonderful affection for the little page; and truly, Master Page is an honest man. No wife in Windsor has a better life than she does. She does what she likes, says what she likes, takes everything, pays for everything, goes to bed when she likes, gets up when she likes; everything is as she wants it, and truly she deserves it, because if there is a kind woman in Windsor, she is one. You must send her your page; there's no way round it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, I will."],"id":425,"m":"Why, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Nay, but do so then, and, look you, he may come and go between you","both; and in any case have a nay-word, that you may know one another's","mind, and the boy never need to understand anything; for 'tis not good","that children should know any wickedness. Old folks, you know, have","discretion, as they say, and know the world."],"id":426,"m":"Then do so, and, you see, he can go back and forth between you both; and in any case have a password, so you can know each other's minds and the boy never needs to understand anything; because it's not good for children to know about any wickedness. Old people, you know, have discretion, as they say, and know the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Fare thee well, commend me to them both. There's my purse; I am yet thy","debtor. Boy, go along with this woman.—This news distracts me."],"id":427,"m":"Goodbye, give them both my regards. Here's my purse; I am still in your debt. Boy, go with this woman. This news drives me wild."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Mistress Quickly and Robin.","id":428},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["This punk is one of Cupid's carriers;","Clap on more sails, pursue; up with your fights;","Give fire! She is my prize, or ocean whelm them all!"],"id":429,"m":"This tart is one of Cupid's cargo ships. Put on more sail, chase her, raise the battle screens, fire! She is my prize, or let the ocean swallow them all!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pistol.","id":430},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Sayst thou so, old Jack? Go thy ways, I'll make more of thy old body","than I have done. Will they yet look after thee? Wilt thou, after the","expense of so much money, be now a gainer? Good body, I thank thee. Let","them say 'tis grossly done; so it be fairly done, no matter."],"id":431,"m":"Is that so, old Jack? Go on, I'll make more of your old body than I have done. Will they still pay attention to you? After spending so much money, will you now make a profit? Good body, thank you. Let them say it's done coarsely; as long as it's done well, no matter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph with a cup of sack.","id":432},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Sir John, there's one Master Brook below would fain speak with you and","be acquainted with you, and hath sent your worship a morning's draught","of sack."],"id":433,"m":"Sir John, there's a Master Brook downstairs who would like to speak with you and get to know you, and he has sent your worship a morning drink of sack."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Brook is his name?"],"id":434,"m":"Brook is his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":435,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Call him in."],"id":436,"m":"Send him in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":437},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Such Brooks are welcome to me, that o'erflow such liquor. Ah, ha,","Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, have I encompassed you? Go to, via!"],"id":438,"m":"Brooks that overflow with such drink are welcome to me. Aha, Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, have I got you surrounded? Come on, onward!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph with Ford disguised as Brook.","id":439},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["God bless you, sir."],"id":440,"m":"God bless you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And you, sir. Would you speak with me?"],"id":441,"m":"And you, sir. Do you want to speak with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I make bold to press with so little preparation upon you."],"id":442,"m":"I take the liberty of calling on you with so little warning."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You're welcome. What's your will?—Give us leave, drawer."],"id":443,"m":"You're welcome. What do you want? Leave us, waiter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":444},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Sir, I am a gentleman that have spent much. My name is Brook."],"id":445,"m":"Sir, I am a gentleman who has spent a great deal. My name is Brook."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good Master Brook, I desire more acquaintance of you."],"id":446,"m":"Good Master Brook, I would like to know you better."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Good Sir John, I sue for yours; not to charge you, for I must let you","understand I think myself in better plight for a lender than you are,","the which hath something emboldened me to this unseasoned intrusion;","for they say, if money go before, all ways do lie open."],"id":447,"m":"Good Sir John, I ask the same of you; not to be a burden to you, because I must tell you that I think I'm in a better position to lend than you are, which has made me bold enough for this sudden intrusion. For they say that if money leads the way, all roads lie open."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Money is a good soldier, sir, and will on."],"id":448,"m":"Money is a good soldier, sir, and will push on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Troth, and I have a bag of money here troubles me. If you will help to","bear it, Sir John, take all, or half, for easing me of the carriage."],"id":449,"m":"Truly, and I have a bag of money here that is bothering me. If you will help me carry it, Sir John, take all of it, or half, to relieve me of the load."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Sir, I know not how I may deserve to be your porter."],"id":450,"m":"Sir, I don't know how I have earned the right to be your porter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I will tell you, sir, if you will give me the hearing."],"id":451,"m":"I'll tell you, sir, if you'll listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Speak, good Master Brook. I shall be glad to be your servant."],"id":452,"m":"Speak, good Master Brook. I'll be glad to serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Sir, I hear you are a scholar—I will be brief with you—and you have","been a man long known to me, though I had never so good means as desire","to make myself acquainted with you. I shall discover a thing to you,","wherein I must very much lay open mine own imperfection. But, good Sir","John, as you have one eye upon my follies, as you hear them unfolded,","turn another into the register of your own, that I may pass with a","reproof the easier, sith you yourself know how easy it is to be such an","offender."],"id":453,"m":"Sir, I hear you are an educated man (I'll be brief with you), and I have known of you for a long time, though I never had the means, only the wish, to get acquainted with you. I am going to reveal something to you in which I must expose my own weakness a great deal. But, good Sir John, while you keep one eye on my follies as you hear them told, turn the other to the record of your own, so that I may get off with a lighter rebuke, since you know yourself how easy it is to commit such an offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Very well, sir, proceed."],"id":454,"m":"Very well, sir, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["There is a gentlewoman in this town, her husband's name is Ford."],"id":455,"m":"There is a lady in this town whose husband's name is Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, sir."],"id":456,"m":"Well, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I have long loved her, and, I protest to you, bestowed much on her,","followed her with a doting observance, engrossed opportunities to meet","her, fee'd every slight occasion that could but niggardly give me sight","of her, not only bought many presents to give her, but have given","largely to many to know what she would have given. Briefly, I have","pursued her as love hath pursued me, which hath been on the wing of all","occasions. But whatsoever I have merited, either in my mind or in my","means, meed, I am sure, I have received none, unless experience be a","jewel. That I have purchased at an infinite rate, and that hath taught","me to say this:","Love like a shadow flies when substance love pursues,","Pursuing that that flies, and flying what pursues."],"id":457,"m":"I have loved her for a long time and, I swear to you, spent a great deal on her. I have followed her with devoted attention, seized every chance to meet her, paid for every small occasion that might give me even a stingy glimpse of her, and not only bought her many presents but paid many people generously to find out what she would like to be given. In short, I have chased her as love has chased me, which is on the wings of every opportunity. But whatever I have deserved, in my feelings or my spending, I am sure I have received no reward, unless experience is a jewel. I have bought that at an enormous price, and it has taught me to say this: Love flies like a shadow when real wealth pursues it, chasing what runs away and running from what chases it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Have you received no promise of satisfaction at her hands?"],"id":458,"m":"Have you had no promise of satisfaction from her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Never."],"id":459,"m":"Never."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Have you importuned her to such a purpose?"],"id":460,"m":"Have you pressed her for it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Never."],"id":461,"m":"Never."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Of what quality was your love, then?"],"id":462,"m":"What kind of love was yours, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Like a fair house built on another man's ground, so that I have lost my","edifice by mistaking the place where I erected it."],"id":463,"m":"Like a fine house built on another man's land, so that I have lost my building by choosing the wrong place to put it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["To what purpose have you unfolded this to me?"],"id":464,"m":"Why have you told me all this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["When I have told you that, I have told you all. Some say that though","she appear honest to me, yet in other places she enlargeth her mirth so","far that there is shrewd construction made of her. Now, Sir John, here","is the heart of my purpose: you are a gentleman of excellent breeding,","admirable discourse, of great admittance, authentic in your place and","person, generally allowed for your many warlike, courtlike, and learned","preparations."],"id":465,"m":"When I've told you that, I've told you everything. Some say that although she seems chaste to me, elsewhere she lets her merriment go so far that people put a bad interpretation on it. Now, Sir John, here is the heart of my purpose: you are a gentleman of excellent breeding, wonderful conversation, welcome everywhere, of real standing in rank and person, widely admired for your many military, courtly and learned accomplishments."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O, sir!"],"id":466,"m":"Oh, sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Believe it, for you know it. There is money. Spend it, spend it; spend","more; spend all I have; only give me so much of your time in exchange","of it as to lay an amiable siege to the honesty of this Ford's wife.","Use your art of wooing, win her to consent to you. If any man may, you","may as soon as any."],"id":467,"m":"Believe it, because you know it's true. There is money. Spend it, spend it; spend more; spend everything I have; only give me enough of your time in return to lay a loving siege to the virtue of this Ford's wife. Use your skill in wooing and win her consent. If any man can, you can as well as anyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Would it apply well to the vehemency of your affection that I should","win what you would enjoy? Methinks you prescribe to yourself very","preposterously."],"id":468,"m":"Would it suit the strength of your passion for me to win what you want to enjoy? It seems to me you are prescribing a very backward treatment for yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["O, understand my drift. She dwells so securely on the excellency of her","honour that the folly of my soul dares not present itself; she is too","bright to be looked against. Now, could I come to her with any","detection in my hand, my desires had instance and argument to commend","themselves. I could drive her then from the ward of her purity, her","reputation, her marriage vow, and a thousand other her defences, which","now are too too strongly embattled against me. What say you to't, Sir","John?"],"id":469,"m":"Oh, understand my aim. She relies so confidently on her spotless honour that my foolish soul doesn't dare to show itself; she is too bright to look at. But if I could come to her with some proof of guilt in my hand, my desires would have an example and an argument to support them. Then I could drive her out of the fortress of her purity, her reputation, her marriage vows and a thousand other defences of hers, which are now far too strongly ranged against me. What do you say to that, Sir John?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Brook, I will first make bold with your money; next, give me","your hand; and last, as I am a gentleman, you shall, if you will, enjoy","Ford's wife."],"id":470,"m":"Master Brook, first I will make free with your money; next, give me your hand; and last, as I am a gentleman, you shall, if you wish, enjoy Ford's wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["O good sir!"],"id":471,"m":"Oh, good sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I say you shall."],"id":472,"m":"I say you shall."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Want no money, Sir John; you shall want none."],"id":473,"m":"Don't go short of money, Sir John; you won't go short."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Want no Mistress Ford, Master Brook; you shall want none. I shall be","with her, I may tell you, by her own appointment; even as you came in","to me, her assistant or go-between parted from me. I say I shall be","with her between ten and eleven, for at that time the jealous rascally","knave her husband will be forth. Come you to me at night. You shall","know how I speed."],"id":474,"m":"Don't go short of Mistress Ford, Master Brook; you won't go short. I'll be with her, I can tell you, at her own invitation; just as you came in to me, her assistant or go-between left me. I tell you I'll be with her between ten and eleven, because at that time her jealous, scoundrelly rogue of a husband will be out. Come to me tonight. You'll hear how I get on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I am blessed in your acquaintance. Do you know Ford, sir?"],"id":475,"m":"I am blessed to know you. Do you know Ford, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hang him, poor cuckoldly knave! I know him not. Yet I wrong him to call","him poor. They say the jealous wittolly knave hath masses of money, for","the which his wife seems to me well-favoured. I will use her as the key","of the cuckoldly rogue's coffer, and there's my harvest-home."],"id":476,"m":"Hang him, the poor cuckolded rogue! I don't know him. But I wrong him to call him poor. They say the jealous, cuckolded rogue has masses of money, which is why his wife seems pretty to me. I'll use her as the key to the cuckold's money chest, and there's my harvest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I would you knew Ford, sir, that you might avoid him if you saw him."],"id":477,"m":"I wish you knew Ford, sir, so you could avoid him if you saw him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hang him, mechanical salt-butter rogue! I will stare him out of his","wits, I will awe him with my cudgel; it shall hang like a meteor o'er","the cuckold's horns. Master Brook, thou shalt know I will predominate","over the peasant, and thou shalt lie with his wife. Come to me soon at","night. Ford's a knave, and I will aggravate his style. Thou, Master","Brook, shalt know him for knave and cuckold. Come to me soon at night."],"id":478,"m":"Hang him, the vulgar, cheap-butter rogue! I'll stare him out of his wits; I'll scare him with my club; it will hang like a meteor over the cuckold's horns. Master Brook, you'll see I'll get the better of the peasant, and you will sleep with his wife. Come to me tonight. Ford's a knave, and I will add to his titles. You, Master Brook, will know him for knave and cuckold. Come to me tonight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":479},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["What a damned epicurean rascal is this! My heart is ready to crack with","impatience. Who says this is improvident jealousy? My wife hath sent to","him, the hour is fixed, the match is made. Would any man have thought","this? See the hell of having a false woman: my bed shall be abused, my","coffers ransacked, my reputation gnawn at; and I shall not only receive","this villanous wrong, but stand under the adoption of abominable terms,","and by him that does me this wrong. Terms, names! Amaimon sounds well;","Lucifer, well; Barbason, well; yet they are devils' additions, the","names of fiends. But cuckold? Wittol? Cuckold? The devil himself hath","not such a name. Page is an ass, a secure ass; he will trust his wife,","he will not be jealous. I will rather trust a Fleming with my butter,","Parson Hugh the Welshman with my cheese, an Irishman with my aqua-vitae","bottle, or a thief to walk my ambling gelding, than my wife with","herself. Then she plots, then she ruminates, then she devises; and what","they think in their hearts they may effect, they will break their","hearts but they will effect. God be praised for my jealousy! Eleven","o'clock the hour. I will prevent this, detect my wife, be revenged on","Falstaff, and laugh at Page. I will about it. Better three hours too","soon than a minute too late. Fie, fie, fie! Cuckold, cuckold, cuckold!"],"id":480,"m":"What a damned, pleasure-loving rascal this is! My heart is ready to burst with fury. Who says this is careless jealousy? My wife has sent to him, the hour is fixed, the deal is done. Who would have thought it? See the hell of having an unfaithful wife: my bed will be abused, my money chests ransacked, my reputation gnawed at; and I will not only suffer this vile wrong but be called disgusting names, and by the very man who wrongs me. Names, titles! Amaimon sounds fine; Lucifer, fine; Barbason, fine; yet they are devils' titles, the names of fiends. But cuckold? Willing cuckold? Cuckold? The devil himself has no such name. Page is an ass, an overconfident ass; he will trust his wife, he will not be jealous. I would sooner trust a Dutchman with my butter, Parson Hugh the Welshman with my cheese, an Irishman with my whisky bottle, or a thief to walk my horse, than trust my wife alone with herself. Then she plots, then she broods, then she schemes; and what women think in their hearts they can do, they will break their hearts to do. God be praised for my jealousy! Eleven o'clock is the hour. I will stop this, catch my wife, take revenge on Falstaff and laugh at Page. I'll get to it. Better three hours too early than a minute too late. Shame, shame, shame! Cuckold, cuckold, cuckold!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":481},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A field near Windsor.","id":482},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Doctor Caius and Rugby.","id":483},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Jack Rugby!"],"id":484,"m":"Jack Rugby!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["Sir?"],"id":485,"m":"Sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vat is de clock, Jack?"],"id":486,"m":"What time is it, Jack?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["'Tis past the hour, sir, that Sir Hugh promised to meet."],"id":487,"m":"It's past the time, sir, when Sir Hugh promised to meet you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, he has save his soul, dat he is no come. He has pray his Pible","well dat he is no come. By gar, Jack Rugby, he is dead already, if he","be come."],"id":488,"m":"By God, he has saved his soul by not coming. He has prayed well from his Bible, since he hasn't come. By God, Jack Rugby, he is a dead man already if he comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["He is wise, sir; he knew your worship would kill him if he came."],"id":489,"m":"He's wise, sir; he knew your worship would kill him if he came."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, de herring is no dead so as I vill kill him. Take your rapier,","Jack; I vill tell you how I vill kill him."],"id":490,"m":"By God, a herring is not as dead as I will kill him. Take your rapier, Jack; I will show you how I will kill him."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["Alas, sir, I cannot fence."],"id":491,"m":"Oh, sir, I can't fence."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Villainy, take your rapier."],"id":492,"m":"Villain, take your rapier."},{"t":"sp","who":"RUGBY","lines":["Forbear; here's company."],"id":493,"m":"Stop; here come some people."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Shallow, Slender and Host.","id":494},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["God bless thee, bully doctor!"],"id":495,"m":"God bless you, my brave doctor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["God save you, Master Doctor Caius!"],"id":496,"m":"God save you, Doctor Caius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Now, good Master Doctor!"],"id":497,"m":"Good day, good Doctor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Give you good morrow, sir."],"id":498,"m":"Good morning to you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vat be all you, one, two, tree, four, come for?"],"id":499,"m":"What have you all come for, one, two, three, four of you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["To see thee fight, to see thee foin, to see thee traverse; to see thee","here, to see thee there; to see thee pass thy punto, thy stock, thy","reverse, thy distance, thy montant. Is he dead, my Ethiopian? Is he","dead, my Francisco? Ha, bully? What says my Aesculapius, my Galen, my","heart of elder, ha? Is he dead, bully stale? Is he dead?"],"id":500,"m":"To see you fight, to see you thrust, to see you dodge; to see you here, to see you there; to see you make your thrust, your stab, your backhand, your distance, your upward cut. Is he dead, my dark one? Is he dead, my Frenchman? Ha, my lad? What does my god of medicine say, my Galen, my heart of soft wood, ha? Is he dead, my fine piss-pot? Is he dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, he is de coward Jack-priest of de vorld. He is not show his","face."],"id":501,"m":"By God, he is the most cowardly rascal of a priest in the world. He hasn't shown his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Thou art a Castalion King Urinal Hector of Greece, my boy!"],"id":502,"m":"You are a Spanish king of urinals, a Hector of Greece, my boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["I pray you, bear witness that me have stay six or seven, two, tree","hours for him, and he is no come."],"id":503,"m":"Please bear witness that I have waited six or seven, two, three hours for him, and he hasn't come."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He is the wiser man, Master doctor. He is a curer of souls, and you a","curer of bodies. If you should fight, you go against the hair of your","professions. Is it not true, Master Page?"],"id":504,"m":"He's the wiser man, Doctor. He is a healer of souls and you are a healer of bodies. If you fought, you would go against the grain of your professions. Isn't that true, Master Page?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Master Shallow, you have yourself been a great fighter, though now a","man of peace."],"id":505,"m":"Master Shallow, you have been a great fighter yourself, though you're a man of peace now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Bodykins, Master Page, though I now be old, and of the peace, if I see","a sword out, my finger itches to make one. Though we are justices and","doctors and churchmen, Master Page, we have some salt of our youth in","us. We are the sons of women, Master Page."],"id":506,"m":"By God's body, Master Page, old as I am now, and a Justice of the Peace, if I see a drawn sword my finger itches to join in. Justices, doctors and churchmen as we are, Master Page, we still have some of the salt of our youth in us. We are the sons of women, Master Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["'Tis true, Master Shallow."],"id":507,"m":"That's true, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It will be found so, Master Page.—Master Doctor Caius, I come to fetch","you home. I am sworn of the peace. You have showed yourself a wise","physician, and Sir Hugh hath shown himself a wise and patient","churchman. You must go with me, Master Doctor."],"id":508,"m":"You'll find it so, Master Page. Doctor Caius, I have come to take you home. I am sworn to keep the peace. You have shown yourself a wise physician, and Sir Hugh has shown himself a wise and patient churchman. You must come with me, Doctor."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Pardon, guest justice.—A word, Monsieur Mockwater."],"id":509,"m":"Excuse me, my guest the Justice. A word, Monsieur Mockwater."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Mockvater? Vat is dat?"],"id":510,"m":"Mockwater? What is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Mockwater, in our English tongue, is valour, bully."],"id":511,"m":"Mockwater, in our English tongue, means courage, my lad."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, then I have as much mockvater as de Englishman. Scurvy jack-dog","priest! By gar, me vill cut his ears."],"id":512,"m":"By God, then I have as much mockwater as the Englishman. Lousy cur of a priest! By God, I will cut off his ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["He will clapper-claw thee tightly, bully."],"id":513,"m":"He will give you a sound thrashing, my lad."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Clapper-de-claw? Vat is dat?"],"id":514,"m":"Clapper-claw? What is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["That is, he will make thee amends."],"id":515,"m":"That is, he will apologise to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, me do look he shall clapper-de-claw me, for, by gar, me vill","have it."],"id":516,"m":"By God, I expect him to clapper-claw me, because, by God, I will have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["And I will provoke him to't, or let him wag."],"id":517,"m":"And I will urge him to do it, or let him go hang."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Me tank you for dat."],"id":518,"m":"I thank you for that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["And, moreover, bully—but first, Master guest, and Master Page, and eke","Cavaliero Slender, go you through the town to Frogmore."],"id":519,"m":"And besides, my lad... but first, my guest, and Master Page, and also Sir Slender, go through the town to Frogmore."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[Aside to Host] Sir Hugh is there, is he?"],"id":520,"m":"(Aside to Host) Sir Hugh is there, is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["[Aside to Page] He is there. See what humour he is in; and I will","bring the doctor about by the fields. Will it do well?"],"id":521,"m":"(Aside to Page) He's there. See what mood he's in, and I will bring the doctor round by the fields. Will that work?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["[Aside to Host] We will do it."],"id":522,"m":"(Aside to Host) We'll do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE, SHALLOW AND SLENDER","lines":["Adieu, good Master Doctor."],"id":523,"m":"Goodbye, good Doctor."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Page, Shallow and Slender.","id":524},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, me vill kill de priest, for he speak for a jackanape to Anne","Page."],"id":525,"m":"By God, I will kill the priest, because he is speaking for a monkey to Anne Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Let him die. Sheathe thy impatience; throw cold water on thy choler. Go","about the fields with me through Frogmore. I will bring thee where","Mistress Anne Page is, at a farm-house a-feasting, and thou shalt woo","her. Cried game! Said I well?"],"id":526,"m":"Let him die. Put away your impatience; throw cold water on your anger. Come round by the fields with me through Frogmore. I will take you to where Mistress Anne Page is feasting at a farmhouse, and you can woo her. The game is up! Well said?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, me tank you for dat. By gar, I love you; and I shall procure-a","you de good guest: de earl, de knight, de lords, de gentlemen, my","patients."],"id":527,"m":"By God, I thank you for that. By God, I love you; and I will get you good guests: the earl, the knight, the lords, the gentlemen, my patients."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["For the which I will be thy adversary toward Anne Page. Said I well?"],"id":528,"m":"For which I will be your adversary with Anne Page. Well said?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, 'tis good; vell said."],"id":529,"m":"By God, it is good; well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Let us wag, then."],"id":530,"m":"Let's go, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Come at my heels, Jack Rugby."],"id":531,"m":"Follow at my heels, Jack Rugby."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":532},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"A field near Frogmore.","id":533},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Hugh Evans and Simple.","id":534},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["I pray you now, good Master Slender's servingman, and friend Simple by","your name, which way have you looked for Master Caius, that calls","himself doctor of physic?"],"id":535,"m":"Please now, good Master Slender's servant, friend Simple by name, which way have you looked for Master Caius, who calls himself a doctor of medicine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Marry, sir, the Petty-ward, the Park-ward, every way; old Windsor way,","and every way but the town way."],"id":536,"m":"Well, sir, towards the town ward, towards the park, every way; the old Windsor way, and every way except the town way."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["I most fehemently desire you, you will also look that way."],"id":537,"m":"I most earnestly ask you to look that way too."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["I will, Sir."],"id":538,"m":"I will, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Simple.","id":539},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Pless my soul, how full of cholers I am, and trempling of mind! I shall","be glad if he have deceived me. How melancholies I am! I will knog his","urinals about his knave's costard when I have good opportunities for","the 'ork. Pless my soul!"],"id":540,"m":"Bless my soul, how full of anger I am, and trembling in my mind! I'll be glad if he has let me down. How melancholy I am! I'll knock his urine flasks about his rascal's head when I have a good chance to do the job. Bless my soul!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":541},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","cont":1,"lines":["~To shallow rivers, to whose falls","~Melodious birds sings madrigals.","~There will we make our peds of roses","~And a thousand fragrant posies.","~To shallow—","Mercy on me, I have a great dispositions to cry."],"id":542,"m":"To shallow rivers, by whose waterfalls melodious birds sing madrigals. There we will make our beds of roses and a thousand fragrant posies. To shallow... Mercy on me, I have a great urge to cry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":543},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","cont":1,"lines":["~Melodious birds sing madrigals—","~Whenas I sat in Pabylon—","~And a thousand vagram posies.","~To shallow rivers, to whose falls","~Melodious birds sing madrigals."],"id":544,"m":"Melodious birds sing madrigals... When I sat in Babylon... And a thousand fragrant posies. To shallow rivers, by whose waterfalls melodious birds sing madrigals."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Simple.","id":545},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Yonder he is, coming this way, Sir Hugh."],"id":546,"m":"There he is, coming this way, Sir Hugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["He's welcome."],"id":547,"m":"He's welcome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":548},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","cont":1,"lines":["~To shallow rivers, to whose falls—"],"id":549,"m":"To shallow rivers, by whose waterfalls..."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","cont":1,"lines":["Heaven prosper the right! What weapons is he?"],"id":550,"m":"Heaven favour the right side! What weapons does he have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["No weapons, sir. There comes my master, Master Shallow, and another","gentleman, from Frogmore, over the stile, this way."],"id":551,"m":"No weapons, sir. Here comes my master, Master Shallow, and another gentleman, from Frogmore, over the stile, this way."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Pray you, give me my gown—or else keep it in your arms."],"id":552,"m":"Please give me my gown, or no, keep it in your arms."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Shallow and Slender.","id":553},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["How now, Master Parson? Good morrow, good Sir Hugh. Keep a gamester","from the dice, and a good student from his book, and it is wonderful."],"id":554,"m":"Hello, Master Parson! Good morning, good Sir Hugh. Keep a gambler from his dice and a good student from his book, and it's a marvel."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["[Aside] Ah, sweet Anne Page!"],"id":555,"m":"(Aside) Ah, sweet Anne Page!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["God save you, good Sir Hugh!"],"id":556,"m":"God save you, good Sir Hugh!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["God pless you from his mercy sake, all of you!"],"id":557,"m":"God bless you all, for his mercy's sake!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["What, the sword and the word? Do you study them both, Master Parson?"],"id":558,"m":"What, the sword and the Bible? Do you study both, Master Parson?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["And youthful still—in your doublet and hose, this raw rheumatic day?"],"id":559,"m":"And still youthful, in just your jacket and breeches on this raw, damp day?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["There is reasons and causes for it."],"id":560,"m":"There are reasons and causes for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["We are come to you to do a good office, Master Parson."],"id":561,"m":"We have come to you to do a good deed, Master Parson."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Fery well; what is it?"],"id":562,"m":"Very well; what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Yonder is a most reverend gentleman who, belike having received wrong","by some person, is at most odds with his own gravity and patience that","ever you saw."],"id":563,"m":"Over there is a most respectable gentleman who, apparently having been wronged by someone, is more at odds with his own dignity and patience than you have ever seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I have lived fourscore years and upward; I never heard a man of his","place, gravity, and learning, so wide of his own respect."],"id":564,"m":"I have lived eighty years and more, and I never heard of a man of his position, dignity and learning so far from his own self-respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["What is he?"],"id":565,"m":"Who is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["I think you know him: Master Doctor Caius, the renowned French","physician."],"id":566,"m":"I think you know him: Doctor Caius, the famous French physician."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Got's will and His passion of my heart! I had as lief you would tell me","of a mess of porridge."],"id":567,"m":"God's will and His passion on my heart! I'd as soon you told me about a bowl of porridge."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Why?"],"id":568,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["He has no more knowledge in Hibbocrates and Galen, and he is a knave","besides, a cowardly knave as you would desires to be acquainted withal."],"id":569,"m":"He knows no more of Hippocrates and Galen than that, and he's a rogue besides, as cowardly a rogue as you could wish to meet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["I warrant you, he's the man should fight with him."],"id":570,"m":"I bet you he's the man who was meant to fight him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["[Aside] O, sweet Anne Page!"],"id":571,"m":"(Aside) Oh, sweet Anne Page!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It appears so by his weapons. Keep them asunder. Here comes Doctor","Caius."],"id":572,"m":"It looks like it, from his weapons. Keep them apart. Here comes Doctor Caius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Host, Caius and Rugby.","id":573},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Nay, good Master Parson, keep in your weapon."],"id":574,"m":"No, good Master Parson, put away your weapon."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["So do you, good Master Doctor."],"id":575,"m":"And you too, good Doctor."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Disarm them, and let them question. Let them keep their limbs whole and","hack our English."],"id":576,"m":"Disarm them and let them argue. Let them keep their limbs in one piece and hack our English instead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["I pray you, let-a me speak a word with your ear. Verefore will you not","meet-a me?"],"id":577,"m":"Please, let me have a word in your ear. Why didn't you come to meet me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["[Aside to Caius] Pray you, use your patience. In good time."],"id":578,"m":"(Aside to Caius) Please be patient. All in good time."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, you are de coward, de Jack dog, John ape."],"id":579,"m":"By God, you are a coward, a cur, a monkey."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["[Aside to Caius] Pray you, let us not be laughing stocks to other","men's humours. I desire you in friendship, and I will one way or other","make you amends.","[Aloud] By Jeshu, I will knog your urinal about your knave's","cogscomb."],"id":580,"m":"(Aside to Caius) Please, let's not be laughing stocks for other men's amusement. I ask for your friendship, and I will make it up to you one way or another. (Aloud) By Jesus, I'll knock your urine flask about your fool's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Diable! Jack Rugby, mine Host de Jarteer, have I not stay for him to","kill him? Have I not, at de place I did appoint?"],"id":581,"m":"Devil! Jack Rugby, my landlord of the Garter, haven't I waited for him to kill him? Haven't I, at the place I appointed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["As I am a Christians soul, now look you, this is the place appointed.","I'll be judgment by mine host of the Garter."],"id":582,"m":"As I am a Christian soul, look, this is the place appointed. I'll accept the judgement of my landlord of the Garter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Peace, I say, Gallia and Gaul, French and Welsh, soul-curer and","body-curer!"],"id":583,"m":"Quiet, I say, Gaul and Welshman, French and Welsh, soul-healer and body-healer!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Ay, dat is very good; excellent."],"id":584,"m":"Yes, that is very good; excellent."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Peace, I say! Hear mine host of the Garter. Am I politic? Am I subtle?","Am I a Machiavel? Shall I lose my doctor? No, he gives me the potions","and the motions. Shall I lose my parson, my priest, my Sir Hugh? No, he","gives me the proverbs and the no-verbs. [To Caius] Give me thy hand,","terrestrial; so. [To Evans] Give me thy hand, celestial; so. Boys of","art, I have deceived you both. I have directed you to wrong places.","Your hearts are mighty, your skins are whole, and let burnt sack be the","issue. Come, lay their swords to pawn. Follow me, lads of peace,","follow, follow, follow."],"id":585,"m":"Quiet, I say! Listen to your landlord of the Garter. Am I clever? Am I cunning? Am I a Machiavelli? Shall I lose my doctor? No, he gives me the medicines and the bowel movements. Shall I lose my parson, my priest, my Sir Hugh? No, he gives me the proverbs and the no-verbs. (To Caius) Give me your hand, earthly one; there. (To Evans) Give me your hand, heavenly one; there. Men of learning, I have tricked you both. I sent you to the wrong places. Your hearts are brave, your skins are unbroken, and let hot sack be the outcome. Come, let's pawn their swords. Follow me, lads of peace, follow, follow, follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Host.","id":586},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Afore God, a mad host! Follow, gentlemen, follow."],"id":587,"m":"Good God, a mad landlord! Follow, gentlemen, follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["[Aside] O, sweet Anne Page!"],"id":588,"m":"(Aside) Oh, sweet Anne Page!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Shallow, Slender and Page.","id":589},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Ha, do I perceive dat? Have you make-a de sot of us, ha, ha?"],"id":590,"m":"Ha, do I see what's going on? Have you made fools of us, ha, ha?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["This is well, he has made us his vlouting-stog. I desire you that we","may be friends, and let us knog our prains together to be revenge on","this same scall, scurvy, cogging companion, the host of the Garter."],"id":591,"m":"This is fine: he has made us his laughing stock. I ask that we be friends, and let us put our heads together to get revenge on this scabby, lousy, cheating fellow, the landlord of the Garter."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, with all my heart. He promise to bring me where is Anne Page;","by gar, he deceive me too."],"id":592,"m":"By God, with all my heart. He promised to take me to where Anne Page is; by God, he has tricked me too."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Well, I will smite his noddles. Pray you follow."],"id":593,"m":"Well, I will hit him on the head. Please follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":594},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"A street in Windsor.","id":595},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page following Robin.","id":596},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Nay, keep your way, little gallant. You were wont to be a follower, but","now you are a leader. Whether had you rather, lead mine eyes, or eye","your master's heels?"],"id":597,"m":"No, go on ahead, little gallant. You used to be a follower, but now you are a leader. Which would you rather do, lead my eyes, or eye your master's heels?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["I had rather, forsooth, go before you like a man than follow him like a","dwarf."],"id":598,"m":"Truly, I'd rather walk in front of you like a man than follow him like a dwarf."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["O, you are a flattering boy! Now I see you'll be a courtier."],"id":599,"m":"Oh, you're a flattering boy! I can see you'll be a courtier."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford.","id":600},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well met, Mistress Page. Whither go you?"],"id":601,"m":"Well met, Mistress Page. Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Truly, sir, to see your wife. Is she at home?"],"id":602,"m":"To see your wife, sir. Is she at home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Ay, and as idle as she may hang together, for want of company. I think","if your husbands were dead you two would marry."],"id":603,"m":"Yes, and as bored as she can be without falling apart, for lack of company. I think if your husbands were dead, you two would marry each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Be sure of that—two other husbands."],"id":604,"m":"You can be sure of that: two other husbands."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Where had you this pretty weathercock?"],"id":605,"m":"Where did you get this pretty weathercock?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I cannot tell what the dickens his name is my husband had him of. What","do you call your knight's name, sirrah?"],"id":606,"m":"I can't remember what the dickens the name is of the man my husband got him from. What is your knight's name, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["Sir John Falstaff."],"id":607,"m":"Sir John Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Sir John Falstaff!"],"id":608,"m":"Sir John Falstaff!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["He, he; I can never hit on's name. There is such a league between my","good man and he! Is your wife at home indeed?"],"id":609,"m":"That's him, that's him; I can never remember his name. There's such a friendship between my husband and him! Is your wife really at home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Indeed she is."],"id":610,"m":"She really is."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["By your leave, sir, I am sick till I see her."],"id":611,"m":"Excuse me, sir; I'm sick until I see her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Mistress Page and Robin.","id":612},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Has Page any brains? Hath he any eyes? Hath he any thinking? Sure, they","sleep; he hath no use of them. Why, this boy will carry a letter twenty","mile as easy as a cannon will shoot point-blank twelve score. He pieces","out his wife's inclination, he gives her folly motion and advantage.","And now she's going to my wife, and Falstaff's boy with her. A man may","hear this shower sing in the wind. And Falstaff's boy with her! Good","plots they are laid, and our revolted wives share damnation together.","Well, I will take him, then torture my wife, pluck the borrowed veil of","modesty from the so-seeming Mistress Page, divulge Page himself for a","secure and wilful Actaeon, and to these violent proceedings all my","neighbours shall cry aim. [Clock strikes] The clock gives me my cue,","and my assurance bids me search. There I shall find Falstaff. I shall","be rather praised for this than mocked, for it is as positive as the","earth is firm that Falstaff is there. I will go."],"id":613,"m":"Has Page any brains? Has he any eyes? Has he any power of thought? Surely they are asleep; he has no use for them. Why, this boy would carry a letter twenty miles as easily as a cannon shoots point-blank at two hundred and forty yards. He feeds his wife's inclinations; he gives her folly room and opportunity. And now she's going to my wife, with Falstaff's boy. A man can hear this storm coming in the wind. And Falstaff's boy with her! Fine plots they have laid, and our rebellious wives share their damnation together. Well, I will catch him, then torment my wife, pull the borrowed veil of modesty off the so-seemingly virtuous Mistress Page, and expose Page himself as an overconfident and willing cuckold, and all my neighbours will applaud these violent actions. (Clock strikes) The clock gives me my cue, and my certainty tells me to search. I'll find Falstaff there. I'll be praised for this rather than mocked, because it is as certain as the earth is solid that Falstaff is there. I'll go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Shallow, Slender, Host, Sir Hugh Evans, Caius and Rugby.","id":614},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW AND PAGE","lines":["Well met, Master Ford."],"id":615,"m":"Well met, Master Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Trust me, a good knot. I have good cheer at home, and I pray you all go","with me."],"id":616,"m":"Honestly, a fine group. I have good food at home, and I beg you all to come with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I must excuse myself, Master Ford."],"id":617,"m":"I must excuse myself, Master Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["And so must I, sir; we have appointed to dine with Mistress Anne, and I","would not break with her for more money than I'll speak of."],"id":618,"m":"And so must I, sir; we have arranged to dine with Mistress Anne, and I wouldn't break my word to her for more money than I'll mention."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["We have lingered about a match between Anne Page and my cousin Slender,","and this day we shall have our answer."],"id":619,"m":"We have been slow to settle a match between Anne Page and my cousin Slender, and today we'll get our answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I hope I have your good will, father Page."],"id":620,"m":"I hope I have your blessing, father Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["You have, Master Slender, I stand wholly for you.—But my wife, Master","doctor, is for you altogether."],"id":621,"m":"You do, Master Slender; I'm entirely on your side. But my wife, Doctor, is completely on yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Ay, be-gar; and de maid is love-a me! My nursh-a Quickly tell me so","mush."],"id":622,"m":"Yes, by God; and the girl loves me! My nurse Quickly told me so."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What say you to young Master Fenton? He capers, he dances, he has eyes","of youth, he writes verses, he speaks holiday, he smells April and May.","He will carry 't, he will carry 't. 'Tis in his buttons he will carry","'t."],"id":623,"m":"What do you say to young Master Fenton? He capers, he dances, he has young eyes, he writes poems, he talks in holiday style, he smells of April and May. He will win her, he will win her. It is in his nature to win her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Not by my consent, I promise you. The gentleman is of no having. He","kept company with the wild Prince and Poins. He is of too high a","region, he knows too much. No, he shall not knit a knot in his fortunes","with the finger of my substance. If he take her, let him take her","simply. The wealth I have waits on my consent, and my consent goes not","that way."],"id":624,"m":"Not with my consent, I promise you. The gentleman has no money. He kept company with the wild Prince Hal and Poins. He is of too high a rank, and he knows too much. No, he shall not tie up the loose ends of his fortune with the help of my wealth. If he takes her, let him take her with nothing. My wealth depends on my consent, and my consent doesn't go that way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I beseech you, heartily, some of you go home with me to dinner. Besides","your cheer, you shall have sport: I will show you a monster. Master","Doctor, you shall go; so shall you, Master Page, and you, Sir Hugh."],"id":625,"m":"I sincerely beg some of you to come home with me to dinner. Besides the food, you'll have some fun: I'll show you a monster. Doctor, you will come; so will you, Master Page, and you, Sir Hugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Well, fare you well. We shall have the freer wooing at Master Page's."],"id":626,"m":"Well, goodbye. We'll have freer wooing at Master Page's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Shallow and Slender.","id":627},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Go home, John Rugby; I come anon."],"id":628,"m":"Go home, John Rugby; I'll come soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Rugby.","id":629},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Farewell, my hearts. I will to my honest knight Falstaff, and drink","canary with him."],"id":630,"m":"Goodbye, my hearties. I'll go to my honest knight Falstaff and drink canary wine with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Host.","id":631},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["[Aside] I think I shall drink in pipe-wine first with him; I'll make","him dance.—Will you go, gentles?"],"id":632,"m":"(Aside) I think I'll make him drink something sharper first; I'll make him dance. Shall we go, gentlemen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Have with you to see this monster."],"id":633,"m":"We're with you, to see this monster."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":634},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"A room in Ford's house.","id":635},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford and Mistress Page.","id":636},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What, John! What, Robert!"],"id":637,"m":"John! Robert!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Quickly, quickly! Is the buck-basket—"],"id":638,"m":"Quick, quick! Is the laundry basket—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I warrant.—What, Robin, I say!"],"id":639,"m":"It's ready, I promise. Robin, come here, I say!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter John and Robert with a great buck-basket.","id":640},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Come, come, come."],"id":641,"m":"Come on, come on, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Here, set it down."],"id":642,"m":"Here, put it down."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Give your men the charge; we must be brief."],"id":643,"m":"Give your men their orders; we must be quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Marry, as I told you before, John and Robert, be ready here hard by in","the brew-house; and when I suddenly call you, come forth, and, without","any pause or staggering, take this basket on your shoulders. That done,","trudge with it in all haste, and carry it among the whitsters in","Datchet Mead, and there empty it in the muddy ditch close by the Thames","side."],"id":644,"m":"Well, as I told you before, John and Robert, wait close by here in the brew-house; and when I suddenly call you, come out and, without stopping or stumbling, lift this basket onto your shoulders. Then hurry off with it as fast as you can, carry it to the women bleaching linen in Datchet Meadow, and empty it into the muddy ditch beside the Thames."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["You will do it?"],"id":645,"m":"You'll do it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I ha' told them over and over, they lack no direction.—Be gone, and","come when you are called."],"id":646,"m":"I've told them over and over; they don't need any more instructions. Go, and come when you're called."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt John and Robert.","id":647},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Here comes little Robin."],"id":648,"m":"Here comes little Robin."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Robin.","id":649},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["How now, my eyas-musket, what news with you?"],"id":650,"m":"Well, my little sparrowhawk, what news do you have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["My Master, Sir John, is come in at your back door, Mistress Ford, and","requests your company."],"id":651,"m":"My master, Sir John, has come in at your back door, Mistress Ford, and asks for your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["You little Jack-a-Lent, have you been true to us?"],"id":652,"m":"You little puppet, have you been loyal to us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["Ay, I'll be sworn. My master knows not of your being here, and hath","threatened to put me into everlasting liberty if I tell you of it; for","he swears he'll turn me away."],"id":653,"m":"Yes, I swear it. My master doesn't know you're here, and he has threatened to give me everlasting freedom if I tell you about it, because he swears he'll sack me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Thou'rt a good boy, this secrecy of thine shall be a tailor to thee,","and shall make thee a new doublet and hose. I'll go hide me."],"id":654,"m":"You're a good boy. This secret of yours will be your tailor and make you a new jacket and breeches. I'll go and hide."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Do so.—Go tell thy master I am alone."],"id":655,"m":"Do. Go and tell your master I'm alone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Robin.","id":656},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","cont":1,"lines":["Mistress Page, remember you your cue."],"id":657,"m":"Mistress Page, remember your cue."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I warrant thee. If I do not act it, hiss me."],"id":658,"m":"I promise you. If I don't play my part, hiss me off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Page.","id":659},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Go to, then. We'll use this unwholesome humidity, this gross watery","pumpion; we'll teach him to know turtles from jays."],"id":660,"m":"Go on, then. We'll deal with this unhealthy bag of moisture, this great watery pumpkin; we'll teach him to tell faithful turtledoves from chattering jays."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":661},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["\"Have I caught thee, my heavenly jewel?\" Why, now let me die, for I","have lived long enough. This is the period of my ambition. O this","blessed hour!"],"id":662,"m":"\"Have I caught you, my heavenly jewel?\" Why, now let me die, for I have lived long enough. This is the summit of my ambition. Oh, this blessed hour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["O, sweet Sir John!"],"id":663,"m":"Oh, sweet Sir John!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mistress Ford, I cannot cog, I cannot prate, Mistress Ford. Now shall I","sin in my wish: I would thy husband were dead. I'll speak it before the","best lord: I would make thee my lady."],"id":664,"m":"Mistress Ford, I can't flatter, I can't chatter, Mistress Ford. Now I'll sin in my wish: I wish your husband were dead. I'd say it in front of the greatest lord: I would make you my lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I your lady, Sir John? Alas, I should be a pitiful lady."],"id":665,"m":"Me, your lady, Sir John? Oh dear, I'd be a pitiful lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let the court of France show me such another. I see how thine eye would","emulate the diamond. Thou hast the right arched beauty of the brow that","becomes the ship-tire, the tire-valiant, or any tire of Venetian","admittance."],"id":666,"m":"Let the French court show me another like you. I can see how your eyes would rival a diamond. You have just the right arched beauty of the forehead to suit a ship-shaped headdress, a fancy headdress, or any headdress in the Venetian fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["A plain kerchief, Sir John. My brows become nothing else, nor that well","neither."],"id":667,"m":"A plain headscarf, Sir John. Nothing else suits my forehead, and that doesn't suit it very well either."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["By the Lord, thou art a traitor to say so. Thou wouldst make an","absolute courtier, and the firm fixture of thy foot would give an","excellent motion to thy gait in a semi-circled farthingale. I see what","thou wert, if Fortune thy foe were not, Nature thy friend. Come, thou","canst not hide it."],"id":668,"m":"By the Lord, you are a traitor to say so. You'd make a perfect lady of the court, and the firm way you set your foot would give your walk an excellent motion in a hooped skirt. I can see what you would be, if Fortune were not your enemy when Nature is your friend. Come, you can't hide it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Believe me, there's no such thing in me."],"id":669,"m":"Believe me, there's nothing like that in me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What made me love thee? Let that persuade thee there's something","extraordinary in thee. Come, I cannot cog and say thou art this and","that, like a many of these lisping hawthorn buds that come like women","in men's apparel, and smell like Bucklersbury in simple-time. I cannot.","But I love thee, none but thee; and thou deservest it."],"id":670,"m":"What made me love you? Let that convince you there's something extraordinary in you. Come, I can't flatter and say you are this and that, like so many of these lisping young dandies who come dressed up like women in men's clothes and smell like a herb shop in summer. I can't. But I love you, no one but you, and you deserve it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Do not betray me, sir; I fear you love Mistress Page."],"id":671,"m":"Don't deceive me, sir; I'm afraid you love Mistress Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thou mightst as well say I love to walk by the Counter gate, which is","as hateful to me as the reek of a lime-kiln."],"id":672,"m":"You might as well say I love walking past the debtors' prison gate, which I hate as much as the stink of a lime-kiln."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Well, heaven knows how I love you, and you shall one day find it."],"id":673,"m":"Well, heaven knows how I love you, and one day you will find out."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Keep in that mind, I'll deserve it."],"id":674,"m":"Keep thinking that way; I'll deserve it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, I must tell you, so you do; or else I could not be in that mind."],"id":675,"m":"Well, I must tell you, you do deserve it, or I couldn't think that way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Robin.","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBIN","lines":["Mistress Ford, Mistress Ford, here's Mistress Page at the door,","sweating and blowing and looking wildly, and would needs speak with you","presently."],"id":677,"m":"Mistress Ford, Mistress Ford, Mistress Page is at the door, sweating and panting and looking wild, and she must speak to you at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["She shall not see me; I will ensconce me behind the arras."],"id":678,"m":"She mustn't see me; I'll hide behind the tapestry."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Pray you, do so; she's a very tattling woman."],"id":679,"m":"Please do; she's a terrible gossip."},{"t":"sd","text":"Falstaff hides himself behind the arras.","id":680},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page.","id":681},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","cont":1,"lines":["What's the matter? How now?"],"id":682,"m":"What's the matter? What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["O Mistress Ford, what have you done? You're shamed, you're overthrown,","you're undone for ever!"],"id":683,"m":"Oh, Mistress Ford, what have you done? You're disgraced, you're ruined, you're finished for ever!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What's the matter, good Mistress Page?"],"id":684,"m":"What's the matter, good Mistress Page?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["O well-a-day, Mistress Ford, having an honest man to your husband, to","give him such cause of suspicion!"],"id":685,"m":"Oh, alas, Mistress Ford, when you have an honest man for a husband, to give him such cause for suspicion!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What cause of suspicion?"],"id":686,"m":"What cause for suspicion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What cause of suspicion? Out upon you! How am I mistook in you!"],"id":687,"m":"What cause for suspicion? Shame on you! How wrong I was about you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, alas, what's the matter?"],"id":688,"m":"Why, oh dear, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Your husband's coming hither, woman, with all the officers in Windsor,","to search for a gentleman that he says is here now in the house, by","your consent, to take an ill advantage of his absence. You are undone."],"id":689,"m":"Your husband is coming here, woman, with all the officers in Windsor, to search for a gentleman who he says is in the house right now with your consent, to take wicked advantage of his absence. You're ruined."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["'Tis not so, I hope."],"id":690,"m":"It's not true, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Pray heaven it be not so, that you have such a man here! But 'tis most","certain your husband's coming, with half Windsor at his heels, to","search for such a one. I come before to tell you. If you know yourself","clear, why, I am glad of it; but if you have a friend here, convey,","convey him out. Be not amazed, call all your senses to you; defend your","reputation, or bid farewell to your good life for ever."],"id":691,"m":"Pray heaven it isn't true that you have such a man here! But it's absolutely certain your husband is coming, with half of Windsor at his heels, to search for such a man. I came ahead to tell you. If you know you're innocent, why, I'm glad; but if you have a friend here, get him out, get him out. Don't panic; gather your wits; defend your reputation, or say goodbye to your good life for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What shall I do? There is a gentleman, my dear friend; and I fear not","mine own shame as much as his peril. I had rather than a thousand pound","he were out of the house."],"id":692,"m":"What shall I do? There is a gentleman here, my dear friend; and I'm not as afraid of my own shame as of his danger. I'd give a thousand pounds to have him out of the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["For shame! Never stand \"you had rather\" and \"you had rather\". Your","husband's here at hand. Bethink you of some conveyance. In the house","you cannot hide him. O, how have you deceived me! Look, here is a","basket. If he be of any reasonable stature, he may creep in here; and","throw foul linen upon him, as if it were going to bucking. Or—it is","whiting-time—send him by your two men to Datchet Mead."],"id":693,"m":"For shame! Don't stand there saying \"I'd give\" and \"I'd give\". Your husband is close by. Think of some way to get him out. You can't hide him in the house. Oh, how you have deceived me! Look, here is a basket. If he's of any reasonable size, he can creep in here; then throw dirty linen over him, as if it were going to the wash. Or, since it's bleaching time, send him with your two men to Datchet Meadow."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["He's too big to go in there. What shall I do?"],"id":694,"m":"He's too big to get in there. What shall I do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[Comes out of hiding] Let me see 't, let me see 't! O, let me see","'t! I'll in, I'll in. Follow your friend's counsel. I'll in."],"id":695,"m":"(Coming out of hiding) Let me see it, let me see it! Oh, let me see it! I'll get in, I'll get in. Take your friend's advice. I'll get in."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What, Sir John Falstaff? Are these your letters, knight?"],"id":696,"m":"What, Sir John Falstaff? Are these your letters, knight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I love thee, and none but thee. Help me away. Let me creep in here.","I'll never—"],"id":697,"m":"I love you, and no one but you. Help me get away. Let me creep in here. I'll never—"},{"t":"sd","text":"He goes into the basket; they cover him with dirty clothes.","id":698},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Help to cover your master, boy.—Call your men, Mistress Ford.—You","dissembling knight!"],"id":699,"m":"Help cover your master, boy. Call your men, Mistress Ford. You lying knight!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Robin.","id":700},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What, John! Robert! John!"],"id":701,"m":"John! Robert! John!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter John and Robert.","id":702},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","cont":1,"lines":["Go, take up these clothes here, quickly. Where's the cowl-staff? Look","how you drumble! Carry them to the laundress in Datchet Mead; quickly,","come."],"id":703,"m":"Go on, pick up these clothes here, quickly. Where's the carrying pole? Look how you dawdle! Take them to the laundress in Datchet Meadow; quickly, come on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford, Page, Caius and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":704},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Pray you come near. If I suspect without cause, why then make sport at","me, then let me be your jest; I deserve it.—How now? Whither bear you","this?"],"id":705,"m":"Please come in. If I suspect without cause, then laugh at me, make me your joke; I'll deserve it. What's this? Where are you taking this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN AND ROBERT","lines":["To the laundress, forsooth."],"id":706,"m":"To the laundress, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, what have you to do whither they bear it? You were best meddle","with buck-washing!"],"id":707,"m":"Why, what business is it of yours where they take it? You'd better leave the washing to us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Buck? I would I could wash myself of the buck! Buck, buck, buck! Ay,","buck! I warrant you, buck, and of the season too, it shall appear."],"id":708,"m":"Buck-washing? I wish I could wash myself clean of the buck! Buck, buck, buck! Yes, buck! I promise you, a horned buck, and in the rutting season too, as you'll see."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt John and Robert with the basket.","id":709},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","cont":1,"lines":["Gentlemen, I have dreamed tonight; I'll tell you my dream. Here, here,","here be my keys. Ascend my chambers, search, seek, find out. I'll","warrant we'll unkennel the fox. Let me stop this way first. [Locks the","door.] So, now uncape."],"id":710,"m":"Gentlemen, I had a dream last night; I'll tell you my dream. Here, here, here are my keys. Go up to my rooms, search, look, find him. I bet we'll drive the fox out of his hole. Let me block this way first. (Locks the door) There, now start the hunt."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Good Master Ford, be contented: you wrong yourself too much."],"id":711,"m":"Good Master Ford, calm down; you do yourself too much wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["True, Master Page.—Up, gentlemen, you shall see sport anon. Follow me,","gentlemen."],"id":712,"m":"True, Master Page. Upstairs, gentlemen; you'll see some sport soon. Follow me, gentlemen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Ford.","id":713},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["This is fery fantastical humours and jealousies."],"id":714,"m":"These are very fantastic moods and jealousies."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, 'tis no the fashion of France; it is not jealous in France."],"id":715,"m":"By God, it is not the fashion in France; nobody is jealous in France."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Nay, follow him, gentlemen; see the issue of his search."],"id":716,"m":"Well, follow him, gentlemen; let's see how his search turns out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Page, Evans and Caius.","id":717},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Is there not a double excellency in this?"],"id":718,"m":"Isn't this doubly excellent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I know not which pleases me better, that my husband is deceived, or Sir","John."],"id":719,"m":"I don't know which pleases me more, that my husband is fooled, or Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["What a taking was he in when your husband asked who was in the basket!"],"id":720,"m":"What a state he was in when your husband asked what was in the basket!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I am half afraid he will have need of washing, so throwing him into the","water will do him a benefit."],"id":721,"m":"I'm half afraid he'll need a wash, so throwing him in the water will do him good."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Hang him, dishonest rascal! I would all of the same strain were in the","same distress."],"id":722,"m":"Hang him, the lecherous rascal! I wish everyone of his kind were in the same trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I think my husband hath some special suspicion of Falstaff's being","here, for I never saw him so gross in his jealousy till now."],"id":723,"m":"I think my husband has some particular suspicion that Falstaff is here, because I've never seen him so wildly jealous until now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I will lay a plot to try that, and we will yet have more tricks with","Falstaff. His dissolute disease will scarce obey this medicine."],"id":724,"m":"I'll lay a plan to test that, and we'll play more tricks on Falstaff yet. His lustful disease will hardly be cured by this one dose."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Shall we send that foolish carrion Mistress Quickly to him, and excuse","his throwing into the water, and give him another hope, to betray him","to another punishment?"],"id":725,"m":"Shall we send that foolish old bag Mistress Quickly to him, to apologise for his being thrown into the water and give him new hope, so we can lure him into another punishment?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["We will do it. Let him be sent for tomorrow eight o'clock to have","amends."],"id":726,"m":"We'll do it. Let him be invited tomorrow at eight o'clock, to make up for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford, Page, Caius and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":727},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I cannot find him. Maybe the knave bragged of that he could not","compass."],"id":728,"m":"I can't find him. Perhaps the rogue boasted of something he couldn't manage."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["[Aside to Mistress Ford] Heard you that?"],"id":729,"m":"(Aside to Mistress Ford) Did you hear that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["You use me well, Master Ford, do you?"],"id":730,"m":"You treat me well, Master Ford, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Ay, I do so."],"id":731,"m":"Yes, I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Heaven make you better than your thoughts!"],"id":732,"m":"Heaven make you better than your thoughts!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Amen!"],"id":733,"m":"Amen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["You do yourself mighty wrong, Master Ford."],"id":734,"m":"You do yourself great wrong, Master Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Ay, ay; I must bear it."],"id":735,"m":"Yes, yes; I must put up with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["If there be anypody in the house, and in the chambers, and in the","coffers, and in the presses, heaven forgive my sins at the day of","judgment!"],"id":736,"m":"If there's anybody in the house, in the bedrooms, in the chests or in the cupboards, may heaven forgive my sins on Judgement Day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Be gar, nor I too; there is nobodies."],"id":737,"m":"By God, nor me either; there is nobody."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Fie, fie, Master Ford, are you not ashamed? What spirit, what devil","suggests this imagination? I would not ha' your distemper in this kind","for the wealth of Windsor Castle."],"id":738,"m":"Shame, shame, Master Ford, aren't you ashamed? What spirit, what devil puts this idea in your head? I wouldn't have your sickness of this kind for all the wealth of Windsor Castle."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["'Tis my fault, Master Page. I suffer for it."],"id":739,"m":"It's my fault, Master Page. I suffer for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["You suffer for a pad conscience. Your wife is as honest a 'omans as I","will desires among five thousand, and five hundred too."],"id":740,"m":"You suffer for a bad conscience. Your wife is as faithful a woman as I could wish for among five thousand, and five hundred more."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["By gar, I see 'tis an honest woman."],"id":741,"m":"By God, I see she is an honest woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well, I promised you a dinner. Come, come, walk in the park. I pray you","pardon me; I will hereafter make known to you why I have done this.","Come, wife, come, Mistress Page, I pray you pardon me. Pray heartily,","pardon me."],"id":742,"m":"Well, I promised you a dinner. Come, come, let's walk in the park. Please forgive me; later I'll tell you why I have done this. Come, wife; come, Mistress Page; please forgive me. Please, with all my heart, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Let's go in, gentlemen; but, trust me, we'll mock him. I do invite you","tomorrow morning to my house to breakfast; after, we'll a-birding","together; I have a fine hawk for the bush. Shall it be so?"],"id":743,"m":"Let's go in, gentlemen; but, believe me, we'll make fun of him. I invite you to my house for breakfast tomorrow morning; afterwards we'll go bird hunting together; I have a fine hawk for hunting in the bushes. Shall we?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Anything."],"id":744,"m":"Anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["If there is one, I shall make two in the company."],"id":745,"m":"If there is one, I will make two in the party."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["If there be one or two, I shall make-a the turd."],"id":746,"m":"If there are one or two, I will make the third."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Pray you go, Master Page."],"id":747,"m":"Please go on, Master Page."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Evans and Caius.","id":748},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["I pray you now, remembrance tomorrow on the lousy knave, mine host."],"id":749,"m":"Please, now, remember tomorrow about that lousy rogue, our landlord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Dat is good, by gar, with all my heart."],"id":750,"m":"That is good, by God, with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["A lousy knave, to have his gibes and his mockeries!"],"id":751,"m":"A lousy rogue, with his jokes and his mockery!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":752},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"A room in Page's house.","id":753},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fenton and Anne Page.","id":754},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["I see I cannot get thy father's love;","Therefore no more turn me to him, sweet Nan."],"id":755,"m":"I see I can't win your father's love, so don't send me to him any more, sweet Nan."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Alas, how then?"],"id":756,"m":"Oh dear, what then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Why, thou must be thyself.","He doth object I am too great of birth,","And that my state being galled with my expense,","I seek to heal it only by his wealth.","Besides these, other bars he lays before me:","My riots past, my wild societies—","And tells me 'tis a thing impossible","I should love thee but as a property."],"id":757,"m":"Why, you must be yourself and choose. He objects that I am of too high a birth, and that with my estate damaged by my spending, I am only trying to mend it with his money. Besides these, he puts other obstacles in my way: my wild past, my rowdy companions; and he tells me it is impossible that I could love you except as property."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Maybe he tells you true."],"id":758,"m":"Perhaps he's telling you the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["No, heaven so speed me in my time to come!","Albeit I will confess thy father's wealth","Was the first motive that I wooed thee, Anne,","Yet, wooing thee, I found thee of more value","Than stamps in gold or sums in sealed bags.","And 'tis the very riches of thyself","That now I aim at."],"id":759,"m":"No, so help me heaven in the time to come! Though I admit your father's wealth was the first reason I wooed you, Anne, yet as I wooed you I found you worth more than stamped gold or sums in sealed bags. And it is the true riches of yourself that I am after now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Gentle Master Fenton,","Yet seek my father's love, still seek it, sir.","If opportunity and humblest suit","Cannot attain it, why then—hark you hither."],"id":760,"m":"Gentle Master Fenton, still try to win my father's love; keep trying, sir. If opportunity and the humblest pleading can't win it, why then... listen here."},{"t":"sd","text":"They talk apart.","id":761},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shallow, Slender and Mistress Quickly.","id":762},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Break their talk, Mistress Quickly. My kinsman shall speak for himself."],"id":763,"m":"Break up their talk, Mistress Quickly. My kinsman will speak for himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I'll make a shaft or a bolt on 't. 'Slid, 'tis but venturing."],"id":764,"m":"I'll have a try one way or another. By God's eyelid, it's only a risk."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Be not dismayed."],"id":765,"m":"Don't lose heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["No, she shall not dismay me. I care not for that, but that I am afeard."],"id":766,"m":"No, she won't make me lose heart. I don't care about that, except that I'm scared."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Hark ye, Master Slender would speak a word with you."],"id":767,"m":"Listen, Master Slender would like a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I come to him.","[Aside] This is my father's choice.","O, what a world of vile ill-favoured faults","Looks handsome in three hundred pounds a year!"],"id":768,"m":"I'm coming to him. (Aside) This is my father's choice. Oh, what a world of vile, ugly faults looks handsome with three hundred pounds a year!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["And how does good Master Fenton? Pray you, a word with you."],"id":769,"m":"And how is good Master Fenton? Please, a word with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"They talk aside.","id":770},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["[To Slender] She's coming; to her, coz. O boy, thou hadst a father!"],"id":771,"m":"(To Slender) She's coming; go to her, cousin. Oh boy, remember you had a father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I had a father, Mistress Anne; my uncle can tell you good jests of","him.—Pray you, uncle, tell Mistress Anne the jest how my father stole","two geese out of a pen, good uncle."],"id":772,"m":"I had a father, Mistress Anne; my uncle can tell you good jokes about him. Please, uncle, tell Mistress Anne the joke about how my father stole two geese out of a pen, good uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Mistress Anne, my cousin loves you."],"id":773,"m":"Mistress Anne, my cousin loves you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, that I do, as well as I love any woman in Gloucestershire."],"id":774,"m":"Yes, I do, as much as I love any woman in Gloucestershire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He will maintain you like a gentlewoman."],"id":775,"m":"He will keep you like a gentlewoman."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, that I will, come cut and long-tail, under the degree of a squire."],"id":776,"m":"Yes, I will, come what may, as well as anyone below the rank of squire."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He will make you a hundred and fifty pounds jointure."],"id":777,"m":"He will settle a hundred and fifty pounds on you as your widow's share."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Good Master Shallow, let him woo for himself."],"id":778,"m":"Good Master Shallow, let him woo for himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Marry, I thank you for it, I thank you for that good comfort.—She calls","you, coz; I'll leave you."],"id":779,"m":"Well, thank you for that, thank you for that encouragement. She's calling you, cousin; I'll leave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Now, Master Slender."],"id":780,"m":"Now, Master Slender."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Now, good Mistress Anne."],"id":781,"m":"Now, good Mistress Anne."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["What is your will?"],"id":782,"m":"What is your will?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["My will? 'Od's heartlings, that's a pretty jest indeed! I ne'er made my","will yet, I thank heaven. I am not such a sickly creature, I give","heaven praise."],"id":783,"m":"My will? By God's little heart, that's a good joke indeed! I haven't made my will yet, thank heaven. I'm not such a sickly creature, heaven be praised."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["I mean, Master Slender, what would you with me?"],"id":784,"m":"I mean, Master Slender, what do you want with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Truly, for mine own part I would little or nothing with you. Your","father and my uncle hath made motions. If it be my luck, so; if not,","happy man be his dole. They can tell you how things go better than I","can. You may ask your father. Here he comes."],"id":785,"m":"Honestly, for my own part I want little or nothing with you. Your father and my uncle have made proposals. If I'm lucky, fine; if not, good luck to the winner. They can tell you how things stand better than I can. You can ask your father. Here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page and Mistress Page.","id":786},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Now, Master Slender.—Love him, daughter Anne.—","Why, how now? What does Master Fenton here?","You wrong me, sir, thus still to haunt my house.","I told you, sir, my daughter is disposed of."],"id":787,"m":"Now then, Master Slender. Love him, daughter Anne. Why, what's this? What is Master Fenton doing here? You wrong me, sir, by haunting my house like this. I told you, sir, my daughter is spoken for."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Nay, Master Page, be not impatient."],"id":788,"m":"Now, Master Page, don't be impatient."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Good Master Fenton, come not to my child."],"id":789,"m":"Good Master Fenton, don't come after my child."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["She is no match for you."],"id":790,"m":"She's not a match for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Sir, will you hear me?"],"id":791,"m":"Sir, will you hear me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["No, good Master Fenton.—","Come, Master Shallow; come, son Slender, in.—","Knowing my mind, you wrong me, Master Fenton."],"id":792,"m":"No, good Master Fenton. Come, Master Shallow; come in, son Slender. You know my mind, so you wrong me, Master Fenton."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Page, Shallow and Slender.","id":793},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Speak to Mistress Page."],"id":794,"m":"Speak to Mistress Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Good Mistress Page, for that I love your daughter","In such a righteous fashion as I do,","Perforce, against all checks, rebukes, and manners,","I must advance the colours of my love","And not retire. Let me have your good will."],"id":795,"m":"Good Mistress Page, because I love your daughter as honourably as I do, I am forced, in spite of all setbacks, rebukes and good manners, to march forward under the flag of my love and not retreat. Let me have your goodwill."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Good mother, do not marry me to yond fool."],"id":796,"m":"Good mother, don't marry me to that fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I mean it not; I seek you a better husband."],"id":797,"m":"I don't mean to; I'm looking for a better husband for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["That's my master, Master Doctor."],"id":798,"m":"That's my master, the Doctor."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Alas, I had rather be set quick i' th' earth,","And bowled to death with turnips."],"id":799,"m":"Oh no, I'd rather be buried alive and pelted to death with turnips."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Come, trouble not yourself, good Master Fenton,","I will not be your friend, nor enemy.","My daughter will I question how she loves you,","And as I find her, so am I affected.","Till then, farewell, sir. She must needs go in;","Her father will be angry."],"id":800,"m":"Come, don't trouble yourself, good Master Fenton. I will be neither your friend nor your enemy. I'll ask my daughter how much she loves you, and my feelings will follow what I find. Until then, goodbye, sir. She must go in; her father will be angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Farewell, gentle mistress. Farewell, Nan."],"id":801,"m":"Goodbye, gentle lady. Goodbye, Nan."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Mistress Page and Anne.","id":802},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["This is my doing now. \"Nay,\" said I, \"will you cast away your child on","a fool, and a physician? Look on Master Fenton.\" This is my doing."],"id":803,"m":"This is my doing now. \"No,\" I said, \"will you throw your child away on a fool, or a physician? Look at Master Fenton.\" This is my doing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["I thank thee; and I pray thee, once tonight","Give my sweet Nan this ring. There's for thy pains."],"id":804,"m":"Thank you; and please, some time tonight, give my sweet Nan this ring. That's for your trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Now Heaven send thee good fortune!"],"id":805,"m":"Now heaven send you good luck!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Fenton.","id":806},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","cont":1,"lines":["A kind heart he hath. A woman would run through fire and water for such","a kind heart. But yet I would my master had Mistress Anne, or I would","Master Slender had her; or, in sooth, I would Master Fenton had her. I","will do what I can for them all three, for so I have promised and I'll","be as good as my word—but speciously for Master Fenton. Well, I must of","another errand to Sir John Falstaff from my two mistresses. What a","beast am I to slack it!"],"id":807,"m":"He has a kind heart. A woman would go through fire and water for such a kind heart. But still, I wish my master had Mistress Anne; or I wish Master Slender had her; or, honestly, I wish Master Fenton had her. I'll do what I can for all three of them, because that's what I promised, and I'll be as good as my word, but especially for Master Fenton. Well, I must go on another errand to Sir John Falstaff from my two ladies. What a beast I am to be so slow about it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":808},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":809},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":810},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Bardolph, I say!"],"id":811,"m":"Bardolph, I say!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph.","id":812},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":813,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Go fetch me a quart of sack; put a toast in 't."],"id":814,"m":"Go and get me a quart of sack; put some toast in it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":815},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Have I lived to be carried in a basket like a barrow of butcher's","offal, and to be thrown in the Thames? Well, if I be served such","another trick, I'll have my brains ta'en out and buttered, and give","them to a dog for a New Year's gift. 'Sblood, the rogues slighted me","into the river with as little remorse as they would have drowned a","blind bitch's puppies, fifteen i' the litter; and you may know by my","size that I have a kind of alacrity in sinking; if the bottom were as","deep as hell, I should down. I had been drowned, but that the shore was","shelvy and shallow—a death that I abhor, for the water swells a man,","and what a thing should I have been when I had been swelled! I should","have been a mountain of mummy."],"id":816,"m":"Have I lived to be carried in a basket like a barrowful of butcher's scraps and thrown in the Thames? Well, if I'm served another trick like that, I'll have my brains taken out and buttered and give them to a dog as a New Year's present. By God's blood, the rogues tipped me into the river with as little pity as if they were drowning a blind bitch's puppies, fifteen in the litter; and you can tell by my size that I have a sort of talent for sinking. If the bottom were as deep as hell, I'd go down. I would have drowned if the shore hadn't been sloping and shallow, a death I loathe, because water makes a man swell, and what a sight I would have been swollen! I'd have been a mountain of mummified flesh."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph with sack.","id":817},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Here's Mistress Quickly, sir, to speak with you."],"id":818,"m":"Mistress Quickly is here, sir, to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, let me pour in some sack to the Thames water, for my belly's as","cold as if I had swallowed snowballs for pills to cool the reins. Call","her in."],"id":819,"m":"Come, let me pour some sack in on top of the Thames water, because my belly is as cold as if I'd swallowed snowballs as pills to cool my lust. Send her in."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Come in, woman."],"id":820,"m":"Come in, woman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly.","id":821},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["By your leave, I cry you mercy. Give your worship good morrow."],"id":822,"m":"Excuse me, I beg your pardon. Good morning to your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Take away these chalices. Go, brew me a pottle of sack finely."],"id":823,"m":"Take away these cups. Go and make me a half-gallon of hot sack, nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["With eggs, sir?"],"id":824,"m":"With eggs, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Simple of itself. I'll no pullet sperm in my brewage."],"id":825,"m":"Plain, on its own. I'll have no chicken sperm in my drink."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":826},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now?"],"id":827,"m":"Well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Marry, sir, I come to your worship from Mistress Ford."],"id":828,"m":"Well, sir, I come to your worship from Mistress Ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mistress Ford? I have had ford enough. I was thrown into the ford, I","have my belly full of ford."],"id":829,"m":"Mistress Ford? I've had enough of fords. I was thrown into the ford; I have a bellyful of ford."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Alas the day, good heart, that was not her fault. She does so take on","with her men; they mistook their erection."],"id":830,"m":"Oh dear, good heart, that wasn't her fault. She is so angry with her men; they misunderstood their erection, I mean direction."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["So did I mine, to build upon a foolish woman's promise."],"id":831,"m":"So did I mine, building on a foolish woman's promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Well, she laments, sir, for it, that it would yearn your heart to see","it. Her husband goes this morning a-birding; she desires you once more","to come to her, between eight and nine. I must carry her word quickly.","She'll make you amends, I warrant you."],"id":832,"m":"Well, she's so sorry for it, sir, it would melt your heart to see it. Her husband goes bird hunting this morning; she begs you to come to her once more, between eight and nine. I must take her your answer quickly. She'll make it up to you, I promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, I will visit her. Tell her so, and bid her think what a man is.","Let her consider his frailty, and then judge of my merit."],"id":833,"m":"Well, I'll visit her. Tell her so, and tell her to think what a man is. Let her consider his weakness, and then judge how much I deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["I will tell her."],"id":834,"m":"I'll tell her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Do so. Between nine and ten, sayst thou?"],"id":835,"m":"Do. Between nine and ten, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Eight and nine, sir."],"id":836,"m":"Eight and nine, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, be gone. I will not miss her."],"id":837,"m":"Well, go. I won't miss her."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Peace be with you, sir."],"id":838,"m":"Peace be with you, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Quickly.","id":839},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I marvel I hear not of Master Brook; he sent me word to stay within. I","like his money well. O, here he comes."],"id":840,"m":"I'm surprised I haven't heard from Master Brook; he sent word that I should stay in. I like his money a lot. Oh, here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford disguised.","id":841},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["God bless you, sir."],"id":842,"m":"God bless you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now, Master Brook, you come to know what hath passed between me and","Ford's wife?"],"id":843,"m":"Well, Master Brook, you've come to find out what has happened between me and Ford's wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["That indeed, Sir John, is my business."],"id":844,"m":"That is indeed my business, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Brook, I will not lie to you. I was at her house the hour she","appointed me."],"id":845,"m":"Master Brook, I won't lie to you. I was at her house at the hour she gave me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["And how sped you, sir?"],"id":846,"m":"And how did you get on, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Very ill-favouredly, Master Brook."],"id":847,"m":"Very badly, Master Brook."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["How so, sir? Did she change her determination?"],"id":848,"m":"How was that, sir? Did she change her mind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No. Master Brook, but the peaking cornuto her husband, Master Brook,","dwelling in a continual 'larum of jealousy, comes me in the instant of","our encounter, after we had embraced, kissed, protested, and, as it","were, spoke the prologue of our comedy; and at his heels a rabble of","his companions, thither provoked and instigated by his distemper, and,","forsooth, to search his house for his wife's love."],"id":849,"m":"No, Master Brook; but her sneaking cuckold of a husband, Master Brook, who lives in a constant alarm of jealousy, came in at the very moment of our meeting, after we had embraced, kissed, made our vows and, so to speak, spoken the prologue of our comedy; and at his heels came a rabble of his friends, stirred up and brought there by his madness, to search his house, if you please, for his wife's lover."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["What, while you were there?"],"id":850,"m":"What, while you were there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["While I was there."],"id":851,"m":"While I was there."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["And did he search for you, and could not find you?"],"id":852,"m":"And he searched for you and couldn't find you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You shall hear. As good luck would have it, comes in one Mistress Page,","gives intelligence of Ford's approach; and, in her invention and Ford's","wife's distraction, they conveyed me into a buck-basket."],"id":853,"m":"You'll hear. As luck would have it, in comes a Mistress Page, who brings news that Ford is coming; and through her quick thinking and Ford's wife's panic, they smuggled me into a laundry basket."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["A buck-basket!"],"id":854,"m":"A laundry basket!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["By the Lord, a buck-basket! Rammed me in with foul shirts and smocks,","socks, foul stockings, greasy napkins, that, Master Brook, there was","the rankest compound of villainous smell that ever offended nostril."],"id":855,"m":"By the Lord, a laundry basket! They rammed me in with dirty shirts and petticoats, socks, dirty stockings and greasy napkins, so that, Master Brook, there was the foulest mixture of vile smells that ever offended a nostril."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["And how long lay you there?"],"id":856,"m":"And how long did you lie there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Nay, you shall hear, Master Brook, what I have suffered to bring this","woman to evil for your good. Being thus crammed in the basket, a couple","of Ford's knaves, his hinds, were called forth by their mistress to","carry me in the name of foul clothes to Datchet Lane. They took me on","their shoulders, met the jealous knave their master in the door, who","asked them once or twice what they had in their basket. I quaked for","fear lest the lunatic knave would have searched it; but Fate, ordaining","he should be a cuckold, held his hand. Well, on went he for a search,","and away went I for foul clothes. But mark the sequel, Master Brook. I","suffered the pangs of three several deaths: first, an intolerable","fright to be detected with a jealous rotten bell-wether; next, to be","compassed like a good bilbo in the circumference of a peck, hilt to","point, heel to head; and then, to be stopped in, like a strong","distillation, with stinking clothes that fretted in their own grease.","Think of that, a man of my kidney, think of that—that am as subject to","heat as butter; a man of continual dissolution and thaw. It was a","miracle to 'scape suffocation. And in the height of this bath, when I","was more than half stewed in grease, like a Dutch dish, to be thrown","into the Thames and cooled, glowing hot, in that surge, like a","horseshoe! Think of that—hissing hot—think of that, Master Brook."],"id":857,"m":"Now, you'll hear, Master Brook, what I have suffered trying to lead this woman astray for your benefit. Crammed into the basket like that, I was carried by a couple of Ford's servants, his farmhands, whom their mistress called to take me as dirty clothes to Datchet Lane. They lifted me onto their shoulders and met their jealous rogue of a master at the door, who asked them once or twice what they had in their basket. I shook with fear that the lunatic would search it; but Fate, having decided he would be a cuckold, held back his hand. Well, off he went to search, and off I went as dirty clothes. But listen to what followed, Master Brook. I suffered the agonies of three separate deaths: first, the unbearable fright of being caught by a jealous, rotten old ram; next, being bent like a good sword into the round of a bucket, hilt to point, heel to head; and then being sealed in, like a strong brew in a still, with stinking clothes fermenting in their own grease. Think of that, a man of my build, think of that, who melts in the heat like butter, a man always dissolving and thawing. It was a miracle I didn't suffocate. And at the height of this bath, when I was more than half stewed in grease like a Dutch dish, to be thrown into the Thames and cooled, glowing hot, in the waves, like a horseshoe! Think of that, hissing hot; think of that, Master Brook."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["In good sadness, sir, I am sorry that for my sake you have suffered all","this. My suit, then, is desperate. You'll undertake her no more?"],"id":858,"m":"In all seriousness, sir, I'm sorry you've suffered all this for my sake. My suit is hopeless, then. You won't try her any more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Brook, I will be thrown into Etna, as I have been into Thames,","ere I will leave her thus. Her husband is this morning gone a-birding;","I have received from her another embassy of meeting. 'Twixt eight and","nine is the hour, Master Brook."],"id":859,"m":"Master Brook, I'll let myself be thrown into Mount Etna, as I was into the Thames, before I give her up like this. Her husband has gone bird hunting this morning; I have had another message from her arranging a meeting. Between eight and nine is the time, Master Brook."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["'Tis past eight already, sir."],"id":860,"m":"It's already past eight, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Is it? I will then address me to my appointment. Come to me at your","convenient leisure, and you shall know how I speed; and the conclusion","shall be crowned with your enjoying her. Adieu. You shall have her,","Master Brook. Master Brook, you shall cuckold Ford."],"id":861,"m":"Is it? Then I'll be off to my appointment. Come to me when it suits you, and you'll hear how I get on; and the end of it will be crowned by your enjoying her. Goodbye. You'll have her, Master Brook. Master Brook, you'll make a cuckold of Ford."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":862},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Hum! Ha! Is this a vision? Is this a dream? Do I sleep? Master Ford,","awake; awake, Master Ford! There's a hole made in your best coat,","Master Ford. This 'tis to be married; this 'tis to have linen and","buck-baskets! Well, I will proclaim myself what I am. I will now take","the lecher. He is at my house. He cannot scape me. 'Tis impossible he","should. He cannot creep into a half-penny purse, nor into a pepperbox.","But, lest the devil that guides him should aid him, I will search","impossible places. Though what I am I cannot avoid, yet to be what I","would not shall not make me tame. If I have horns to make one mad, let","the proverb go with me: I'll be horn-mad."],"id":863,"m":"Hmm! Ha! Is this a vision? Is this a dream? Am I asleep? Master Ford, wake up; wake up, Master Ford! There's a hole in your best coat, Master Ford. This is what it is to be married; this is what it is to have linen and laundry baskets! Well, I'll announce myself for what I am. Now I'll catch the lecher. He's at my house. He can't escape me. It's impossible. He can't creep into a halfpenny purse, or into a pepper pot. But in case the devil who guides him helps him, I'll search impossible places. Though I can't avoid being what I am, being what I don't want to be won't make me meek. If I have horns to drive a man mad, let the proverb fit me: I'll be horn-mad."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":864},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Windsor. A street.","id":865},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page, Mistress Quickly and William.","id":866},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Is he at Master Ford's already, think'st thou?"],"id":867,"m":"Do you think he's at Master Ford's already?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Sure he is by this; or will be presently. But truly he is very","courageous mad about his throwing into the water. Mistress Ford desires","you to come suddenly."],"id":868,"m":"He's sure to be by now, or will be soon. But honestly, he's furious about being thrown into the water. Mistress Ford wants you to come at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I'll be with her by and by. I'll but bring my young man here to school.","Look where his master comes; 'tis a playing day, I see."],"id":869,"m":"I'll be with her soon. I'm just taking my young man here to school. Look, here comes his teacher; it's a holiday, I see."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Hugh Evans.","id":870},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Sir Hugh, no school today?"],"id":871,"m":"Hello, Sir Hugh, no school today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["No, Master Slender is let the boys leave to play."],"id":872,"m":"No, Master Slender has got the boys a holiday to play."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Blessing of his heart!"],"id":873,"m":"Bless his heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Sir Hugh, my husband says my son profits nothing in the world at his","book. I pray you ask him some questions in his accidence."],"id":874,"m":"Sir Hugh, my husband says my son is learning nothing at all from his books. Please ask him some questions from his Latin grammar."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Come hither, William. Hold up your head, come."],"id":875,"m":"Come here, William. Hold your head up; come."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Come on, sirrah. Hold up your head. Answer your master, be not afraid."],"id":876,"m":"Come on, boy. Hold your head up. Answer your teacher; don't be afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["William, how many numbers is in nouns?"],"id":877,"m":"William, how many numbers are there in nouns?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Two."],"id":878,"m":"Two."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Truly, I thought there had been one number more, because they say","\"'Od's nouns.\""],"id":879,"m":"Honestly, I thought there was one number more, because people say \"God's nouns\", I mean wounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Peace your tattlings! What is \"fair,\" William?"],"id":880,"m":"Stop your chattering! What is the Latin for \"fair\", William?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Pulcher."],"id":881,"m":"Pulcher."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Polecats? There are fairer things than polecats, sure."],"id":882,"m":"Polecats? There are fairer things than polecats, surely."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["You are a very simplicity 'oman; I pray you, peace.—What is lapis,","William?"],"id":883,"m":"You are a very simple woman; please be quiet. What is lapis, William?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["A stone."],"id":884,"m":"A stone."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["And what is \"a stone,\" William?"],"id":885,"m":"And what is \"a stone\", William?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["A pebble."],"id":886,"m":"A pebble."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["No, it is lapis. I pray you remember in your prain."],"id":887,"m":"No, it is lapis. Please remember that in your brain."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Lapis."],"id":888,"m":"Lapis."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["That is a good William. What is he, William, that does lend articles?"],"id":889,"m":"That's a good William. What is it, William, that lends articles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Articles are borrowed of the pronoun, and be thus declined:","singulariter, nominativo, hic, haec, hoc."],"id":890,"m":"Articles are borrowed from the pronoun, and are declined like this: singular, nominative, hic, haec, hoc."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Nominativo, hig, haeg, hog, pray you, mark: genitivo, huius. Well,","what is your accusative case?"],"id":891,"m":"Nominative, hig, haeg, hog; please note: genitive, huius. Well, what is your accusative case?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Accusativo, hinc."],"id":892,"m":"Accusative, hinc."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["I pray you, have your remembrance, child. Accusativo, hung, hang,","hog."],"id":893,"m":"Please use your memory, child. Accusative, hung, hang, hog."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["\"Hang-hog\" is Latin for bacon, I warrant you."],"id":894,"m":"\"Hang-hog\" is Latin for bacon, I bet."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Leave your prabbles, 'oman.—What is the focative case, William?"],"id":895,"m":"Stop your babbling, woman. What is the vocative case, William?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["O—vocativo—O—"],"id":896,"m":"O... vocative... O..."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Remember, William; focative is caret."],"id":897,"m":"Remember, William; the vocative is caret, it is lacking."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["And that's a good root."],"id":898,"m":"And a carrot is a good root."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["'Oman, forbear."],"id":899,"m":"Woman, stop."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Peace."],"id":900,"m":"Quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["What is your genitive case plural, William?"],"id":901,"m":"What is your genitive case plural, William?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Genitive case?"],"id":902,"m":"Genitive case?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Ay."],"id":903,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Genitive: horum, harum, horum."],"id":904,"m":"Genitive: horum, harum, horum."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Vengeance of Jenny's case, fie on her! Never name her, child, if she be","a whore."],"id":905,"m":"Curse Jenny's case, shame on her! Never mention her, child, if she's a whore."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["For shame, 'oman."],"id":906,"m":"For shame, woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["You do ill to teach the child such words.—He teaches him to hick and to","hack, which they'll do fast enough of themselves; and to call \"whore","'m\"!—Fie upon you!"],"id":907,"m":"You do wrong to teach the child such words. He's teaching him to hic and to hac, meaning drink and lechery, which they'll learn fast enough by themselves; and to say \"whore 'em\"! Shame on you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["'Oman, art thou lunatics? Hast thou no understandings for thy cases,","and the numbers of the genders? Thou art as foolish Christian creatures","as I would desires."],"id":908,"m":"Woman, are you mad? Don't you understand cases, and the numbers of the genders? You are as foolish a Christian creature as anyone could wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["[To Quickly] Prithee, hold thy peace."],"id":909,"m":"(To Quickly) Please, be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Show me now, William, some declensions of your pronouns."],"id":910,"m":"Now show me, William, some declensions of your pronouns."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLIAM","lines":["Forsooth, I have forgot."],"id":911,"m":"Honestly, I've forgotten."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["It is qui, quae, quod. If you forget your quis, your quaes, and","your quods, you must be preeches. Go your ways and play, go."],"id":912,"m":"It is qui, quae, quod. If you forget your quis, your quaes and your quods, you'll be getting your breeches down for a whipping. Off you go and play, go."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["He is a better scholar than I thought he was."],"id":913,"m":"He's a better scholar than I thought he was."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["He is a good sprag memory. Farewell, Mistress Page."],"id":914,"m":"He has a good quick memory. Goodbye, Mistress Page."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Adieu, good Sir Hugh."],"id":915,"m":"Goodbye, good Sir Hugh."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Sir Hugh Evans.","id":916},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","cont":1,"lines":["Get you home, boy. Come, we stay too long."],"id":917,"m":"Go home, boy. Come, we've stayed too long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":918},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in Ford's house.","id":919},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff and Mistress Ford.","id":920},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mistress Ford, your sorrow hath eaten up my sufferance. I see you are","obsequious in your love, and I profess requital to a hair's breadth,","not only, Mistress Ford, in the simple office of love, but in all the","accoutrement, compliment, and ceremony of it. But are you sure of your","husband now?"],"id":921,"m":"Mistress Ford, your sorrow has swallowed up my suffering. I can see you are devoted in your love, and I promise to repay it to a hair's breadth, Mistress Ford, not only in the simple act of love, but in all its trappings, compliments and ceremony. But are you sure of your husband now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["He's a-birding, sweet Sir John."],"id":922,"m":"He's out bird hunting, sweet Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["[Within] What ho, gossip Ford, what ho!"],"id":923,"m":"Hello there, neighbour Ford, hello!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Step into the chamber, Sir John."],"id":924,"m":"Step into the bedroom, Sir John."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":925},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page.","id":926},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["How now, sweetheart, who's at home besides yourself?"],"id":927,"m":"Hello, sweetheart, who's at home apart from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, none but mine own people."],"id":928,"m":"Why, nobody but my own servants."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Indeed?"],"id":929,"m":"Really?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["No, certainly.","[Aside to her] Speak louder."],"id":930,"m":"No, certainly. (Aside to her) Speak louder."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Truly, I am so glad you have nobody here."],"id":931,"m":"Honestly, I'm so glad you have nobody here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why?"],"id":932,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why, woman, your husband is in his old lunes again. He so takes on","yonder with my husband, so rails against all married mankind, so curses","all Eve's daughters, of what complexion soever, and so buffets himself","on the forehead, crying \"Peer out, peer out!\" that any madness I ever","yet beheld seemed but tameness, civility, and patience, to this his","distemper he is in now. I am glad the fat knight is not here."],"id":933,"m":"Why, woman, your husband is in one of his old fits of madness again. He's raving so to my husband over there, ranting so against all married men, cursing all Eve's daughters of whatever colouring, and beating himself on the forehead crying \"Come out, come out!\", that every madness I ever saw seems like tameness, good manners and patience compared to the fit he is in now. I'm glad the fat knight isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, does he talk of him?"],"id":934,"m":"Why, is he talking about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Of none but him, and swears he was carried out, the last time he","searched for him, in a basket; protests to my husband he is now here;","and hath drawn him and the rest of their company from their sport, to","make another experiment of his suspicion. But I am glad the knight is","not here. Now he shall see his own foolery."],"id":935,"m":"About nobody but him; and he swears that the last time he searched for him, he was carried out in a basket. He insists to my husband that he's here now, and he has dragged him and the rest of their party away from their sport to test his suspicion again. But I'm glad the knight isn't here. Now he'll see his own foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["How near is he, Mistress Page?"],"id":936,"m":"How close is he, Mistress Page?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Hard by, at street end. He will be here anon."],"id":937,"m":"Close by, at the end of the street. He'll be here soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I am undone! The knight is here."],"id":938,"m":"I'm ruined! The knight is here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why, then, you are utterly shamed, and he's but a dead man. What a","woman are you! Away with him, away with him! Better shame than murder."],"id":939,"m":"Why, then you are completely disgraced, and he's a dead man. What a woman you are! Get him out, get him out! Better shame than murder."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Which way should he go? How should I bestow him? Shall I put him into","the basket again?"],"id":940,"m":"Which way should he go? Where should I put him? Shall I put him in the basket again?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":941},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, I'll come no more i' the basket. May I not go out ere he come?"],"id":942,"m":"No, I won't go in the basket again. Can't I get out before he comes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Alas, three of Master Ford's brothers watch the door with pistols, that","none shall issue out, otherwise you might slip away ere he came. But","what make you here?"],"id":943,"m":"Alas, three of Master Ford's brothers are guarding the door with pistols so that nobody can get out; otherwise you could slip away before he came. But what are you doing here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What shall I do? I'll creep up into the chimney."],"id":944,"m":"What shall I do? I'll climb up the chimney."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["There they always use to discharge their birding-pieces."],"id":945,"m":"That's where they always fire off their hunting guns."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Creep into the kiln-hole."],"id":946,"m":"Crawl into the oven."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Where is it?"],"id":947,"m":"Where is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["He will seek there, on my word. Neither press, coffer, chest, trunk,","well, vault, but he hath an abstract for the remembrance of such","places, and goes to them by his note. There is no hiding you in the","house."],"id":948,"m":"He'll look there, I promise you. There isn't a cupboard, coffer, chest, trunk, well or vault that he hasn't got on a list to remind him, and he goes to them by his notes. There's no hiding you in the house."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I'll go out then."],"id":949,"m":"Then I'll go out."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["If you go out in your own semblance, you die, Sir John—unless you go","out disguised."],"id":950,"m":"If you go out looking like yourself, you're dead, Sir John, unless you go out in disguise."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["How might we disguise him?"],"id":951,"m":"How could we disguise him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Alas the day, I know not. There is no woman's gown big enough for him;","otherwise he might put on a hat, a muffler, and a kerchief, and so","escape."],"id":952,"m":"Oh dear, I don't know. There's no woman's dress big enough for him; otherwise he could put on a hat, a scarf and a headscarf, and escape that way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good hearts, devise something. Any extremity rather than a mischief."],"id":953,"m":"Good hearts, think of something. Anything extreme rather than disaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["My maid's aunt, the fat woman of Brentford, has a gown above."],"id":954,"m":"My maid's aunt, the fat woman of Brentford, has a dress upstairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["On my word, it will serve him. She's as big as he is. And there's her","thrummed hat, and her muffler too.—Run up, Sir John."],"id":955,"m":"On my word, it will fit him. She's as big as he is. And there's her tufted hat and her scarf too. Run upstairs, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Go, go, sweet Sir John. Mistress Page and I will look some linen for","your head."],"id":956,"m":"Go, go, sweet Sir John. Mistress Page and I will find some linen for your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Quick, quick! We'll come dress you straight; put on the gown the while."],"id":957,"m":"Quick, quick! We'll come and dress you straight away; put on the dress in the meantime."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":958},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I would my husband would meet him in this shape. He cannot abide the","old woman of Brentford; he swears she's a witch, forbade her my house,","and hath threatened to beat her."],"id":959,"m":"I wish my husband would meet him dressed like that. He can't stand the old woman of Brentford; he swears she's a witch, has banned her from my house and has threatened to beat her."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Heaven guide him to thy husband's cudgel and the devil guide his cudgel","afterwards!"],"id":960,"m":"Heaven lead him to your husband's club, and the devil guide the club after that!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["But is my husband coming?"],"id":961,"m":"But is my husband really coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Ay, in good sadness is he, and talks of the basket too, howsoever he","hath had intelligence."],"id":962,"m":"Yes, in all seriousness he is, and he's talking about the basket too, however he found out."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["We'll try that; for I'll appoint my men to carry the basket again, to","meet him at the door with it as they did last time."],"id":963,"m":"We'll test that, because I'll tell my men to carry the basket again and meet him at the door with it, as they did last time."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Nay, but he'll be here presently. Let's go dress him like the witch of","Brentford."],"id":964,"m":"No, but he'll be here any minute. Let's go and dress him up as the witch of Brentford."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I'll first direct my men what they shall do with the basket. Go up,","I'll bring linen for him straight."],"id":965,"m":"First I'll tell my men what to do with the basket. Go up; I'll bring linen for him straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Ford.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Hang him, dishonest varlet! We cannot misuse him enough.","We'll leave a proof, by that which we will do,","Wives may be merry and yet honest too.","We do not act that often jest and laugh;","'Tis old but true: \"Still swine eats all the draff.\""],"id":967,"m":"Hang him, the lecherous rogue! We can't treat him badly enough. We'll prove, by what we do, that wives can be merry and still be faithful. We who often joke and laugh don't actually do it; the old saying is true: \"The quiet pig eats all the swill.\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":968},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford with John and Robert.","id":969},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Go, sirs, take the basket again on your shoulders. Your master is hard","at door; if he bid you set it down, obey him. Quickly, dispatch."],"id":970,"m":"Go on, men, lift the basket onto your shoulders again. Your master is nearly at the door; if he tells you to put it down, obey him. Quickly, hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Ford.","id":971},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN","lines":["Come, come, take it up."],"id":972,"m":"Come on, come on, pick it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROBERT","lines":["Pray heaven it be not full of knight again."],"id":973,"m":"Pray heaven it isn't full of knight again."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN","lines":["I hope not, I had lief as bear so much lead."],"id":974,"m":"I hope not; I'd as soon carry the same weight in lead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford, Page, Shallow, Caius and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":975},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Ay, but if it prove true, Master Page, have you any way then to unfool","me again?—Set down the basket, villain! Somebody call my wife. Youth in","a basket! O you panderly rascals! There's a knot, a gin, a pack, a","conspiracy against me. Now shall the devil be shamed.—What, wife, I","say! Come, come forth! Behold what honest clothes you send forth to","bleaching!"],"id":976,"m":"Yes, but if it turns out to be true, Master Page, do you have any way of un-fooling me then? Put down the basket, villain! Somebody call my wife. A young lover in a basket! Oh, you pimping rascals! There's a gang, a trap, a pack, a conspiracy against me. Now the devil will be shamed. Wife, I say! Come, come out! Look what honest clothes you're sending out to be bleached!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Why, this passes, Master Ford! You are not to go loose any longer; you","must be pinioned."],"id":977,"m":"Why, this goes too far, Master Ford! You can't be let loose any longer; you need to be tied up."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Why, this is lunatics, this is mad as a mad dog."],"id":978,"m":"Why, this is lunacy; this is as mad as a mad dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Indeed, Master Ford, this is not well, indeed."],"id":979,"m":"Really, Master Ford, this is not right, really."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["So say I too, sir."],"id":980,"m":"I say so too, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford.","id":981},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","cont":1,"lines":["Come hither, Mistress Ford—Mistress Ford, the honest woman, the modest","wife, the virtuous creature, that hath the jealous fool to her husband!","I suspect without cause, mistress, do I?"],"id":982,"m":"Come here, Mistress Ford. Mistress Ford, the honest woman, the modest wife, the virtuous creature, who has a jealous fool for a husband! I suspect without cause, madam, do I?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Heaven be my witness you do, if you suspect me in any dishonesty."],"id":983,"m":"Heaven be my witness, you do, if you suspect me of any unfaithfulness."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well said, brazen-face, hold it out.—Come forth, sirrah."],"id":984,"m":"Well said, brazen face; keep it up. Come out, you rogue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pulls clothes out of the basket.","id":985},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["This passes."],"id":986,"m":"This goes too far."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Are you not ashamed? Let the clothes alone."],"id":987,"m":"Aren't you ashamed? Leave the clothes alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I shall find you anon."],"id":988,"m":"I'll find you soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["'Tis unreasonable. Will you take up your wife's clothes? Come, away."],"id":989,"m":"It's unreasonable. Will you pick up your wife's clothes? Come away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Empty the basket, I say."],"id":990,"m":"Empty the basket, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, man, why?"],"id":991,"m":"Why, man, why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Master Page, as I am a man, there was one conveyed out of my house","yesterday in this basket. Why may not he be there again? In my house I","am sure he is. My intelligence is true, my jealousy is","reasonable.—Pluck me out all the linen."],"id":992,"m":"Master Page, as I'm a man, someone was smuggled out of my house in this basket yesterday. Why couldn't he be in it again? I'm sure he's in my house. My information is true; my jealousy is reasonable. Pull out all the linen for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["If you find a man there, he shall die a flea's death."],"id":993,"m":"If you find a man there, he'll die like a flea."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Here's no man."],"id":994,"m":"There's no man here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By my fidelity, this is not well, Master Ford, this wrongs you."],"id":995,"m":"On my word, this is not right, Master Ford; this does you wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Master Ford, you must pray, and not follow the imaginations of your own","heart. This is jealousies."],"id":996,"m":"Master Ford, you must pray, and not follow the fancies of your own heart. This is jealousy."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well, he's not here I seek for."],"id":997,"m":"Well, the man I'm looking for isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["No, nor nowhere else but in your brain."],"id":998,"m":"No, nor anywhere else but in your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Help to search my house this one time. If I find not what I seek, show","no colour for my extremity, let me for ever be your table-sport. Let","them say of me \"As jealous as Ford, that searched a hollow walnut for","his wife's leman.\" Satisfy me once more, once more search with me."],"id":999,"m":"Help me search my house this one time. If I don't find what I'm looking for, don't make any excuses for my extreme behaviour; let me be your joke at the dinner table for ever. Let them say of me, \"As jealous as Ford, who searched a hollow walnut for his wife's lover.\" Satisfy me once more; search with me once more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt John and Robert with the basket.","id":1000},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What, ho, Mistress Page! Come you and the old woman down; my husband","will come into the chamber."],"id":1001,"m":"Hello, Mistress Page! Come down, you and the old woman; my husband is coming into the bedroom."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Old woman? What old woman's that?"],"id":1002,"m":"Old woman? What old woman is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Why, it is my maid's aunt of Brentford."],"id":1003,"m":"Why, it's my maid's aunt from Brentford."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["A witch, a quean, an old cozening quean! Have I not forbid her my","house? She comes of errands, does she? We are simple men; we do not","know what's brought to pass under the profession of fortune-telling.","She works by charms, by spells, by the figure, and such daubery as this","is, beyond our element. We know nothing.—Come down, you witch, you hag,","you! Come down, I say!"],"id":1004,"m":"A witch, a hussy, an old cheating hussy! Haven't I banned her from my house? She comes on errands, does she? We are simple men; we don't know what goes on under the pretence of fortune-telling. She works with charms, with spells, with astrological charts and that kind of trickery, beyond our understanding. We know nothing. Come down, you witch, you hag, you! Come down, I say!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, good sweet husband!—Good gentlemen, let him not strike the old","woman."],"id":1005,"m":"No, good sweet husband! Good gentlemen, don't let him hit the old woman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff disguised as an old woman, led by Mistress Page.","id":1006},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Come, Mother Prat; come, give me your hand."],"id":1007,"m":"Come, Mother Prat; come, give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I'll prat her. [Beats him] Out of my door, you witch, you rag, you","baggage, you polecat, you runnion! Out, out! I'll conjure you, I'll","fortune-tell you."],"id":1008,"m":"I'll prat her. (Beats him) Out of my house, you witch, you rag, you slut, you polecat, you scab! Out, out! I'll cast a spell on you, I'll tell your fortune for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Falstaff.","id":1009},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Are you not ashamed? I think you have killed the poor woman."],"id":1010,"m":"Aren't you ashamed? I think you've killed the poor woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, he will do it. 'Tis a goodly credit for you."],"id":1011,"m":"Yes, he'll do it. It's a fine credit to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Hang her, witch!"],"id":1012,"m":"Hang her, the witch!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["By yea and no, I think the 'oman is a witch indeed. I like not when a","'oman has a great peard. I spy a great peard under her muffler."],"id":1013,"m":"Yes and no, I think the woman really is a witch. I don't like it when a woman has a big beard. I saw a big beard under her scarf."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Will you follow, gentlemen? I beseech you follow, see but the issue of","my jealousy. If I cry out thus upon no trail, never trust me when I","open again."],"id":1014,"m":"Will you follow me, gentlemen? I beg you to follow; just see how my jealousy turns out. If I bark like this on no scent, never trust me when I give tongue again."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Let's obey his humour a little further. Come, gentlemen."],"id":1015,"m":"Let's humour his mood a little longer. Come, gentlemen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Ford, Page, Caius, Evans and Shallow.","id":1016},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Trust me, he beat him most pitifully."],"id":1017,"m":"Honestly, he beat him most pitifully."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Nay, by th' mass, that he did not; he beat him most unpitifully,","methought."],"id":1018,"m":"No, by the Mass, he didn't; he beat him most pitilessly, I thought."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I'll have the cudgel hallowed and hung o'er the altar. It hath done","meritorious service."],"id":1019,"m":"I'll have the club blessed and hung over the altar. It has done praiseworthy service."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["What think you? May we, with the warrant of womanhood and the witness","of a good conscience, pursue him with any further revenge?"],"id":1020,"m":"What do you think? Can we, with a woman's right and a clear conscience, pursue him with any more revenge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["The spirit of wantonness is sure scared out of him. If the devil have","him not in fee-simple, with fine and recovery, he will never, I think,","in the way of waste, attempt us again."],"id":1021,"m":"The spirit of lechery has surely been frightened out of him. Unless the devil owns him outright, by every legal title, I don't think he will ever try to ruin us again."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Shall we tell our husbands how we have served him?"],"id":1022,"m":"Shall we tell our husbands how we've treated him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Yes, by all means, if it be but to scrape the figures out of your","husband's brains. If they can find in their hearts the poor unvirtuous","fat knight shall be any further afflicted, we two will still be the","ministers."],"id":1023,"m":"Yes, by all means, if only to scrape the fantasies out of your husband's brain. If they can find it in their hearts to have the poor sinful fat knight punished any further, we two will still carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["I'll warrant they'll have him publicly shamed, and methinks there would","be no period to the jest should he not be publicly shamed."],"id":1024,"m":"I bet they'll want him publicly shamed, and I think the joke won't be complete unless he is publicly shamed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Come, to the forge with it, then shape it. I would not have things","cool."],"id":1025,"m":"Come, to the forge with it, then, and hammer it into shape. I don't want things to cool off."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1026},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":1027},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Host and Bardolph.","id":1028},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Sir, the Germans desire to have three of your horses. The Duke himself","will be tomorrow at court, and they are going to meet him."],"id":1029,"m":"Sir, the Germans want three of your horses. The Duke himself will be at court tomorrow, and they are going to meet him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What duke should that be comes so secretly? I hear not of him in the","court. Let me speak with the gentlemen. They speak English?"],"id":1030,"m":"What duke could that be who comes so secretly? I've heard nothing of him at court. Let me speak to the gentlemen. Do they speak English?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Ay, sir. I'll call them to you."],"id":1031,"m":"Yes, sir. I'll call them for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["They shall have my horses, but I'll make them pay, I'll sauce them.","They have had my house a week at command; I have turned away my other","guests. They must come off, I'll sauce them. Come."],"id":1032,"m":"They can have my horses, but I'll make them pay; I'll overcharge them. They've had my house at their command for a week; I've turned away my other guests. They'll have to pay up; I'll overcharge them. Come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1033},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"A room in Ford's house.","id":1034},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Ford, Mistress Page, Mistress Ford and Sir Hugh Evans.","id":1035},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["'Tis one of the best discretions of a 'oman as ever I did look upon."],"id":1036,"m":"It is one of the most sensible things a woman ever did that I have seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["And did he send you both these letters at an instant?"],"id":1037,"m":"And did he send you both these letters at the same moment?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Within a quarter of an hour."],"id":1038,"m":"Within a quarter of an hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Pardon me, wife. Henceforth, do what thou wilt.","I rather will suspect the sun with cold","Than thee with wantonness. Now doth thy honour stand,","In him that was of late an heretic,","As firm as faith."],"id":1039,"m":"Forgive me, wife. From now on, do what you like. I'd sooner suspect the sun of being cold than you of being unfaithful. Now your honour stands as firm as faith in a man who was lately a heretic."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["'Tis well, 'tis well, no more.","Be not as extreme in submission as in offence.","But let our plot go forward. Let our wives","Yet once again, to make us public sport,","Appoint a meeting with this old fat fellow,","Where we may take him and disgrace him for it."],"id":1040,"m":"That's fine, that's fine, no more. Don't be as extreme in your apology as you were in your offence. But let our plot go ahead. Let our wives, once more, to give us public fun, arrange a meeting with this fat old fellow, where we can catch him and disgrace him for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["There is no better way than that they spoke of."],"id":1041,"m":"There's no better way than the one they described."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["How? To send him word they'll meet him in the park at midnight? Fie,","fie, he'll never come."],"id":1042,"m":"What? To send him word that they'll meet him in the park at midnight? No, no, he'll never come."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["You say he has been thrown in the rivers, and has been grievously","peaten as an old 'oman. Methinks there should be terrors in him, that","he should not come. Methinks his flesh is punished; he shall have no","desires."],"id":1043,"m":"You say he has been thrown in the river and badly beaten as an old woman. I think he must be terrified, so he won't come. I think his flesh has been punished; he won't have any desires."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["So think I too."],"id":1044,"m":"I think so too."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Devise but how you'll use him when he comes,","And let us two devise to bring him thither."],"id":1045,"m":"You just work out how you'll treat him when he comes, and let us two work out how to get him there."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["There is an old tale goes that Herne the hunter,","Sometime a keeper here in Windsor Forest,","Doth all the winter time, at still midnight,","Walk round about an oak, with great ragged horns,","And there he blasts the tree, and takes the cattle,","And makes milch-kine yield blood, and shakes a chain","In a most hideous and dreadful manner.","You have heard of such a spirit, and well you know","The superstitious idle-headed eld","Received and did deliver to our age,","This tale of Herne the hunter for a truth."],"id":1046,"m":"There is an old story that Herne the hunter, once a keeper here in Windsor Forest, walks round an oak tree all winter at dead of midnight, with great ragged horns; and there he withers the tree, bewitches the cattle, makes milking cows give blood, and rattles a chain in a hideous and dreadful way. You have heard of such a spirit, and you know well that superstitious, foolish old people accepted this tale of Herne the hunter as true and handed it down to our times."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Why, yet there want not many that do fear","In deep of night to walk by this Herne's oak.","But what of this?"],"id":1047,"m":"Why, there are still plenty of people who are afraid to walk by this Herne's oak in the dead of night. But what about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Marry, this is our device,","That Falstaff at that oak shall meet with us,","Disguised like Herne, with huge horns on his head."],"id":1048,"m":"Well, this is our plan: that Falstaff will meet us at that oak, disguised as Herne, with huge horns on his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Well, let it not be doubted but he'll come,","And in this shape; when you have brought him thither,","What shall be done with him? What is your plot?"],"id":1049,"m":"Well, don't doubt that he'll come, and in that disguise. When you've got him there, what shall be done with him? What's your plan?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["That likewise have we thought upon, and thus:","Nan Page my daughter, and my little son,","And three or four more of their growth, we'll dress","Like urchins, oafs and fairies, green and white,","With rounds of waxen tapers on their heads","And rattles in their hands. Upon a sudden,","As Falstaff, she, and I are newly met,","Let them from forth a sawpit rush at once","With some diffused song; upon their sight","We two in great amazedness will fly.","Then let them all encircle him about,","And fairy-like, to pinch the unclean knight,","And ask him why, that hour of fairy revel,","In their so sacred paths he dares to tread","In shape profane."],"id":1050,"m":"We've thought about that too, and this is it: we'll dress my daughter Nan Page, my little son, and three or four more of their size as goblins, elves and fairies in green and white, with circles of wax candles on their heads and rattles in their hands. Suddenly, just as Falstaff, she and I have met, let them all rush out together from a sawpit, singing some wild song. When we see them, we two will run off in great alarm. Then let them all make a circle round him and, like fairies, pinch the filthy knight, and ask him why, at the hour of the fairies' revels, he dares to tread their sacred paths in such an unholy shape."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["And till he tell the truth,","Let the supposed fairies pinch him sound","And burn him with their tapers."],"id":1051,"m":"And until he tells the truth, let the pretend fairies pinch him hard and burn him with their candles."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["The truth being known,","We'll all present ourselves, dis-horn the spirit,","And mock him home to Windsor."],"id":1052,"m":"When the truth is out, we'll all show ourselves, take the spirit's horns off and mock him all the way home to Windsor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["The children must","Be practised well to this, or they'll ne'er do 't."],"id":1053,"m":"The children must be well rehearsed for this, or they'll never manage it."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["I will teach the children their behaviours, and I will be like a","jackanapes also, to burn the knight with my taber."],"id":1054,"m":"I will teach the children how to behave, and I will dress like a monkey too, to burn the knight with my taper."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["That will be excellent. I'll go buy them vizards."],"id":1055,"m":"That will be excellent. I'll go and buy them masks."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["My Nan shall be the queen of all the fairies,","Finely attired in a robe of white."],"id":1056,"m":"My Nan shall be the queen of all the fairies, beautifully dressed in a white robe."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["That silk will I go buy.","[Aside] And in that time","Shall Master Slender steal my Nan away,","And marry her at Eton.—Go, send to Falstaff straight."],"id":1057,"m":"I'll go and buy the silk. (Aside) And in that time Master Slender will steal my Nan away and marry her at Eton. Go, send to Falstaff at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Nay, I'll to him again in name of Brook.","He'll tell me all his purpose. Sure, he'll come."],"id":1058,"m":"No, I'll go to him again as Brook. He'll tell me all his plans. He'll certainly come."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Fear not you that. Go, get us properties","And tricking for our fairies."],"id":1059,"m":"Don't you worry about that. Go and get us props and costumes for our fairies."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Let us about it. It is admirable pleasures and fery honest knaveries."],"id":1060,"m":"Let's get on with it. It is wonderful fun and very honest mischief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Page, Ford and Evans.","id":1061},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Go, Mistress Ford.","Send quickly to Sir John to know his mind."],"id":1062,"m":"Go, Mistress Ford. Send quickly to Sir John to find out what he thinks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Ford.","id":1063},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","cont":1,"lines":["I'll to the Doctor. He hath my good will,","And none but he, to marry with Nan Page.","That Slender, though well landed, is an idiot,","And he my husband best of all affects.","The Doctor is well moneyed, and his friends","Potent at court. He, none but he, shall have her,","Though twenty thousand worthier come to crave her."],"id":1064,"m":"I'll go to the Doctor. He has my blessing, and no one but him, to marry Nan Page. That Slender, though he owns plenty of land, is an idiot, and my husband prefers him above all. The Doctor has plenty of money, and his friends are powerful at court. He, and no one else, shall have her, even if twenty thousand worthier men come to ask for her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1065},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":1066},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Host and Simple.","id":1067},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What wouldst thou have, boor? What, thick-skin? Speak, breathe,","discuss; brief, short, quick, snap."],"id":1068,"m":"What do you want, peasant? Well, thick-skull? Speak, breathe, explain; brief, short, quick, snap."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Marry, sir, I come to speak with Sir John Falstaff from Master Slender."],"id":1069,"m":"Well, sir, I've come to speak with Sir John Falstaff on behalf of Master Slender."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["There's his chamber, his house, his castle, his standing-bed and","truckle-bed. 'Tis painted about with the story of the Prodigal, fresh","and new. Go, knock and call. He'll speak like an Anthropophaginian unto","thee. Knock, I say."],"id":1070,"m":"There's his room, his house, his castle, his four-poster bed and his trundle bed. It's painted all round with the story of the Prodigal Son, fresh and new. Go, knock and call. He'll speak to you like a cannibal. Knock, I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["There's an old woman, a fat woman, gone up into his chamber. I'll be so","bold as stay, sir, till she come down. I come to speak with her,","indeed."],"id":1071,"m":"There's an old woman, a fat woman, gone up into his room. I'll make so bold as to wait, sir, until she comes down. It's really her I've come to speak to."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Ha? A fat woman? The knight may be robbed. I'll call.—Bully knight!","Bully Sir John! Speak from thy lungs military. Art thou there? It is","thine host, thine Ephesian, calls."],"id":1072,"m":"What? A fat woman? The knight might be robbed. I'll call. My fine knight! My fine Sir John! Speak up from your soldier's lungs. Are you there? It's your landlord, your good drinking companion, calling."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[Above] How now, mine host?"],"id":1073,"m":"(Above) What is it, landlord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Here's a Bohemian-Tartar tarries the coming down of thy fat woman. Let","her descend, bully, let her descend. My chambers are honourable. Fie!","Privacy? Fie!"],"id":1074,"m":"Here's a wild foreigner waiting for your fat woman to come down. Let her come down, my lad, let her come down. My rooms are respectable. Shame! Private meetings? Shame!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":1075},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["There was, mine host, an old fat woman even now with me, but she's","gone."],"id":1076,"m":"Landlord, there was an old fat woman with me just now, but she's gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Pray you, sir, was't not the wise woman of Brentford?"],"id":1077,"m":"Please, sir, wasn't it the wise woman of Brentford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ay, marry was it, mussel-shell. What would you with her?"],"id":1078,"m":"Yes, it was indeed, mussel-shell. What do you want with her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["My master, sir, my Master Slender, sent to her, seeing her go through","the streets, to know, sir, whether one Nym, sir, that beguiled him of a","chain, had the chain or no."],"id":1079,"m":"My master, sir, my Master Slender, seeing her go through the streets, sent me to her to find out, sir, whether a man called Nym, sir, who cheated him of a chain, had the chain or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I spake with the old woman about it."],"id":1080,"m":"I talked to the old woman about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["And what says she, I pray, sir?"],"id":1081,"m":"And what does she say, please, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Marry, she says that the very same man that beguiled Master Slender of","his chain cozened him of it."],"id":1082,"m":"Well, she says that the very same man who cheated Master Slender of his chain swindled him out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["I would I could have spoken with the woman herself. I had other things","to have spoken with her too, from him."],"id":1083,"m":"I wish I could have spoken to the woman herself. I had other things to ask her too, from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What are they? Let us know."],"id":1084,"m":"What are they? Let's hear them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Ay, come. Quick."],"id":1085,"m":"Yes, come on. Quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["I may not conceal them, sir."],"id":1086,"m":"I mayn't conceal them, I mean reveal them, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Conceal them, or thou diest."],"id":1087,"m":"Conceal them, or you'll die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["Why, sir, they were nothing but about Mistress Anne Page, to know if it","were my master's fortune to have her or no."],"id":1088,"m":"Well, sir, they were only about Mistress Anne Page, to find out whether my master is fated to have her or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Tis, 'tis his fortune."],"id":1089,"m":"It is, it is his fate."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["What sir?"],"id":1090,"m":"What is, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["To have her, or no. Go, say the woman told me so."],"id":1091,"m":"To have her, or not. Go and say the woman told me so."},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["May I be bold to say so, sir?"],"id":1092,"m":"May I be so bold as to say so, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ay, sir; like who more bold?"],"id":1093,"m":"Yes, sir; who could be bolder?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SIMPLE","lines":["I thank your worship; I shall make my master glad with these tidings."],"id":1094,"m":"Thank you, your worship; I'll make my master happy with this news."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Simple.","id":1095},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Thou art clerkly, thou art clerkly, Sir John. Was there a wise woman","with thee?"],"id":1096,"m":"You're clever, you're clever, Sir John. Was there really a wise woman with you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ay, that there was, mine host; one that hath taught me more wit than","ever I learned before in my life; and I paid nothing for it neither,","but was paid for my learning."],"id":1097,"m":"Yes, there was, landlord; one who has taught me more sense than I ever learned before in my life; and I didn't pay anything for it either, but was paid for my lesson."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph.","id":1098},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Out, alas, sir, cozenage, mere cozenage!"],"id":1099,"m":"Oh no, sir, a swindle, a complete swindle!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Where be my horses? Speak well of them, varletto."],"id":1100,"m":"Where are my horses? Give me good news of them, you rascal."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Run away, with the cozeners. For so soon as I came beyond Eton, they","threw me off from behind one of them, in a slough of mire, and set","spurs and away, like three German devils, three Doctor Faustuses."],"id":1101,"m":"Run off with the swindlers. As soon as we got past Eton, they threw me off from behind one of them into a muddy bog, and spurred off like three German devils, three Doctor Faustuses."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["They are gone but to meet the Duke, villain, do not say they be fled.","Germans are honest men."],"id":1102,"m":"They've only gone to meet the Duke, villain; don't say they've run off. Germans are honest men."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Hugh Evans.","id":1103},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Where is mine host?"],"id":1104,"m":"Where is my landlord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["What is the matter, sir?"],"id":1105,"m":"What's the matter, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Have a care of your entertainments. There is a friend of mine come to","town tells me there is three cozen-Germans that has cozened all the","hosts of Readings, of Maidenhead, of Colebrook, of horses and money. I","tell you for good will, look you. You are wise, and full of gibes and","vlouting-stocks, and 'tis not convenient you should be cozened. Fare","you well."],"id":1106,"m":"Be careful who you take in. A friend of mine who has come to town tells me there are three cheating cousin-Germans who have swindled all the innkeepers of Reading, Maidenhead and Colnbrook out of horses and money. I tell you this out of goodwill, you see. You are clever, full of jokes and mockery, and it wouldn't be fitting for you to be swindled. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Evans.","id":1107},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Doctor Caius.","id":1108},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vere is mine host de Jarteer?"],"id":1109,"m":"Where is my landlord of the Garter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Here, Master Doctor, in perplexity and doubtful dilemma."],"id":1110,"m":"Here, Doctor, in a state of confusion and uncertain dilemma."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["I cannot tell vat is dat, but it is tell-a me dat you make grand","preparation for a Duke de Jamany. By my trot, dere is no duke that the","court is know to come. I tell you for good will. Adieu."],"id":1111,"m":"I don't know what that is, but I am told that you are making great preparations for a Duke of Germany. On my word, the court knows of no duke who is coming. I tell you out of goodwill. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Doctor Caius.","id":1112},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Hue and cry, villain, go!—Assist me, knight, I am undone.—Fly, run, hue","and cry, villain, I am undone!"],"id":1113,"m":"Raise the alarm, villain, go! Help me, knight; I'm ruined. Hurry, run, raise the alarm, villain; I'm ruined!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Host and Bardolph.","id":1114},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would all the world might be cozened, for I have been cozened and","beaten too. If it should come to the ear of the court how I have been","transformed, and how my transformation hath been washed and cudgelled,","they would melt me out of my fat drop by drop, and liquor fishermen's","boots with me. I warrant they would whip me with their fine wits till I","were as crestfallen as a dried pear. I never prospered since I forswore","myself at primero. Well, if my wind were but long enough, I would","repent."],"id":1115,"m":"I wish the whole world could be swindled, because I've been swindled and beaten too. If the court ever heard how I've been transformed, and how my transformation has been washed and clubbed, they would melt the fat out of me drop by drop and grease fishermen's boots with me. I bet they'd whip me with their sharp wit until I was as shrivelled as a dried pear. I've never had any luck since I cheated at cards. Well, if I had breath enough, I would repent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly.","id":1116},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Now, whence come you?"],"id":1117,"m":"Now, where have you come from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["From the two parties, forsooth."],"id":1118,"m":"From the two parties, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The devil take one party and his dam the other, and so they shall be","both bestowed. I have suffered more for their sakes, more than the","villainous inconstancy of man's disposition is able to bear."],"id":1119,"m":"The devil take one party and his mother the other, and then both will be taken care of. I have suffered more for their sakes than the wretched weakness of man's nature can bear."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["And have not they suffered? Yes, I warrant, speciously one of them.","Mistress Ford, good heart, is beaten black and blue, that you cannot","see a white spot about her."],"id":1120,"m":"And haven't they suffered? Yes, I promise you, especially one of them. Mistress Ford, poor heart, has been beaten black and blue, so you can't see a white spot on her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What tellst thou me of black and blue? I was beaten myself into all the","colours of the rainbow, and was like to be apprehended for the witch of","Brentford. But that my admirable dexterity of wit, my counterfeiting","the action of an old woman, delivered me, the knave constable had set","me i' the stocks, i' the common stocks, for a witch."],"id":1121,"m":"Why are you telling me about black and blue? I was beaten myself into all the colours of the rainbow, and nearly arrested as the witch of Brentford. If my wonderful quick wits, my imitation of the way an old woman walks, hadn't saved me, that rogue of a constable would have put me in the stocks, the public stocks, as a witch."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Sir, let me speak with you in your chamber, you shall hear how things","go, and, I warrant, to your content. Here is a letter will say","somewhat. Good hearts, what ado here is to bring you together! Sure,","one of you does not serve heaven well, that you are so crossed."],"id":1122,"m":"Sir, let me talk to you in your room; you'll hear how things stand, and to your satisfaction, I promise. Here is a letter that will tell you something. Goodness, what trouble there is in bringing you together! Surely one of you isn't serving heaven well, since you are so thwarted."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come up into my chamber."],"id":1123,"m":"Come up to my room."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1124},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"Another room in the Garter Inn.","id":1125},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fenton and Host.","id":1126},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Master Fenton, talk not to me. My mind is heavy. I will give over all."],"id":1127,"m":"Master Fenton, don't talk to me. My mind is heavy. I'll give up everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Yet hear me speak. Assist me in my purpose,","And, as I am a gentleman, I'll give thee","A hundred pound in gold more than your loss."],"id":1128,"m":"But hear me out. Help me with my plan, and as I am a gentleman, I'll give you a hundred pounds in gold on top of what you've lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["I will hear you, Master Fenton, and I will, at the least, keep your","counsel."],"id":1129,"m":"I'll hear you, Master Fenton, and at the very least I'll keep your secret."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["From time to time I have acquainted you","With the dear love I bear to fair Anne Page,","Who mutually hath answered my affection,","So far forth as herself might be her chooser,","Even to my wish. I have a letter from her","Of such contents as you will wonder at,","The mirth whereof so larded with my matter","That neither singly can be manifested","Without the show of both, wherein fat Falstaff","Hath a great scene; the image of the jest","I'll show you here at large. Hark, good mine host:","Tonight at Herne's oak, just 'twixt twelve and one,","Must my sweet Nan present the Fairy Queen—","The purpose why is here—in which disguise,","While other jests are something rank on foot,","Her father hath commanded her to slip","Away with Slender, and with him at Eton","Immediately to marry. She hath consented. Now, sir,","Her mother, even strong against that match","And firm for Doctor Caius, hath appointed","That he shall likewise shuffle her away,","While other sports are tasking of their minds,","And at the dean'ry, where a priest attends,","Straight marry her. To this her mother's plot","She, seemingly obedient, likewise hath","Made promise to the doctor. Now thus it rests:","Her father means she shall be all in white","And in that habit, when Slender sees his time","To take her by the hand and bid her go,","She shall go with him. Her mother hath intended","The better to denote her to the doctor,","For they must all be masked and vizarded—","That quaint in green she shall be loose enrobed,","With ribbons pendant flaring 'bout her head;","And when the doctor spies his vantage ripe,","To pinch her by the hand, and on that token","The maid hath given consent to go with him."],"id":1130,"m":"From time to time I have told you about my deep love for lovely Anne Page, who has returned my affection as fully as I could wish, as far as she is free to choose for herself. I have a letter from her with contents that will amaze you. The fun of it is so mixed up with my own business that neither can be explained without the other, and fat Falstaff has a big part in it. I'll show you the whole outline of the joke. Listen, good landlord. Tonight at Herne's oak, between twelve and one, my sweet Nan is to play the Fairy Queen (the reason is in here). In that disguise, while the other jokes are in full swing, her father has ordered her to slip away with Slender and marry him straight away at Eton. She has agreed. Now, sir, her mother, who is firmly against that match and set on Doctor Caius, has arranged that he will also spirit her away while the others are busy with their sport, and marry her at once at the deanery, where a priest will be waiting. To this plan of her mother's, she, seeming obedient, has also given her promise to the doctor. So this is how it stands. Her father means her to be dressed all in white, and in that costume, when Slender sees his chance to take her by the hand and tell her to come, she will go with him. Her mother intends, to mark her out better for the doctor (since they must all be masked and disguised), that she will be dressed loosely and prettily in green, with ribbons streaming round her head; and when the doctor sees his moment, he is to squeeze her hand, and at that signal the girl has agreed to go with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Which means she to deceive, father or mother?"],"id":1131,"m":"Which one does she mean to deceive, her father or her mother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["Both, my good host, to go along with me.","And here it rests, that you'll procure the vicar","To stay for me at church, 'twixt twelve and one,","And, in the lawful name of marrying,","To give our hearts united ceremony."],"id":1132,"m":"Both, my good landlord, by going off with me. And this is where you come in: get the vicar to wait for me at the church between twelve and one and, in the lawful name of marriage, give our united hearts their ceremony."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOST","lines":["Well, husband your device; I'll to the vicar.","Bring you the maid, you shall not lack a priest."],"id":1133,"m":"Well, manage your plan; I'll go to the vicar. You bring the girl, and you won't lack a priest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["So shall I evermore be bound to thee;","Besides, I'll make a present recompense."],"id":1134,"m":"Then I'll be in your debt for ever; and besides, I'll pay you something right away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1135},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"A room in the Garter Inn.","id":1136},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff and Mistress Quickly.","id":1137},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Prithee, no more prattling. Go. I'll hold. This is the third time; I","hope good luck lies in odd numbers. Away, go! They say there is","divinity in odd numbers, either in nativity, chance, or death. Away!"],"id":1138,"m":"Please, no more chatter. Go. I'll keep the appointment. This is the third time; I hope there's good luck in odd numbers. Off you go! They say there is something divine in odd numbers, whether in birth, chance or death. Off!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["I'll provide you a chain, and I'll do what I can to get you a pair of","horns."],"id":1139,"m":"I'll get you a chain, and I'll do what I can to get you a pair of horns."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Away, I say; time wears. Hold up your head, and mince."],"id":1140,"m":"Off, I say; time is passing. Hold your head up, and trip along."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Mistress Quickly.","id":1141},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ford.","id":1142},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Master Brook! Master Brook, the matter will be known tonight","or never. Be you in the park about midnight, at Herne's oak, and you","shall see wonders."],"id":1143,"m":"Hello, Master Brook! Master Brook, it will be settled tonight or never. Be in the park around midnight, at Herne's oak, and you will see wonders."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Went you not to her yesterday, sir, as you told me you had appointed?"],"id":1144,"m":"Didn't you go to her yesterday, sir, as you told me you had arranged?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I went to her, Master Brook, as you see, like a poor old man, but I","came from her, Master Brook, like a poor old woman. That same knave","Ford, her husband, hath the finest mad devil of jealousy in him, Master","Brook, that ever governed frenzy. I will tell you he beat me","grievously, in the shape of a woman; for in the shape of man, Master","Brook, I fear not Goliath with a weaver's beam, because I know also","life is a shuttle. I am in haste. Go along with me; I'll tell you all,","Master Brook. Since I plucked geese, played truant, and whipped top, I","knew not what 'twas to be beaten till lately. Follow me, I'll tell you","strange things of this knave Ford, on whom tonight I will be revenged,","and I will deliver his wife into your hand. Follow. Strange things in","hand, Master Brook! Follow."],"id":1145,"m":"I went to her, Master Brook, as you see me, like a poor old man; but I came away from her, Master Brook, like a poor old woman. That rogue Ford, her husband, has the finest mad devil of jealousy in him, Master Brook, that ever ruled a frenzy. I tell you, he beat me badly while I was dressed as a woman; because as a man, Master Brook, I wouldn't be afraid of Goliath with his spear like a weaver's beam, since I also know that life is as quick as a weaver's shuttle. I'm in a hurry. Come with me; I'll tell you everything, Master Brook. Since I was a boy plucking geese, playing truant and whipping tops, I never knew what it was to be beaten until lately. Follow me; I'll tell you strange things about this rogue Ford, on whom I'll have my revenge tonight, and I'll hand his wife over to you. Follow me. Strange things are afoot, Master Brook! Follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1146},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Windsor Park.","id":1147},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Shallow and Slender.","id":1148},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Come, come. We'll couch i' the castle ditch till we see the light of","our fairies. Remember, son Slender, my daughter—"],"id":1149,"m":"Come, come. We'll hide in the castle ditch until we see the light of our fairies. Remember, son Slender, my daughter—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Ay, forsooth. I have spoke with her, and we have a nay-word how to know","one another. I come to her in white and cry \"mum\"; she cries \"budget\";","and by that we know one another."],"id":1150,"m":"Yes, truly. I've spoken with her, and we have a password to recognise each other. I come to her in white and say \"mum\"; she says \"budget\"; and by that we know each other."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["That's good too. But what needs either your \"mum\" or her \"budget\"? The","white will decipher her well enough. It hath struck ten o'clock."],"id":1151,"m":"That's good too. But what need is there for your \"mum\" or her \"budget\"? The white will identify her well enough. It has struck ten o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["The night is dark. Light and spirits will become it well. Heaven","prosper our sport! No man means evil but the devil, and we shall know","him by his horns. Let's away; follow me."],"id":1152,"m":"The night is dark. Lights and spirits will suit it well. Heaven make our fun succeed! No one means harm except the devil, and we'll know him by his horns. Let's go; follow me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1153},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The street in Windsor.","id":1154},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Page, Mistress Ford and Doctor Caius.","id":1155},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Master Doctor, my daughter is in green. When you see your time, take","her by the hand, away with her to the deanery, and dispatch it quickly.","Go before into the park. We two must go together."],"id":1156,"m":"Doctor, my daughter is in green. When you see your chance, take her by the hand, off with her to the deanery and get it done quickly. Go ahead into the park. We two must go together."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["I know vat I have to do. Adieu."],"id":1157,"m":"I know what I have to do. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Fare you well, sir."],"id":1158,"m":"Goodbye, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Caius.","id":1159},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","cont":1,"lines":["My husband will not rejoice so much at the abuse of Falstaff as he will","chafe at the doctor's marrying my daughter. But 'tis no matter. Better","a little chiding than a great deal of heartbreak."],"id":1160,"m":"My husband won't be as pleased at the trick on Falstaff as he'll be angry at the doctor marrying my daughter. But it doesn't matter. Better a little scolding than a great deal of heartbreak."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Where is Nan now, and her troop of fairies, and the Welsh devil Hugh?"],"id":1161,"m":"Where are Nan and her troop of fairies now, and that Welsh devil Hugh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["They are all couched in a pit hard by Herne's oak, with obscured","lights, which, at the very instant of Falstaff's and our meeting, they","will at once display to the night."],"id":1162,"m":"They're all hiding in a pit close to Herne's oak with their lights covered, and at the very moment Falstaff meets us, they will suddenly reveal them to the night."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["That cannot choose but amaze him."],"id":1163,"m":"That's bound to terrify him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["If he be not amazed, he will be mocked; if he be amazed, he will every","way be mocked."],"id":1164,"m":"If he isn't terrified, he'll be mocked; if he is terrified, he'll be mocked all the more."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["We'll betray him finely."],"id":1165,"m":"We'll trap him nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Against such lewdsters and their lechery,","Those that betray them do no treachery."],"id":1166,"m":"Against such lechers and their lechery, those who trap them commit no treachery."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["The hour draws on. To the oak, to the oak!"],"id":1167,"m":"The hour is coming. To the oak, to the oak!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1168},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Windsor Park.","id":1169},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir Hugh Evans disguised, and children as Fairies.","id":1170},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Trib, trib, fairies. Come, and remember your parts. Be pold, I pray","you, follow me into the pit, and when I give the watch-'ords, do as I","pid you. Come, come; trib, trib."],"id":1171,"m":"Trip, trip, fairies. Come, and remember your parts. Be bold, please; follow me into the pit, and when I give the password, do as I told you. Come, come; trip, trip."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1172},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Another part of the Park.","id":1173},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff wearing a buck's head.","id":1174},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The Windsor bell hath struck twelve, the minute draws on. Now the","hot-blooded gods assist me! Remember, Jove, thou wast a bull for thy","Europa; love set on thy horns. O powerful love, that in some respects,","makes a beast a man, in some other a man a beast! You were also,","Jupiter, a swan for the love of Leda. O omnipotent love, how near the","god drew to the complexion of a goose! A fault done first in the form","of a beast; O Jove, a beastly fault! And then another fault in the","semblance of a fowl; think on't, Jove, a foul fault! When gods have hot","backs, what shall poor men do? For me, I am here a Windsor stag, and","the fattest, I think, i' the forest. Send me a cool rut-time, Jove, or","who can blame me to piss my tallow? Who comes here? My doe?"],"id":1175,"m":"The Windsor bell has struck twelve; the moment is coming. Now may the hot-blooded gods help me! Remember, Jove, you became a bull for your Europa; love gave you horns. Oh, powerful love, which in some ways turns a beast into a man, and in others a man into a beast! You were also a swan, Jupiter, for love of Leda. Oh, all-powerful love, how close the god came to looking like a goose! A sin committed first in the shape of a beast, oh Jove, a beastly sin! And then another sin in the shape of a bird; think about it, Jove, a fowl sin! When gods are on heat, what are poor men to do? As for me, here I am, a Windsor stag, and the fattest in the forest, I think. Send me a cool mating season, Jove, or who can blame me for sweating off my fat? Who's coming? My doe?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Ford and Mistress Page.","id":1176},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Sir John? Art thou there, my deer, my male deer?"],"id":1177,"m":"Sir John? Are you there, my deer, my male deer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My doe with the black scut! Let the sky rain potatoes, let it thunder","to the tune of \"Greensleeves\", hail kissing-comfits and snow eringoes;","let there come a tempest of provocation, I will shelter me here."],"id":1178,"m":"My doe with the black tail! Let the sky rain sweet potatoes, let it thunder to the tune of \"Greensleeves\", let it hail kissing sweets and snow aphrodisiacs; let a whole storm of temptation come, I'll shelter here."},{"t":"sd","text":"He embraces her.","id":1179},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Mistress Page is come with me, sweetheart."],"id":1180,"m":"Mistress Page has come with me, sweetheart."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Divide me like a bribed buck, each a haunch. I will keep my sides to","myself, my shoulders for the fellow of this walk, and my horns I","bequeath your husbands. Am I a woodman, ha? Speak I like Herne the","hunter? Why, now is Cupid a child of conscience; he makes restitution.","As I am a true spirit, welcome!"],"id":1181,"m":"Divide me like a stolen deer, a haunch each. I'll keep my ribs for myself, my shoulders for the keeper of this part of the forest, and my horns I leave to your husbands. Am I a huntsman, ha? Do I speak like Herne the hunter? Why, now Cupid is a child with a conscience; he is making up for things. As I am a true spirit, welcome!"},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise of horns within.","id":1182},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Alas, what noise?"],"id":1183,"m":"Oh no, what's that noise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Heaven forgive our sins!"],"id":1184,"m":"Heaven forgive our sins!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What should this be?"],"id":1185,"m":"What could this be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD AND MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Away, away!"],"id":1186,"m":"Run, run!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They run off.","id":1187},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I think the devil will not have me damned, lest the oil that's in me","should set hell on fire; he would never else cross me thus."],"id":1188,"m":"I think the devil doesn't want me damned, in case the fat in me sets hell on fire; otherwise he would never thwart me like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mistress Quickly as the Queen of Fairies, Sir Hugh Evans as a Satyr, Pistol as Hobgoblin, Anne Page and children as Fairies, carrying tapers.","id":1189},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Fairies, black, grey, green, and white,","You moonshine revellers and shades of night,","You orphan heirs of fixed destiny,","Attend your office and your quality.","Crier Hobgoblin, make the fairy oyes."],"id":1190,"m":"Fairies, black, grey, green and white, you moonlight revellers and shadows of the night, you orphaned children of unchanging fate, do your duty and your work. Hobgoblin, as crier, call the fairies to order."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Elves, list your names; silence, you airy toys!","Cricket, to Windsor chimneys shalt thou leap,","Where fires thou find'st unraked and hearths unswept,","There pinch the maids as blue as bilberry.","Our radiant queen hates sluts and sluttery."],"id":1191,"m":"Elves, listen for your names; silence, you airy creatures! Cricket, you will leap to Windsor's chimneys, and where you find fires not banked down and hearths not swept, pinch the maids there as blue as bilberries. Our radiant queen hates sluts and slovenliness."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["They are fairies, he that speaks to them shall die.","I'll wink and couch. No man their works must eye."],"id":1192,"m":"They are fairies; whoever speaks to them will die. I'll shut my eyes and lie down. No man may watch what they do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lies down upon his face.","id":1193},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Where's Bead? Go you, and where you find a maid","That ere she sleep has thrice her prayers said,","Rein up the organs of her fantasy;","Sleep she as sound as careless infancy.","But those as sleep and think not on their sins,","Pinch them, arms, legs, backs, shoulders, sides, and shins."],"id":1194,"m":"Where's Bead? Go, and where you find a girl who has said her prayers three times before she sleeps, calm her imagination so she sleeps as soundly as a carefree baby. But those who sleep without thinking about their sins, pinch them on their arms, legs, backs, shoulders, sides and shins."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["About, about!","Search Windsor castle, elves, within and out.","Strew good luck, oafs, on every sacred room,","That it may stand till the perpetual doom","In state as wholesome as in state 'tis fit,","Worthy the owner and the owner it.","The several chairs of order look you scour","With juice of balm and every precious flower.","Each fair instalment, coat, and several crest,","With loyal blazon, evermore be blest!","And nightly, meadow-fairies, look you sing,","Like to the Garter's compass, in a ring.","Th' expressure that it bears, green let it be,","More fertile-fresh than all the field to see;","And Honi soit qui mal y pense write","In em'rald tufts, flowers purple, blue and white,","Like sapphire, pearl, and rich embroidery,","Buckled below fair knighthood's bending knee.","Fairies use flowers for their charactery.","Away, disperse! But till 'tis one o'clock,","Our dance of custom round about the oak","Of Herne the hunter let us not forget."],"id":1195,"m":"Off you go, off you go! Search Windsor Castle, elves, inside and out. Scatter good luck, goblins, in every sacred room, so that it may stand until Judgement Day in a condition as sound as it is fitting, worthy of its owner and its owner worthy of it. Make sure you polish the stalls of the Order with balm and every precious flower. May every fair stall, coat of arms and crest, with its loyal emblem, be blessed for ever! And every night, meadow fairies, make sure you sing in a ring like the circle of the Garter. Let the mark it leaves in the grass be green, fresher and more fertile than the whole field around; and write \"Honi soit qui mal y pense\", the Garter's motto, in emerald tufts and purple, blue and white flowers, like sapphires, pearls and rich embroidery fastened below the bending knee of noble knighthood. Fairies use flowers for their writing. Away, scatter! But until one o'clock let us not forget our customary dance around the oak of Herne the hunter."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Pray you, lock hand in hand, yourselves in order set;","And twenty glow-worms shall our lanterns be,","To guide our measure round about the tree.","But stay, I smell a man of middle earth."],"id":1196,"m":"Please join hands, and put yourselves in order; and twenty glow-worms will be our lanterns to guide our dance round the tree. But wait, I smell a mortal man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Heavens defend me from that Welsh fairy, lest he transform me to a","piece of cheese!"],"id":1197,"m":"Heaven protect me from that Welsh fairy, in case he turns me into a piece of cheese!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Vile worm, thou wast o'erlooked even in thy birth."],"id":1198,"m":"Vile worm, you were bewitched from the moment you were born."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["With trial-fire touch me his finger-end.","If he be chaste, the flame will back descend","And turn him to no pain; but if he start,","It is the flesh of a corrupted heart."],"id":1199,"m":"Touch the tip of his finger with the testing fire. If he is chaste, the flame will turn back and cause him no pain; but if he flinches, it is the flesh of a corrupt heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["A trial, come."],"id":1200,"m":"A test, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Come, will this wood take fire?"],"id":1201,"m":"Come, will this wood catch fire?"},{"t":"sd","text":"They put the tapers to his fingers, and he starts.","id":1202},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O, o, o!"],"id":1203,"m":"Oh, oh, oh!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","lines":["Corrupt, corrupt, and tainted in desire!","About him, fairies, sing a scornful rhyme,","And, as you trip, still pinch him to your time."],"id":1204,"m":"Corrupt, corrupt and tainted with desire! Circle round him, fairies, sing a scornful rhyme, and as you dance, keep pinching him in time."},{"t":"sp","who":"FAIRIES","lines":["~Fie on sinful fantasy!","~Fie on lust and luxury!","~Lust is but a bloody fire,","~Kindled with unchaste desire,","~Fed in heart, whose flames aspire,","~As thoughts do blow them, higher and higher.","~Pinch him, fairies, mutually;","~Pinch him for his villainy.","~Pinch him and burn him and turn him about,","~Till candles and starlight and moonshine be out."],"id":1205,"m":"Shame on sinful fancies! Shame on lust and lechery! Lust is just a bloody fire, kindled by unchaste desire, fed in the heart, whose flames rise as thoughts blow on them, higher and higher. Pinch him, fairies, all together; pinch him for his villainy. Pinch him and burn him and turn him about, till candles and starlight and moonlight are out."},{"t":"sd","text":"During the song they pinch him, and Doctor Caius comes one way and steals away a boy in green; and Slender another way takes a boy in white; Fenton comes in and steals away Anne Page. A noise of hunting is heard within and all the fairies run away. Falstaff pulls off his buck's head, and rises up.","id":1206},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Page, Ford, Mistress Page and Mistress Ford.","id":1207},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Nay, do not fly. I think we have watched you now.","Will none but Herne the hunter serve your turn?"],"id":1208,"m":"No, don't run away. I think we've caught you now. Will nothing but Herne the hunter do for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["I pray you, come, hold up the jest no higher.—","Now, good Sir John, how like you Windsor wives?","See you these, husband?"],"id":1209,"m":"Please, come, don't push the joke any further. Now, good Sir John, how do you like Windsor wives? Do you see these, husband?"},{"t":"sd","text":"She points to the horns.","id":1210},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","cont":1,"lines":["Do not these fair yokes","Become the forest better than the town?"],"id":1211,"m":"Don't these fine horns suit the forest better than the town?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Now, sir, who's a cuckold now? Master Brook, Falstaff's a knave, a","cuckoldly knave. Here are his horns, Master Brook. And, Master Brook,","he hath enjoyed nothing of Ford's but his buck-basket, his cudgel, and","twenty pounds of money, which must be paid to Master Brook. His horses","are arrested for it, Master Brook."],"id":1212,"m":"Now, sir, who's the cuckold now? Master Brook, Falstaff is a rogue, a cuckolded rogue. Here are his horns, Master Brook. And, Master Brook, he has enjoyed nothing of Ford's except his laundry basket, his club and twenty pounds of money, which must be paid back to Master Brook. His horses have been seized for it, Master Brook."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS FORD","lines":["Sir John, we have had ill luck, we could never meet. I will never take","you for my love again, but I will always count you my deer."],"id":1213,"m":"Sir John, we've had bad luck; we could never meet. I'll never take you as my love again, but I'll always count you my dear deer."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass."],"id":1214,"m":"I'm beginning to see that I've been made an ass of."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Ay, and an ox too. Both the proofs are extant."],"id":1215,"m":"Yes, and an ox too. Both proofs are on your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And these are not fairies? I was three or four times in the thought","they were not fairies; and yet the guiltiness of my mind, the sudden","surprise of my powers, drove the grossness of the foppery into a","received belief, in despite of the teeth of all rhyme and reason, that","they were fairies. See now how wit may be made a Jack-a-Lent when 'tis","upon ill employment!"],"id":1216,"m":"And these aren't fairies? Three or four times I thought they weren't fairies; and yet my guilty conscience and the sudden shock to my senses turned this crude trickery into a firm belief, against all rhyme and reason, that they were fairies. See now how wit can be made a puppet when it's put to bad use!"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Sir John Falstaff, serve Got, and leave your desires, and fairies will","not pinse you."],"id":1217,"m":"Sir John Falstaff, serve God and give up your desires, and fairies won't pinch you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Well said, fairy Hugh."],"id":1218,"m":"Well said, Fairy Hugh."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["And leave you your jealousies too, I pray you."],"id":1219,"m":"And you give up your jealousy too, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["I will never mistrust my wife again, till thou art able to woo her in","good English."],"id":1220,"m":"I'll never mistrust my wife again until you're able to woo her in good English."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Have I laid my brain in the sun, and dried it, that it wants matter to","prevent so gross o'erreaching as this? Am I ridden with a Welsh goat","too? Shall I have a cox-comb of frieze? 'Tis time I were choked with a","piece of toasted cheese."],"id":1221,"m":"Have I left my brain out in the sun and dried it out, so that it lacks the sense to avoid such a crude trick as this? Am I made a fool of by a Welsh goat as well? Shall I wear a fool's cap made of Welsh wool? It's time I choked on a piece of toasted cheese."},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["Seese is not good to give putter. Your belly is all putter."],"id":1222,"m":"Cheese is not good to give with butter. Your belly is all butter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["\"Seese\" and \"putter\"? Have I lived to stand at the taunt of one that","makes fritters of English? This is enough to be the decay of lust and","late-walking through the realm."],"id":1223,"m":"\"Seese\" and \"putter\"? Have I lived to be mocked by someone who makes fritters of English? This is enough to ruin lust and late-night prowling throughout the kingdom."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why, Sir John, do you think, though we would have thrust virtue out of","our hearts by the head and shoulders, and have given ourselves without","scruple to hell, that ever the devil could have made you our delight?"],"id":1224,"m":"Why, Sir John, do you think that even if we had thrown virtue out of our hearts by the head and shoulders and given ourselves to hell without a qualm, the devil could ever have made you our delight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["What, a hodge-pudding? A bag of flax?"],"id":1225,"m":"What, a stuffed sausage? A sack of flax?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["A puffed man?"],"id":1226,"m":"A bloated man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Old, cold, withered, and of intolerable entrails?"],"id":1227,"m":"Old, cold, withered and with unbearable guts?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["And one that is as slanderous as Satan?"],"id":1228,"m":"And as slanderous as Satan?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["And as poor as Job?"],"id":1229,"m":"And as poor as Job?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["And as wicked as his wife?"],"id":1230,"m":"And as wicked as Job's wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"EVANS","lines":["And given to fornications, and to taverns, and sack, and wine, and","metheglins, and to drinkings and swearings and starings, pribbles and","prabbles?"],"id":1231,"m":"And given to fornication, and to taverns, and sack, and wine, and mead, and to drinking and swearing and staring, squabbling and quarrelling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, I am your theme. You have the start of me. I am dejected, I am","not able to answer the Welsh flannel. Ignorance itself is a plummet","o'er me. Use me as you will."],"id":1232,"m":"Well, I'm your subject. You have the advantage of me. I'm downcast; I'm not able to answer this Welsh flannel. Ignorance itself has sunk me. Do what you like with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Marry, sir, we'll bring you to Windsor to one Master Brook, that you","have cozened of money, to whom you should have been a pander. Over and","above that you have suffered, I think to repay that money will be a","biting affliction."],"id":1233,"m":"Well, sir, we'll take you to Windsor to a certain Master Brook, whom you have swindled out of money and for whom you were going to be a pimp. On top of everything you have suffered, I think paying back that money will be a painful torment."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Yet be cheerful, knight. Thou shalt eat a posset tonight at my house,","where I will desire thee to laugh at my wife, that now laughs at thee.","Tell her Master Slender hath married her daughter."],"id":1234,"m":"But cheer up, knight. You'll have a hot spiced drink at my house tonight, where I'll ask you to laugh at my wife, who is laughing at you now. Tell her Master Slender has married her daughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["[Aside] Doctors doubt that. If Anne Page be my daughter, she is, by","this, Doctor Caius' wife."],"id":1235,"m":"(Aside) Doctors doubt that. If Anne Page is my daughter, she's Doctor Caius's wife by now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Slender.","id":1236},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Whoa, ho, ho, father Page!"],"id":1237,"m":"Hey, hey, father Page!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Son, how now! How now, son, have you dispatched?"],"id":1238,"m":"Son, what news? What news, son, have you done it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["Dispatched? I'll make the best in Gloucestershire know on't. Would I","were hanged, la, else!"],"id":1239,"m":"Done it? I'll make the greatest men in Gloucestershire hear about it. Or else may I be hanged, I swear!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Of what, son?"],"id":1240,"m":"About what, son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I came yonder at Eton to marry Mistress Anne Page, and she's a great","lubberly boy. If it had not been i' the church, I would have swinged","him, or he should have swinged me. If I did not think it had been Anne","Page, would I might never stir! And 'tis a postmaster's boy."],"id":1241,"m":"I went over to Eton to marry Mistress Anne Page, and she's a great clumsy boy. If we hadn't been in church, I'd have thrashed him, or he would have thrashed me. If I didn't think it was Anne Page, may I never move again! And it's the postmaster's boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Upon my life, then, you took the wrong."],"id":1242,"m":"On my life, then, you took the wrong one."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["What need you tell me that? I think so, when I took a boy for a girl.","If I had been married to him, for all he was in woman's apparel, I","would not have had him."],"id":1243,"m":"Why do you need to tell me that? I think so too, when I took a boy for a girl. If I had married him, even though he was dressed as a woman, I wouldn't have had him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Why, this is your own folly. Did not I tell you how you should know my","daughter by her garments?"],"id":1244,"m":"Why, this is your own stupidity. Didn't I tell you how you would recognise my daughter by her clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLENDER","lines":["I went to her in white and cried \"mum\", and she cried \"budget\", as Anne","and I had appointed, and yet it was not Anne, but a postmaster's boy."],"id":1245,"m":"I went to her in white and said \"mum\", and she said \"budget\", as Anne and I had arranged; and yet it wasn't Anne but a postmaster's boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Good George, be not angry. I knew of your purpose, turned my daughter","into green, and indeed she is now with the doctor at the deanery, and","there married."],"id":1246,"m":"Good George, don't be angry. I knew of your plan and dressed my daughter in green instead, and in fact she's now with the doctor at the deanery, and married there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Doctor Caius.","id":1247},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Vere is Mistress Page? By gar, I am cozened, I ha' married un garçon,","a boy; un paysan, by gar, a boy. It is not Anne Page. By gar, I am","cozened."],"id":1248,"m":"Where is Mistress Page? By God, I've been swindled; I have married a boy, a peasant, by God, a boy. It is not Anne Page. By God, I've been swindled."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why, did you take her in green?"],"id":1249,"m":"Why, did you take the one in green?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAIUS","lines":["Ay, by gar, and 'tis a boy. By gar, I'll raise all Windsor."],"id":1250,"m":"Yes, by God, and it's a boy. By God, I'll raise all of Windsor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["This is strange. Who hath got the right Anne?"],"id":1251,"m":"This is strange. Who has got the real Anne?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fenton and Anne Page.","id":1252},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["My heart misgives me. Here comes Master Fenton.—How now, Master Fenton!"],"id":1253,"m":"I have a bad feeling about this. Here comes Master Fenton. Well, Master Fenton!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANNE","lines":["Pardon, good father. Good my mother, pardon."],"id":1254,"m":"Forgive me, good father. My good mother, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Now, mistress, how chance you went not with Master Slender?"],"id":1255,"m":"Now, young lady, how is it you didn't go with Master Slender?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Why went you not with Master Doctor, maid?"],"id":1256,"m":"Why didn't you go with the Doctor, girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FENTON","lines":["You do amaze her. Hear the truth of it.","You would have married her most shamefully,","Where there was no proportion held in love.","The truth is, she and I, long since contracted,","Are now so sure that nothing can dissolve us.","Th' offence is holy that she hath committed,","And this deceit loses the name of craft,","Of disobedience, or unduteous title,","Since therein she doth evitate and shun","A thousand irreligious cursed hours,","Which forced marriage would have brought upon her."],"id":1257,"m":"You are bewildering her. Hear the truth of it. You would have married her most shamefully, to men she could never match in love. The truth is that she and I, engaged long ago, are now so firmly bound that nothing can separate us. The offence she has committed is a holy one, and this deceit no longer deserves to be called cunning, disobedience or lack of duty, since by it she avoids and escapes a thousand unholy, cursed hours that a forced marriage would have brought her."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Stand not amazed, here is no remedy.","In love, the heavens themselves do guide the state.","Money buys lands, and wives are sold by fate."],"id":1258,"m":"Don't stand there amazed; there's nothing to be done. In love, the heavens themselves decide what happens. Money buys land, but wives are given out by fate."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am glad, though you have ta'en a special stand to strike at me, that","your arrow hath glanced."],"id":1259,"m":"I'm glad that, though you took careful aim at me, your arrow has glanced off and hit you too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Well, what remedy? Fenton, heaven give thee joy!","What cannot be eschewed must be embraced."],"id":1260,"m":"Well, what can be done? Fenton, heaven give you joy! What can't be avoided must be welcomed."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["When night-dogs run, all sorts of deer are chased."],"id":1261,"m":"When dogs run at night, all kinds of deer get hunted."},{"t":"sp","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","lines":["Well, I will muse no further.—Master Fenton,","Heaven give you many, many merry days!","Good husband, let us every one go home,","And laugh this sport o'er by a country fire,","Sir John and all."],"id":1262,"m":"Well, I won't brood about it any more. Master Fenton, heaven give you many, many happy days! Good husband, let's all go home and laugh this game over by a country fire, Sir John and all."},{"t":"sp","who":"FORD","lines":["Let it be so, Sir John,","To Master Brook you yet shall hold your word,","For he tonight shall lie with Mistress Ford."],"id":1263,"m":"So be it. Sir John, you'll still keep your word to Master Brook, because tonight he'll sleep with Mistress Ford."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1264}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Sir John Falstaff","group":"The Garter Inn","desc":"A fat, broke, witty old knight staying at the Garter Inn with his followers. Short of money, he writes identical love letters to Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, hoping to get at their husbands' purses. The wives lead him on three times: he is tipped into the Thames in a laundry basket, beaten as the old woman of Brentford, and pinched and burned by 'fairies' at Herne's oak. Through it all he confides each failure to 'Master Brook', not knowing that Brook is the husband he is trying to wrong. In the end he admits he has been 'made an ass' and is invited home to supper."},{"name":"Mistress Ford","group":"The Fords","desc":"Alice Ford, wife of the jealous Frank Ford. She receives Falstaff's letter, compares it with Mistress Page's, and agrees to 'act any villainy against him' that will not stain her honesty. She plays the willing lover each time Falstaff visits, while making fools of both him and her husband. Her trust in her friend and her quick thinking drive the plot."},{"name":"Ford","group":"The Fords","desc":"Frank Ford, a well-off citizen who is wildly jealous of his wife. Warned by Pistol, he disguises himself as 'Master Brook', pays Falstaff to seduce her, and learns of each meeting in advance. He searches his own house twice, finding only a laundry basket and the 'witch of Brentford', whom he beats. When the wives reveal their tricks he begs forgiveness and joins the plan to shame Falstaff."},{"name":"Mistress Page","group":"The Pages","desc":"Margaret (Meg) Page, Mistress Ford's friend and the play's chief plotter. She invents the laundry basket alarm and the Herne's oak trick, proving that 'Wives may be merry, and yet honest too'. She wants her daughter Anne to marry the rich Doctor Caius and secretly arranges for him to carry Anne off in green, but she is outwitted in turn."},{"name":"Page","group":"The Pages","desc":"George Page, Mistress Page's easy-going husband. Unlike Ford, he trusts his wife completely and laughs at Ford's jealousy. He is set on marrying Anne to Slender, who owns land, and rejects Fenton as a spendthrift friend of the wild Prince Hal. He plans for Slender to take Anne away in white, but accepts Fenton when the match is made."},{"name":"Anne Page","group":"The Pages","desc":"The Pages' daughter, who will inherit seven hundred pounds. Her father wants her to marry Slender and her mother wants Caius; she calls one a fool and would rather be 'bowled to death with turnips' than wed the other. She loves Fenton, agrees to both parents' plans while secretly planning her own, and slips away from the fairy masque to marry him."},{"name":"William Page","group":"The Pages","desc":"The Pages' young son. Sir Hugh tests his Latin grammar in 4.1, while Mistress Quickly hears rude meanings in every answer."},{"name":"Fenton","group":"Anne's suitors","desc":"A young gentleman of high birth who has wasted his money and once kept company with the wild Prince Hal and Poins. Page thinks he wants Anne only for her fortune. Fenton admits that was his first motive but says he came to love her for herself. With the Host's help he gets a vicar to marry them during the fairy masque, and defends the match as a holy offence."},{"name":"Slender","group":"Anne's suitors","desc":"Abraham Slender, Justice Shallow's foolish young cousin, with land worth three hundred pounds a year. He is pushed into wooing Anne, cannot think of anything to say to her, and talks about bears and his father's stolen geese. Page's plan has him carry off Anne in white, but he finds himself at Eton church with 'a great lubberly boy'."},{"name":"Doctor Caius","group":"Anne's suitors","desc":"A hot-tempered French physician who murders English grammar. Mistress Quickly keeps his house. He challenges Sir Hugh to a duel for helping Slender, is tricked by the Host, and joins Evans in getting revenge. Mistress Page's choice for Anne, he carries off the figure in green and marries a boy."},{"name":"Justice Shallow","group":"Anne's suitors","desc":"Robert Shallow, an old country Justice of the Peace from Gloucestershire who boasts of his family's coat of arms and his fighting youth. He comes to Windsor complaining that Falstaff beat his men and killed his deer, and pushes his cousin Slender into courting Anne."},{"name":"Simple","group":"Anne's suitors","desc":"Peter Simple, Slender's servant. He carries Evans's letter to Mistress Quickly, hides in Caius's cupboard, and later visits the 'wise woman of Brentford' to ask about his master's fortunes."},{"name":"Mistress Quickly","group":"Windsor","desc":"Doctor Caius's housekeeper and Windsor's busiest go-between. She promises to help all three of Anne's suitors while favouring Fenton, carries the wives' messages to Falstaff, mangles words ('allicholy', 'fartuous'), and hears rude puns in William's Latin. In the last scene she plays the Queen of Fairies."},{"name":"Sir Hugh Evans","group":"Windsor","desc":"A Welsh parson and schoolmaster whose English is full of Welsh sounds ('Got', 'peat', 'pribbles and prabbles'). He proposes the match between Slender and Anne, is challenged by Caius, and joins him against the Host. He trains the children as fairies and plays a satyr at Herne's oak, and tells Falstaff to 'serve Got, and leave your desires'."},{"name":"Host of the Garter","group":"Windsor","desc":"The loud, joking landlord of the Garter Inn, full of nicknames ('bully rook'). He tricks Caius and Evans into waiting at different places for their duel, helps Ford meet Falstaff as Brook, and loses three horses to 'Germans' who turn out to be swindlers. He helps Fenton by fetching the vicar."},{"name":"Pistol","group":"The Garter Inn","desc":"Falstaff's swaggering follower, who speaks in bits of old ranting plays. He refuses to carry Falstaff's love letter and tells Ford instead. He plays Hobgoblin at Herne's oak."},{"name":"Nym","group":"The Garter Inn","desc":"Falstaff's follower who talks endlessly about 'humours'. He refuses to carry the letter, keeping 'the haviour of reputation', and warns Page."},{"name":"Bardolph","group":"The Garter Inn","desc":"Falstaff's red-faced follower, taken on by the Host as a tapster. He shows 'Master Brook' up to Falstaff and later reports that the Germans have run off with the horses."},{"name":"Robin","group":"The Garter Inn","desc":"Falstaff's small page. He carries his master's letters and messages, but keeps the wives' secret and warns them when Falstaff arrives."},{"name":"Rugby","group":"Windsor","desc":"John Rugby, Doctor Caius's servant, who keeps watch for his master and goes with him to the duel."},{"name":"John and Robert","group":"The Fords","desc":"Ford's servants, who carry the laundry basket with Falstaff inside it and tip it into the ditch by the Thames."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Justice Shallow arrives at Page's house in Windsor, furious that Falstaff has beaten his men, killed his deer and broken into his lodge. Sir Hugh Evans suggests a happier subject: a marriage between Shallow's cousin Slender and Anne Page, who has seven hundred pounds coming to her. Falstaff cheerfully admits everything, including cracking Slender's head, and Slender accuses Bardolph, Nym and Pistol of getting him drunk and picking his pocket. Page invites them all to dinner. Shallow and Evans press Slender to say whether he can love Anne; he says he will marry her if asked, and hopes their love will 'decrease' on acquaintance. Left with Anne, Slender is too awkward to come in to dinner and talks about bears.","key":["Falstaff is introduced as a cheerful lawbreaker: 'I have done all this. That is now answered.'","Anne's fortune of seven hundred pounds makes her a prize for every suitor.","Slender's word-muddles ('dissolutely' for 'resolutely') show he has no idea what love is."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Shallow accuse Falstaff of?","o":["Stealing his best horse from the stable at Windsor","Beating his men, killing his deer and breaking into his lodge","Seducing his wife","Cheating at cards"],"a":1},{"q":"Who first suggests that Slender should marry Anne Page?","o":["Shallow","Page","Sir Hugh Evans","Mistress Quickly"],"a":2},{"q":"How much money did Anne's grandfather leave her?","o":["Three hundred pounds","Seven hundred pounds","A thousand pounds","Twenty pounds"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Sir Hugh sends Slender's servant Simple with a letter to Mistress Quickly, Doctor Caius's housekeeper, asking her to speak to Anne Page on Slender's behalf.","key":["Mistress Quickly is introduced as the person who can reach Anne.","Evans is busy as a matchmaker as well as a parson.","A tiny scene that sets the suitors' plot running."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Evans send with the letter?","o":["Rugby","Robin","Simple","Bardolph"],"a":2},{"q":"Who is the letter for?","o":["Anne Page herself","Mistress Quickly","Mistress Page, Anne's mother","Doctor Caius"],"a":1},{"q":"What is Evans going back to finish?","o":["His sermon","His dinner","His letter","His Latin lesson"],"a":1}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"At the Garter Inn, Falstaff tells the Host he must cut costs, and the Host takes Bardolph on as a tapster. Falstaff then explains his plan: he will make love to Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, who both control their husbands' money, and he has written each of them a letter. Pistol and Nym refuse to carry the letters, so Falstaff sacks them and sends his page Robin instead. Left alone, Pistol and Nym plot revenge by telling Ford and Page.","key":["Falstaff's motive is money: the wives will be 'my East and West Indies'.","He thinks every glance from a woman is an invitation.","His own followers betray him: the plot is doomed before it starts."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Falstaff want to woo the two wives?","o":["He has fallen in love with both of them at once","They control their husbands' money","He wants revenge on Page for an old insult","He was dared by the Host"],"a":1},{"q":"Who refuses to carry the letters?","o":["Robin and Bardolph","Pistol and Nym","The Host and Bardolph","Simple and Rugby"],"a":1},{"q":"What job does Bardolph take at the inn?","o":["Cook","Tapster","Stable boy","Porter"],"a":1}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"Mistress Quickly promises Simple she will help Slender, but hides him in a cupboard when Doctor Caius comes home. Caius finds him, learns that Evans is helping Slender woo Anne, and writes Evans a challenge to a duel, since he means to marry Anne himself. Quickly tells him Anne loves him. After he leaves, Fenton visits and Quickly assures him too that Anne loves him, though alone she admits she does not think so.","key":["Quickly promises all three suitors her help: she knows 'Anne's mind' and tells each what he wants to hear.","Caius's jealous rage sets up the comic duel.","Fenton is introduced as the suitor Anne really favours."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Quickly hide Simple?","o":["Under the bed","In a cupboard","Behind a curtain","In a basket"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Caius do when he learns why Simple came?","o":["Writes a challenge to Evans","Beats Simple and throws him out of the house","Writes a love letter to Anne Page","Goes to Page to ask for Anne"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Fenton give Quickly?","o":["A ring","Money","A letter","A song"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Mistress Page reads Falstaff's love letter and is outraged. Mistress Ford arrives with an identical letter, apart from the name. They agree to lead him on until he has pawned his horses, without staining their honour. Pistol tells Ford, and Nym tells Page, that Falstaff is after their wives. Page laughs it off, but Ford is consumed by suspicion. The Host and Shallow invite Page to watch the duel between Caius and Evans, and Ford bribes the Host to introduce him to Falstaff under the name of Brook.","key":["The two identical letters turn the wives from victims into a team.","Page's trust and Ford's jealousy are set side by side.","Ford's disguise as Brook gives the plot its double trick."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is special about the two letters?","o":["They are in verse","They are identical apart from the names","They are unsigned","They ask for money"],"a":1},{"q":"Who tells Ford about Falstaff's plan?","o":["Nym","Pistol","Robin","Mistress Quickly"],"a":1},{"q":"What name does Ford use in disguise?","o":["Brook","Herne","Prat","Hobgoblin"],"a":0}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Falstaff refuses to lend Pistol money. Mistress Quickly brings word that Mistress Ford's husband will be out between ten and eleven, and that Mistress Page also adores him and wants him to send his page. Ford arrives as Brook with a bag of money and says he loves Mistress Ford but cannot win her. He pays Falstaff to seduce her first, so that her virtue will be broken. Falstaff boasts that he already has a meeting with her between ten and eleven and calls her husband a cuckoldly knave. Alone, Ford rages at the 'hell of having a false woman' and plans to catch them.","key":["Pistol's 'the world's mine oyster' shows how everyone around Falstaff lives by their wits.","Ford pays Falstaff to tell him everything: the jealous husband becomes his own spy.","Ford's soliloquy is the play's only near-tragic rage, and it is comic because the audience knows his wife is faithful."],"quiz":[{"q":"When will Mistress Ford's husband be away?","o":["Between eight and nine","Between ten and eleven","At midnight","All day"],"a":1},{"q":"What does 'Brook' ask Falstaff to do?","o":["Lend him money for the wedding","Seduce Mistress Ford","Challenge Ford","Carry a letter"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff call Ford, not knowing who 'Brook' is?","o":["A cuckoldly knave","A good and loyal friend","A wise man","A Welsh goat"],"a":0}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Caius waits in a field for Evans, who does not come. The Host, Shallow, Slender and Page arrive. The Host mocks Caius with words he does not understand. Shallow and Page go ahead to Frogmore, where Evans is waiting, and the Host promises to take Caius by the fields to where Anne Page is feasting.","key":["The Host sent the duellists to different places: he is the trickster here.","Caius's English makes him an easy target for the Host's jokes.","Shallow brags about his fighting youth even while preaching peace."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Evans not come?","o":["He is afraid of the French doctor's rapier and skill","The Host sent him to a different place","He forgot the time and place of the duel","He is ill"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Host tell Caius 'mockwater' means?","o":["Courage","Cowardice","Water","Urine"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the Host promise Caius?","o":["To find him a sword","To take him to Anne Page","To pay him","To arrest Evans"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Evans waits near Frogmore, nervous and singing scraps of a love song to keep his spirits up. Page, Shallow and Slender arrive, then the Host with Caius. The two are parted before they can fight. The Host admits he sent them to different places to keep his doctor and his parson alive. Realising they have been made fools of, Caius and Evans become friends and plan revenge on the Host.","key":["Evans's song shows he is as frightened as Caius.","The Host enjoys his trick: 'Am I politic? Am I subtle? Am I a Machiavel?'","The rivals unite against a common mocker, which sets up the horse trick in Act 4."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Evans do while he waits?","o":["Prays","Sings","Sleeps","Writes a letter"],"a":1},{"q":"Who stops the duel?","o":["Ford and Page together","The Host and the others","Mistress Quickly","Anne"],"a":1},{"q":"What do Caius and Evans decide?","o":["To fight again later today","To be revenged on the Host","To leave Windsor","To woo Anne together"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Ford meets Mistress Page walking with Falstaff's page Robin and becomes sure she and his wife are plotting with Falstaff. When the clock strikes he decides to search his house. He invites Page, Caius and Evans home to see 'a monster'. Page says he favours Slender for Anne and rejects Fenton, the Host praises Fenton's charm, and the Host goes to drink with Falstaff.","key":["Ford reads every innocent sign as proof of betrayal.","Page will not let Fenton tie his fortunes 'with the finger of my substance'.","Ford gathers witnesses: he wants his shame proved in public."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is walking with Mistress Page?","o":["Simple","Robin","William","Rugby"],"a":1},{"q":"Which suitor does Page favour?","o":["Master Fenton","Doctor Caius","Slender","None"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Ford promise to show his guests?","o":["A monster","His wife","A new hawk","Falstaff's letter"],"a":0}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"The wives have servants ready with a laundry basket. Falstaff arrives and woos Mistress Ford with extravagant praise. Robin warns that Mistress Page is coming, so Falstaff hides behind the arras. Mistress Page pretends Ford is on his way with officers. Falstaff jumps into the basket under the dirty linen and is carried out, past Ford, to be dumped in the ditch by the Thames. Ford searches the house and finds nothing. The wives plan another trick, and Page invites everyone to breakfast and bird hunting.","key":["The wives' rehearsed alarm becomes true: Ford really is coming.","Falstaff's own panic makes the trick work: 'Let me see 't! I'll in, I'll in.'","Ford's 'buck' puns show he is obsessed with the horns of a cuckold."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Falstaff hidden?","o":["Behind the arras, then in a laundry basket","Up the chimney","In the oven","Under the bed"],"a":0},{"q":"Where is the basket to be emptied?","o":["Into the castle ditch by the park gate at night","Into a muddy ditch by the Thames","Into a well","At the laundress's house"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Ford find when he searches?","o":["Falstaff","Nothing","A letter","A witch"],"a":1}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"Fenton tells Anne her father thinks he wants only her money. He admits it was his first motive, but says he now loves her for herself. Shallow pushes Slender forward, and Slender talks about his will and lets his uncle do the wooing. Page sends Fenton away. Mistress Page says she will neither help nor hinder Fenton, while Anne begs not to be married to 'yond fool'. Fenton gives Quickly a ring for Anne, and Quickly decides to help all three suitors, but Fenton most.","key":["Fenton is honest about money, which makes his love believable.","Anne's 'what a world of vile ill-favoured faults Looks handsome in three hundred pounds a year' sums up her parents' choices.","Quickly's promise to all three suitors shows how little her help is worth."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Fenton admit to Anne?","o":["He is poor and has spent all he had at court","Her father's wealth first attracted him","He is married","He loves Mistress Page"],"a":1},{"q":"What would Anne rather be than marry Caius?","o":["A nun in a convent far from Windsor","Bowled to death with turnips","Sent to France as a servant to his household","Married to Slender after all"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Fenton give Quickly to pass to Anne?","o":["A letter","A ring","A purse","A glove"],"a":1}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"Falstaff, soaked and furious, describes being thrown in the Thames 'like a barrow of butcher's offal'. Mistress Quickly brings word that Mistress Ford is sorry and that her husband is going bird hunting, so Falstaff should come again between eight and nine. Ford arrives as Brook, and Falstaff describes the basket, the smell and the river in loving detail, and says he is off to see her again. Alone, Ford resolves to search 'impossible places'.","key":["Falstaff's prose turns humiliation into comedy: 'a kind of alacrity in sinking'.","He tells Brook everything, so Ford knows the time of the next meeting.","Ford: 'If I have horns to make one mad, let the proverb go with me: I'll be horn-mad.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"When should Falstaff visit Mistress Ford again?","o":["Between ten and eleven","Between eight and nine","At midnight","Tomorrow evening"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff say his size gives him?","o":["Courage","A kind of alacrity in sinking","Good luck","A loud voice"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Ford do now?","o":["Leave Windsor for good and never return","Search impossible places","Forgive his wife","Challenge Falstaff"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"On a school holiday, Mistress Page asks Sir Hugh to test her son William's Latin. Evans asks him about nouns, cases and pronouns, while Mistress Quickly mishears the Latin words as English rudeness. William does better than his mother expected.","key":["A comic interlude that slows the plot between the two tricks.","Quickly's mishearings ('horum' as 'whore') echo the play's bawdy talk.","It is the only scene in a school: Windsor is a real town of families."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Evans test William on?","o":["Arithmetic","Latin grammar","The catechism","Writing"],"a":1},{"q":"Who keeps interrupting?","o":["Mistress Page herself","Mistress Quickly","Robin","Simple"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Quickly think 'hang-hog' is Latin for?","o":["Pork","Bacon","Ham","Pig"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Falstaff is with Mistress Ford when Mistress Page arrives. Falstaff hides, and Mistress Page says Ford is coming again, talking of the basket. Falstaff refuses the basket, so the wives dress him in the clothes of the fat old woman of Brentford, whom Ford hates as a witch. The servants carry the empty basket to Ford, who tears out the linen while the gentlemen call him mad. Then 'Mother Prat' comes down and Ford beats her out of the house. Evans notices her beard. The wives decide to tell their husbands everything.","key":["The second trick is harsher: Falstaff is beaten, not just soaked.","Mistress Page's couplet states the play's idea: 'Wives may be merry, and yet honest too'.","Ford is shamed in front of his friends as well."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Falstaff disguise himself as?","o":["Mistress Quickly","The fat woman of Brentford","Herne the hunter","A laundress"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Ford search this time?","o":["The chimney and the oven","The laundry basket","The cellar under the house","The garden"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Evans notice about the 'old woman'?","o":["Her limp as she walks out","Her great beard","Her deep and gruff voice","Her great height"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Bardolph tells the Host that some Germans want three of his horses to go and meet a Duke at court. The Host agrees but plans to overcharge them.","key":["A short scene that sets up the Host's loss.","The 'Duke' and the 'Germans' sound suspicious from the start.","It answers Evans and Caius's promise of revenge in 3.1."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the Germans want?","o":["Rooms","Three horses","Wine","A guide"],"a":1},{"q":"Who are they supposedly going to meet?","o":["The King","A Duke","Falstaff","The Queen"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Host plan?","o":["To refuse them rooms at the inn","To overcharge them","To go with them to court","To warn Page"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"The wives have told their husbands. Ford begs his wife's pardon and swears never to suspect her again. Everyone plans a public shaming. Mistress Page tells the tale of Herne the hunter, a ghost with great horns who haunts an oak in Windsor Forest at midnight. Falstaff will be invited to meet them there dressed as Herne; children dressed as fairies will pinch and burn him until he confesses. Page secretly plans for Slender to steal Anne away; Mistress Page plans the same for Caius.","key":["Ford's apology closes his jealousy plot: 'I rather will suspect the sun with cold'.","Herne's oak brings old folk belief into the town comedy.","Both parents use the masque to marry off Anne behind each other's backs."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who tells the story of Herne the hunter?","o":["Master Ford","Sir Hugh Evans","Mistress Page","Mistress Quickly"],"a":2},{"q":"Who will play the fairies?","o":["The servants","Children","The Host's men","The wives"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Page plan secretly?","o":["For Slender to steal Anne away","For Fenton to marry Anne","To arrest Falstaff","To forgive Falstaff"],"a":0}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"Simple comes to ask the 'wise woman of Brentford' about his master's chances with Anne; Falstaff, back in his own clothes, gives him a joking answer. Bardolph arrives: the Germans threw him in the mud and rode off with the horses. Evans and Caius each come to warn the Host that he has been swindled. Alone, Falstaff says if the court heard of his beatings they would melt him down. Mistress Quickly arrives with a new letter from the wives.","key":["The Host is paid back: the warnings from Evans and Caius look like gloating.","Falstaff is at his lowest, 'as crestfallen as a dried pear'.","Yet one more letter draws him in again."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happened to the Host's horses?","o":["They were sold at the fair at Reading","The 'Germans' stole them","They died","Falstaff took them for his flight"],"a":1},{"q":"Who warns the Host?","o":["Ford and Page","Evans and Caius","Pistol and Nym","Simple and Rugby"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Simple want to know from the wise woman?","o":["Whether Slender will marry Anne","Where Falstaff is hiding in the town tonight","Who stole his master's purse at the inn","His own fortune in love"],"a":0}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"Fenton offers the Host a hundred pounds to help him. Anne's letter explains that at the masque her father wants her to go off with Slender, dressed in white, and her mother wants her to go off with Caius, dressed in green. She has agreed to both. Fenton asks the Host to have the vicar waiting at the church between twelve and one to marry them.","key":["The masque now hides three plots: the wives', Page's and Mistress Page's, and a fourth, Anne's.","Anne's quiet obedience to both parents hides her own choice.","The Host, newly swindled, is now on the side of love."],"quiz":[{"q":"What colour does Page want Anne to wear?","o":["Green","White","Red","Blue"],"a":1},{"q":"What colour does Mistress Page want Anne to wear?","o":["Green","White","Black","Gold"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Fenton ask the Host to arrange?","o":["Horses","A vicar to marry them","A disguise","A supper"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Falstaff agrees to the third meeting: 'there is divinity in odd numbers'. To 'Brook' he describes how he was beaten as an old woman and promises that tonight he will be revenged on Ford.","key":["Falstaff still trusts Brook completely.","His humour survives every beating.","The rule of three: the third trick will be the last."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Falstaff hopeful about the third time?","o":["The wives have promised him this time for certain","There is luck in odd numbers","Brook has paid him more money","The Host will help"],"a":1},{"q":"Where will Brook see 'wonders'?","o":["At the Garter Inn, at the stroke of midnight","In the park at Herne's oak","At Ford's house, behind the arras","At church after the service"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Falstaff say he went to Mistress Ford?","o":["Like a poor old man","Like a knight in full armour","Like Herne the hunter","Like a soldier"],"a":0}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Page, Shallow and Slender wait in the castle ditch. Slender explains the password: he will say 'mum' and Anne will answer 'budget'.","key":["The password is a comic touch: Slender trusts it more than the dress.","Page thinks his plan is secret.","'No man means evil but the devil, and we shall know him by his horns.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Slender's password?","o":["Mum","Budget","Nan","Herne"],"a":0},{"q":"Where do they hide?","o":["In a sawpit near the great oak","In the castle ditch","In the church by the castle","At the inn with the Host"],"a":1},{"q":"What colour is Slender looking for?","o":["Green","White","Red","Black"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Mistress Page tells Caius that Anne is in green and to take her straight to the deanery. She admits her husband will be angry. The wives head for the oak.","key":["Mistress Page plots against her husband as well as Falstaff.","'Better a little chiding than a great deal of heartbreak.'","The wives feel no guilt in deceiving a lecher."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where should Caius take Anne?","o":["To Eton, where the vicar waits","To the deanery","To France, on the next ship","To the Garter Inn"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is hiding with lights near the oak?","o":["The husbands, with their swords and lanterns","Nan, the fairies and Evans","The Host and his German guests","Falstaff's men, Pistol and Nym"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Mistress Page think her husband will react?","o":["He will be pleased","He will be angry","He will not notice","He will leave"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Sir Hugh, disguised as a satyr, leads the children dressed as fairies to their hiding place.","key":["Evans directs the children like a schoolmaster.","The tiny scene builds suspense.","Every trick in the play now meets at one oak."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who leads the fairies?","o":["Mistress Quickly","Sir Hugh Evans","Pistol","Anne"],"a":1},{"q":"Where do they hide?","o":["In the pit","In the castle","Up the oak","At the inn"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Evans tell them to wait for?","o":["A trumpet","His watchwords","Midnight bells","Falstaff's cry"],"a":1}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"At midnight Falstaff arrives wearing a buck's head and is greeted by both wives. A noise of horns sends them running. The fairies, led by Quickly as Fairy Queen, Evans as a satyr and Pistol as Hobgoblin, test him with burning tapers, pinch him and sing a song against lust. In the confusion Caius steals a boy in green, Slender a boy in white, and Fenton steals Anne. The husbands and wives reveal themselves; Falstaff admits he has been made an ass and is mocked by all. Slender and Caius arrive, each married to a boy. Fenton and Anne enter married, and Fenton defends their match. Page and his wife accept it, and everyone goes home to laugh by the fire, Falstaff included.","key":["Falstaff's humiliation is public at last: 'I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass.'","The trickers are tricked: both parents lose to Anne and Fenton.","The ending forgives everyone and brings all of Windsor home together."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Falstaff wear?","o":["A buck's head","A woman's dress","A mask","A crown"],"a":0},{"q":"Who does Caius actually marry?","o":["Anne","A boy","Mistress Quickly","Nobody"],"a":1},{"q":"Who marries Anne?","o":["Slender","Caius","Fenton","Nobody"],"a":2}]}},"notes":{"2":"The Star Chamber was a royal court that heard cases of riot and disorder. Shallow threatens to take a poaching quarrel to the highest court in the land.","3":"Slender garbles Latin legal titles: 'Coram' (for quorum), 'Custalorum' (custos rotulorum, keeper of the records) and 'Ratolorum'. He is trying to sound grand on his cousin's behalf.","7":"Luces are pike fish, shown on Shallow's coat of arms. Evans hears 'louses', and Shallow then confuses the joke further with salt fish. Some readers link the luces to the Lucy family of Charlecote, near Stratford.","57":"Falstaff puns on 'Council' (the King's Council) and 'counsel' (keeping a secret).","59":"'Worts' are cabbages: Falstaff mocks Evans's Welsh pronunciation of 'words'.","65":"Nym's catchword is 'humour', which he uses for mood, plan, joke and anything else. Shakespeare is mocking a fashionable word of the 1590s.","74":"Mill-sixpences were coins made by machine; Edward shovel-boards were old shillings used in the game of shove-ha'penny. Slender values his lost coins very precisely.","77":"Pistol speaks in the high-flown style of old ranting tragedies. 'Mountain-foreigner' sneers at Evans as Welsh; a 'latten bilbo' is a sword made of tin.","81":"'Scarlet and John' are Will Scarlet and Little John from the Robin Hood stories; 'Scarlet' also mocks Bardolph's red face.","97":"Songs and Sonnets was a popular collection of love poems (Tottel's Miscellany). Slender wants ready-made lines to woo Anne with.","119":"Slender's slips turn the proverb upside down: he hopes love will 'decrease' and familiarity breed 'contempt'. He means the opposite, which is the joke.","143":"Sackerson was a famous bear at the Paris Garden bear-baiting ring in London.","169":"The Host piles up nicknames: 'bully rook', 'Keiser', 'Pheazar'. His nonsense words and rhymes are part of his personality.","175":"'Hungarian' sounds like 'hungry', and 'wight' means fellow. Pistol mocks Bardolph's new job in grand style.","180":"'Convey' was thieves' slang for steal. A 'fico' is a fig, with a rude gesture.","189":"Falstaff puns on 'about' (around the waist) and 'about' (busy with), and on 'waste' and 'waist'.","198":"Guiana in South America was thought to be full of gold. Walter Raleigh had just written about his voyage there (1596). The East and West Indies were the great sources of trade wealth.","199":"Pandarus was the go-between who brought Troilus and Cressida together; his name gave English the word 'pander'.","201":"A pinnace is a small boat that serves a bigger ship. Falstaff dresses up his small page as a ship sailing to golden shores.","203":"'Gourd and fullam' were kinds of loaded dice; 'high and low' were dice weighted for high or low numbers.","210":"Yellow was the colour of jealousy. Nym plans to fill Ford with suspicion.","242":"'Horn-mad' means furious, like a bull; it also hints at the horns of a cuckold, the play's running joke.","245":"Caius mixes French and English throughout. His muddled words make him a figure of fun, just like Evans.","270":"Caius threatens to castrate Evans: 'stones' meant testicles.","298":"The verses at the end of Falstaff's letter rhyme on the same sound, in a sing-song style: Mistress Page thinks it ridiculous for a man of his age.","309":"James I sold knighthoods so freely after 1603 that it became a joke; this play is earlier, but knighthoods were already mocked as cheap.","310":"The Hundredth Psalm was a solemn hymn; 'Greensleeves' was a well-known love song. Falstaff's fine words and lustful aims fit together no better.","311":"Mistress Page jokes that Falstaff must have the letters printed, with gaps for different names. In Greek myth, giants piled Mount Pelion on Mount Ossa to attack the gods.","314":"'Boarded' is a sea term for attacking a ship, and also meant accosting someone sexually. The wives extend it into a joke about hatches and decks.","327":"Actaeon saw the goddess Diana bathing and was turned into a stag and killed by his own hounds. Because he grew horns, he became a symbol of the cuckold. Ringwood is a hound's name.","329":"The cuckoo lays its eggs in other birds' nests, and its call 'cuckoo' was heard as 'cuckold'. Pistol's warning is a threat that Ford will be made one.","376":"Ford takes the name 'Brook' (spelled 'Broome' in some early printings). Falstaff later puns on brooks that 'o'erflow such liquor'.","389":"'The world's mine oyster' became a proverb. Pistol means he will take what he needs from the world by force, as one prises open an oyster.","392":"Pickt-hatch was a London district known for brothels and thieves.","414":"Quickly's 'canaries' is her muddle for 'quandary': a flutter or state of confusion. Canary was also a sweet wine and a lively dance.","424":"Quickly's 'infection' means affection; she often uses the wrong word. 'Of all loves' means for love's sake.","429":"Pistol talks of Quickly as a ship to be captured: 'fights' were screens raised during a sea battle.","457":"Ford's couplet is a proverb about love: it runs away from those who chase it and chases those who run.","480":"Amaimon, Lucifer and Barbason were names of devils. Ford thinks 'cuckold' is a worse name than any devil's. A 'wittol' was a man who knew his wife was unfaithful and put up with it.","500":"The Host uses fencing terms (foin, punto, stock, reverse, montant) to mock the duellists, and calls Caius 'Aesculapius' and 'Galen' after famous doctors of the ancient world.","502":"'Castalion King Urinal' joins a Spanish name with a joke about doctors examining urine.","511":"'Mockwater' is the Host's invented insult, mocking doctors who examine water (urine). He tells Caius it means courage.","542":"Evans sings a jumbled version of Marlowe's 'Come live with me and be my love', mixed with a line from a psalm about Babylon. His nerves make him muddle the words.","583":"'Gallia and Gaul': the Host's joke, as Gallia means France and Gaul sounds like Wales (Galles in French).","585":"'Machiavel' meant a cunning schemer, after Niccolò Machiavelli. The Host is proud of his trick.","613":"Point-blank twelve score: a cannon shot fired straight at a target 240 yards away.","624":"The 'wild Prince' and Poins are Prince Hal and his friend from the Henry IV plays, in which Falstaff is Hal's companion. Fenton has been part of Falstaff's old world.","650":"An eyas-musket is a young sparrowhawk: a joking name for small, quick Robin. A Jack-a-Lent was a puppet pelted with stones during Lent.","662":"Falstaff quotes a line from Philip Sidney's sonnet sequence Astrophil and Stella.","666":"Fashionable headdresses: the 'ship-tire' was shaped like a ship, and 'Venetian admittance' means in the latest Venetian style.","670":"Bucklersbury was a London street of herb sellers, sweet-smelling in summer ('simple-time').","672":"The Counter was a debtors' prison in London. Falstaff, always in debt, hates the sight of it.","708":"'Buck' meant laundry (buck-washing) and a male deer. Ford hears the horns of a cuckold in the word.","816":"Falstaff jokes that his weight gives him 'a kind of alacrity in sinking'. 'Mummy' was a medicine made from dead bodies.","830":"Quickly means 'direction' but says 'erection'. Her slips are often accidentally rude.","857":"A bilbo is a flexible Spanish sword that could be bent from hilt to point. A bell-wether is the leading ram of a flock, wearing a bell, and so has horns.","863":"Ford ends with the proverb 'horn-mad': mad with anger, and mad because he thinks he has a cuckold's horns.","880":"William's Latin lesson follows William Lily's Latin Grammar, the book used in every Elizabethan grammar school, including Stratford's.","905":"Quickly hears 'horum' as 'whore' and 'genitive' as 'Jenny's'. Later 'hic and hac' sounds to her like hiccups and hacking.","912":"'Preeches' is Evans's 'breeches': a boy who forgot his Latin would be whipped with his breeches down.","933":"'Lunes' are fits of madness. 'Peer out' is what children shouted to snails to make them put out their horns.","948":"Ford has an 'abstract', a list, of every hiding place in his house: his jealousy has become a system.","955":"A thrummed hat was made of rough, tufted wool. The fat woman of Brentford was a real figure in an old ballad.","967":"Mistress Page's rhyming couplet gives the play its title idea: the wives can be merry and still be honest. 'Still swine eats all the draff' means it is the quiet ones who get away with things.","1004":"Ford accuses the old woman of witchcraft: charms, spells and 'the figure' (astrological charts). Fear of witches was real in Elizabethan England.","1013":"Evans's 'great peard' gives the disguise away. Bearded witches were a stock image.","1021":"'Fee-simple, with fine and recovery' are legal terms for owning land outright. Mistress Page jokes that the devil would need to own Falstaff completely to make him try again.","1032":"The 'Germans' and the Duke may refer to a real German duke who visited Windsor in 1592 and was said to have left without paying.","1046":"Herne the hunter was a local Windsor legend. This is the first written mention of it; Shakespeare may have made up some of the details.","1050":"Urchins were goblins (and hedgehogs). Fairies were believed to punish lazy maids and lustful men by pinching them.","1070":"The story of the Prodigal Son, who wasted his fortune and came home in rags, was a favourite subject for painted cloths in inns.","1097":"Falstaff says he has learned wisdom from the 'wise woman', meaning his beating.","1101":"Doctor Faustus, in Marlowe's play, sold his soul to the devil. The 'Germans' vanish like devils.","1106":"Reading, Maidenhead and Colnbrook were towns on the road from London to Windsor.","1115":"Primero was a card game. Falstaff says his bad luck began when he cheated at it.","1138":"'There is divinity in odd numbers': the belief that odd numbers, especially three, were lucky.","1145":"'Life is a shuttle' comes from the Book of Job: our days pass faster than a weaver's shuttle. Goliath's spear was 'like a weaver's beam'.","1150":"'Mum' and 'budget' together made the phrase 'mumbudget', meaning 'keep quiet'.","1175":"Jove took the shape of a bull to carry off Europa, and of a swan to seduce Leda. Falstaff compares his horns to the god's disguises. To 'piss my tallow' means to sweat away fat, as deer do in the mating season.","1178":"Potatoes (sweet potatoes), kissing-comfits and eringoes (sea holly) were all thought to stir desire.","1181":"A 'bribed buck' is a stolen deer, cut up and shared. Falstaff gives his shoulders to the keeper and his horns to the husbands.","1195":"The Fairy Queen's speech praises Windsor Castle and the Order of the Garter, whose motto is 'Honi soit qui mal y pense' (shame on him who thinks evil of it). The play may have been written for a Garter feast in 1597.","1222":"Evans's 'seese' and 'putter' are cheese and butter, the Welsh being famous for loving cheese.","1230":"Job's wife told him to 'curse God, and die': a byword for a wicked wife.","1234":"A posset was a hot drink of milk curdled with wine or ale.","1258":"Ford's couplet echoes a proverb: 'Marriage and hanging go by destiny.'","1263":"Ford's last joke: 'Master Brook' (Ford himself) will sleep with Mistress Ford tonight, so Falstaff's promise comes true in a way he never meant."},"gloss":{"armigero":"a gentleman entitled to a coat of arms","luces":"pike fish, shown on a coat of arms","quarter":"add another family's arms to your own","vizaments":"advisements, careful thought (Evans's Welsh)","pribbles":"petty squabbles (Evans's word)","prabbles":"brawls, quarrels (Evans's word)","fallow":"pale yellow-brown","cony-catching":"swindling, cheating","mephostophilus":"Mephistopheles, a devil","pauca":"few (Latin), as in 'few words'","groats":"coins worth four pence","latten":"a tin-like metal","bilbo":"a Spanish sword, flexible steel","labras":"lips (mock Spanish)","avised":"advised, careful","fap":"drunk","cashiered":"dismissed; also robbed of cash","forsooth":"truly, indeed","veneys":"bouts in fencing","bully":"fine fellow (friendly)","rook":"fellow; also a cheat","cashier":"dismiss","tapster":"barman who draws beer","jerkin":"a short close-fitting jacket","spigot":"tap of a barrel","tinderbox":"box for starting fires; a hot-tempered man","fico":"a fig, a rude gesture of contempt","kibes":"sore heels, chilblains","ken":"know","wight":"person, fellow","oeillades":"amorous glances","angels":"gold coins stamped with the archangel Michael","exchequers":"treasuries","pinnace":"a small boat serving a larger ship","avaunt":"go away","tester":"a sixpence","welkin":"the sky","eke":"also","varlet":"rogue, rascal","casement":"window","posset":"a hot drink of milk curdled with wine or ale","breed-bate":"troublemaker","shent":"scolded, disgraced","closet":"a small private room or cupboard","simples":"medicinal herbs","jackanape":"monkey, cheeky fellow","jackanapes":"monkey, cheeky fellow","allicholy":"melancholy (Quickly's muddle)","precisian":"a strict, puritanical adviser","sack":"sweet white wine from Spain","gallant":"a fashionable young man","herod":"a ranting tyrant (from the mystery plays)","unweighed":"thoughtless","puddings":"sausages","chariness":"careful guarding","curtal":"with its tail cut short","gallimaufry":"a jumble, a mixed dish","perpend":"consider","cataian":"a scoundrel (literally a man from Cathay)","cuckold":"a man whose wife is unfaithful","cuckoldly":"like a cuckold","wittol":"a man who knows of and tolerates his wife's unfaithfulness","wittolly":"like a wittol","pottle":"a half-gallon pot","cavaliero":"cavalier, gentleman","stoccadoes":"fencing thrusts","gemini":"pair of twins","pickt-hatch":"a London district of brothels","ensconce":"hide, shelter","goodwife":"a married woman of modest rank","canaries":"a flutter (Quickly's word for quandary)","alligant":"elegant (Quickly's muddle)","frampold":"quarrelsome, troubled","fartuous":"virtuous (Quickly's muddle)","nay-word":"password","punk":"prostitute","importuned":"pleaded with, pressed","edifice":"building","epicurean":"devoted to pleasure","aqua-vitae":"spirits, strong drink","foin":"thrust (fencing)","punto":"a point, a thrust (fencing)","montant":"an upward thrust (fencing)","aesculapius":"the Roman god of medicine","clapper-claw":"thrash, maul","costard":"head (literally a large apple)","urinals":"glass flasks for examining urine","madrigals":"part-songs for several voices","stile":"steps over a fence","doublet":"a close-fitting jacket","hose":"breeches, tight trousers","rheumatic":"damp, causing colds","cogging":"cheating","scall":"scabby, scurvy","vlouting-stog":"flouting-stock, laughing stock (Evans's Welsh)","weathercock":"a showy follower; something that turns with the wind","actaeon":"a hunter turned into a stag; so a cuckold","pipe-wine":"wine from the cask; also a beating to music","buck-basket":"laundry basket","whitsters":"bleachers of linen","cowl-staff":"pole used by two men to carry a basket","drumble":"dawdle","eyas-musket":"a young male sparrowhawk","jack-a-lent":"a puppet pelted with stones in Lent","pumpion":"pumpkin","arras":"tapestry hanging on a wall","farthingale":"a hooped petticoat","lime-kiln":"a furnace for making lime, notorious for its smell","cog":"cheat, flatter","prate":"chatter","conveyance":"way of escape; also trickery","bucking":"washing in lye","mummy":"a medicine made from embalmed bodies; a mass of flesh","offal":"waste parts of a slaughtered animal","alacrity":"eagerness, readiness","cornuto":"cuckold (Italian, 'horned')","bell-wether":"the leading ram of a flock, with a bell","accidence":"the basics of grammar","caret":"is missing (Latin)","lunes":"fits of madness","draff":"pig swill","quean":"hussy","cozening":"cheating","cozened":"cheated","cozenage":"cheating","cozeners":"cheats","daubery":"trickery","runnion":"a scabby woman, a term of abuse","cudgel":"a short thick stick","hallowed":"blessed","fee-simple":"absolute ownership","leman":"lover","truckle-bed":"a low bed on wheels kept under a larger one","standing-bed":"a large bed with posts","anthropophaginian":"a cannibal","bohemian-tartar":"a wild foreigner","clerkly":"scholarly, clever","primero":"a card game","crestfallen":"drooping, humiliated","dexterity":"skill, quickness","vizards":"masks","urchins":"goblins","oafs":"elf children","diffused":"wild, disordered","tapers":"wax candles","deanery":"the dean's house by the church","mince":"walk with short, dainty steps","scut":"a deer's short tail","eringoes":"candied sea holly root, thought to stir desire","kissing-comfits":"perfumed sweets for sweetening the breath","oyes":"hear ye, a crier's call for silence","unraked":"not banked down for the night","sluttery":"slovenliness","bilberry":"a small dark-blue berry","charactery":"writing","o'erlooked":"bewitched by the evil eye","hodge-pudding":"a pudding of mixed ingredients","metheglins":"Welsh spiced mead","frieze":"rough Welsh woollen cloth","cox-comb":"fool's cap","plummet":"a lead weight; Falstaff feels sunk","lubberly":"big and clumsy","swinged":"thrashed","evitate":"avoid","eschewed":"avoided","budget":"a leather bag; with 'mum', the word for keeping quiet"}};
const PARTS={"298":[[0,1,"What, did I escape love letters in the springtime of my beauty, only to become a target for them now? Let me see."],[2,8,"(Reads) \"Don't ask me the reason why I love you, for though Love may use Reason as his strict adviser, he never takes him as his counsellor. You are not young, and neither am I. Well then, there's a bond between us. You are merry, and so am I. Ha, ha, there's more of a bond. You love sack, and so do I. Could you want a better bond? Let it be enough for you, Mistress Page, at least if a soldier's love is enough, that I love you. I won't say 'pity me', that is not a soldierly phrase, but I say love me."],[9,15,"Signed, your own true knight, by day or night, or any kind of light, with all his might, for you to fight, John Falstaff.\""],[16,21,"What a ranting tyrant this is! Oh wicked, wicked world! A man almost worn to pieces with age, showing off as a young lover! What careless behaviour has this Dutch drunkard picked out of my conversation, in the devil's name, that he dares try me like this? Why, he's barely been in my company three times! What could I have said to him? I was sparing with my jokes then. Heaven forgive me!"],[22,24,"Why, I'll bring a bill before Parliament to have men abolished. How shall I get my revenge on him? Because revenge I will have, as surely as his guts are made of sausages."]],"310":[[0,1,"We're wasting time. Here, read it, read it. See how I could be made a knight. I'll think worse of fat men as long as I have eyes to tell men apart."],[2,6,"And yet he never swore; he praised women's modesty, and gave such proper, well-mannered criticism of all bad behaviour that I would have sworn his nature matched his words. But they go together no better than the Hundredth Psalm sung to the tune of \"Greensleeves\"."],[7,10,"What storm, I wonder, washed this whale, with so many barrels of oil in his belly, ashore at Windsor? How shall I get my revenge on him? I think the best way would be to keep him hoping until the wicked fire of lust has melted him in his own fat. Have you ever heard anything like it?"]],"392":[[0,2,"That was only fair, you rogue, only fair. Do you think I'll risk my soul for nothing? In a word, stop hanging around me; I am not your gallows. Go off, with a short knife for cutting purses in a crowd, to your manor in the brothel district, go."],[3,10,"You won't carry a letter for me, you rogue? You stand on your honour! Why, you limitless lowlife, it is as much as I can do to keep my own honour exact. Yes, yes, even I, leaving the fear of God on one side and hiding my honour behind my needs, sometimes have to cheat, to dodge and to steal; and yet you, rogue, want to shelter your rags, your wildcat looks, your tavern talk and your loud bullying oaths behind your honour! You won't do it! You!"]],"414":[[0,3,"Well, this is the long and short of it: you have put her in such a flutter, it's amazing. The best courtier of them all, when the court was at Windsor, could never have got her in such a flutter."],[4,9,"And yet there have been knights and lords and gentlemen, with their coaches, I promise you, coach after coach, letter after letter, gift after gift, smelling so sweet, all musk, and rustling, I promise you, in silk and gold, with such elegant words, and such wine and sugar of the finest and best, that would have won any woman's heart; and I promise you, they could never get so much as a wink out of her."],[10,13,"I had twenty gold angels given me myself this morning, but I scorn angels of that kind, as they say, unless it's honestly. And I promise you, they could never get her to so much as sip from a cup with the proudest of them. And there have been earls; no, what's more, royal guards; but, I promise you, it's all the same to her."]],"457":[[0,4,"I have loved her for a long time and, I swear to you, spent a great deal on her. I have followed her with devoted attention, seized every chance to meet her, paid for every small occasion that might give me even a stingy glimpse of her, and not only bought her many presents but paid many people generously to find out what she would like to be given."],[5,9,"In short, I have chased her as love has chased me, which is on the wings of every opportunity. But whatever I have deserved, in my feelings or my spending, I am sure I have received no reward, unless experience is a jewel. I have bought that at an enormous price, and it has taught me to say this:"],[10,11,"Love flies like a shadow when real wealth pursues it, chasing what runs away and running from what chases it."]],"480":[[0,6,"What a damned, pleasure-loving rascal this is! My heart is ready to burst with fury. Who says this is careless jealousy? My wife has sent to him, the hour is fixed, the deal is done. Who would have thought it? See the hell of having an unfaithful wife: my bed will be abused, my money chests ransacked, my reputation gnawed at; and I will not only suffer this vile wrong but be called disgusting names, and by the very man who wrongs me."],[7,9,"Names, titles! Amaimon sounds fine; Lucifer, fine; Barbason, fine; yet they are devils' titles, the names of fiends. But cuckold? Willing cuckold? Cuckold? The devil himself has no such name."],[10,15,"Page is an ass, an overconfident ass; he will trust his wife, he will not be jealous. I would sooner trust a Dutchman with my butter, Parson Hugh the Welshman with my cheese, an Irishman with my whisky bottle, or a thief to walk my horse, than trust my wife alone with herself. Then she plots, then she broods, then she schemes; and what women think in their hearts they can do, they will break their hearts to do."],[16,18,"God be praised for my jealousy! Eleven o'clock is the hour. I will stop this, catch my wife, take revenge on Falstaff and laugh at Page. I'll get to it. Better three hours too early than a minute too late. Shame, shame, shame! Cuckold, cuckold, cuckold!"]],"613":[[0,3,"Has Page any brains? Has he any eyes? Has he any power of thought? Surely they are asleep; he has no use for them. Why, this boy would carry a letter twenty miles as easily as a cannon shoots point-blank at two hundred and forty yards. He feeds his wife's inclinations; he gives her folly room and opportunity."],[4,6,"And now she's going to my wife, with Falstaff's boy. A man can hear this storm coming in the wind. And Falstaff's boy with her! Fine plots they have laid, and our rebellious wives share their damnation together."],[7,10,"Well, I will catch him, then torment my wife, pull the borrowed veil of modesty off the so-seemingly virtuous Mistress Page, and expose Page himself as an overconfident and willing cuckold, and all my neighbours will applaud these violent actions."],[11,13,"(Clock strikes) The clock gives me my cue, and my certainty tells me to search. I'll find Falstaff there. I'll be praised for this rather than mocked, because it is as certain as the earth is solid that Falstaff is there. I'll go."]],"816":[[0,3,"Have I lived to be carried in a basket like a barrowful of butcher's scraps and thrown in the Thames? Well, if I'm served another trick like that, I'll have my brains taken out and buttered and give them to a dog as a New Year's present."],[4,7,"By God's blood, the rogues tipped me into the river with as little pity as if they were drowning a blind bitch's puppies, fifteen in the litter; and you can tell by my size that I have a sort of talent for sinking. If the bottom were as deep as hell, I'd go down."],[8,10,"I would have drowned if the shore hadn't been sloping and shallow, a death I loathe, because water makes a man swell, and what a sight I would have been swollen! I'd have been a mountain of mummified flesh."]],"857":[[0,3,"Now, you'll hear, Master Brook, what I have suffered trying to lead this woman astray for your benefit. Crammed into the basket like that, I was carried by a couple of Ford's servants, his farmhands, whom their mistress called to take me as dirty clothes to Datchet Lane."],[4,8,"They lifted me onto their shoulders and met their jealous rogue of a master at the door, who asked them once or twice what they had in their basket. I shook with fear that the lunatic would search it; but Fate, having decided he would be a cuckold, held back his hand. Well, off he went to search, and off I went as dirty clothes."],[9,14,"But listen to what followed, Master Brook. I suffered the agonies of three separate deaths: first, the unbearable fright of being caught by a jealous, rotten old ram; next, being bent like a good sword into the round of a bucket, hilt to point, heel to head; and then being sealed in, like a strong brew in a still, with stinking clothes fermenting in their own grease."],[15,19,"Think of that, a man of my build, think of that, who melts in the heat like butter, a man always dissolving and thawing. It was a miracle I didn't suffocate. And at the height of this bath, when I was more than half stewed in grease like a Dutch dish, to be thrown into the Thames and cooled, glowing hot, in the waves, like a horseshoe! Think of that, hissing hot; think of that, Master Brook."]],"863":[[0,3,"Hmm! Ha! Is this a vision? Is this a dream? Am I asleep? Master Ford, wake up; wake up, Master Ford! There's a hole in your best coat, Master Ford. This is what it is to be married; this is what it is to have linen and laundry baskets!"],[4,7,"Well, I'll announce myself for what I am. Now I'll catch the lecher. He's at my house. He can't escape me. It's impossible. He can't creep into a halfpenny purse, or into a pepper pot. But in case the devil who guides him helps him, I'll search impossible places."],[8,9,"Though I can't avoid being what I am, being what I don't want to be won't make me meek. If I have horns to drive a man mad, let the proverb fit me: I'll be horn-mad."]],"1046":[[0,6,"There is an old story that Herne the hunter, once a keeper here in Windsor Forest, walks round an oak tree all winter at dead of midnight, with great ragged horns; and there he withers the tree, bewitches the cattle, makes milking cows give blood, and rattles a chain in a hideous and dreadful way."],[7,10,"You have heard of such a spirit, and you know well that superstitious, foolish old people accepted this tale of Herne the hunter as true and handed it down to our times."]],"1050":[[0,5,"We've thought about that too, and this is it: we'll dress my daughter Nan Page, my little son, and three or four more of their size as goblins, elves and fairies in green and white, with circles of wax candles on their heads and rattles in their hands."],[6,9,"Suddenly, just as Falstaff, she and I have met, let them all rush out together from a sawpit, singing some wild song. When we see them, we two will run off in great alarm."],[10,14,"Then let them all make a circle round him and, like fairies, pinch the filthy knight, and ask him why, at the hour of the fairies' revels, he dares to tread their sacred paths in such an unholy shape."]],"1130":[[0,10,"From time to time I have told you about my deep love for lovely Anne Page, who has returned my affection as fully as I could wish, as far as she is free to choose for herself. I have a letter from her with contents that will amaze you. The fun of it is so mixed up with my own business that neither can be explained without the other, and fat Falstaff has a big part in it. I'll show you the whole outline of the joke. Listen, good landlord."],[11,17,"Tonight at Herne's oak, between twelve and one, my sweet Nan is to play the Fairy Queen (the reason is in here). In that disguise, while the other jokes are in full swing, her father has ordered her to slip away with Slender and marry him straight away at Eton. She has agreed."],[18,25,"Now, sir, her mother, who is firmly against that match and set on Doctor Caius, has arranged that he will also spirit her away while the others are busy with their sport, and marry her at once at the deanery, where a priest will be waiting. To this plan of her mother's, she, seeming obedient, has also given her promise to the doctor."],[26,36,"So this is how it stands. Her father means her to be dressed all in white, and in that costume, when Slender sees his chance to take her by the hand and tell her to come, she will go with him. Her mother intends, to mark her out better for the doctor (since they must all be masked and disguised), that she will be dressed loosely and prettily in green, with ribbons streaming round her head; and when the doctor sees his moment, he is to squeeze her hand, and at that signal the girl has agreed to go with him."]],"1145":[[0,6,"I went to her, Master Brook, as you see me, like a poor old man; but I came away from her, Master Brook, like a poor old woman. That rogue Ford, her husband, has the finest mad devil of jealousy in him, Master Brook, that ever ruled a frenzy. I tell you, he beat me badly while I was dressed as a woman; because as a man, Master Brook, I wouldn't be afraid of Goliath with his spear like a weaver's beam, since I also know that life is as quick as a weaver's shuttle."],[7,11,"I'm in a hurry. Come with me; I'll tell you everything, Master Brook. Since I was a boy plucking geese, playing truant and whipping tops, I never knew what it was to be beaten until lately. Follow me; I'll tell you strange things about this rogue Ford, on whom I'll have my revenge tonight, and I'll hand his wife over to you. Follow me. Strange things are afoot, Master Brook! Follow."]],"1175":[[0,4,"The Windsor bell has struck twelve; the moment is coming. Now may the hot-blooded gods help me! Remember, Jove, you became a bull for your Europa; love gave you horns. Oh, powerful love, which in some ways turns a beast into a man, and in others a man into a beast!"],[5,7,"You were also a swan, Jupiter, for love of Leda. Oh, all-powerful love, how close the god came to looking like a goose! A sin committed first in the shape of a beast, oh Jove, a beastly sin! And then another sin in the shape of a bird; think about it, Jove, a fowl sin!"],[8,10,"When gods are on heat, what are poor men to do? As for me, here I am, a Windsor stag, and the fattest in the forest, I think. Send me a cool mating season, Jove, or who can blame me for sweating off my fat? Who's coming? My doe?"]],"1195":[[0,9,"Off you go, off you go! Search Windsor Castle, elves, inside and out. Scatter good luck, goblins, in every sacred room, so that it may stand until Judgement Day in a condition as sound as it is fitting, worthy of its owner and its owner worthy of it. Make sure you polish the stalls of the Order with balm and every precious flower. May every fair stall, coat of arms and crest, with its loyal emblem, be blessed for ever!"],[10,18,"And every night, meadow fairies, make sure you sing in a ring like the circle of the Garter. Let the mark it leaves in the grass be green, fresher and more fertile than the whole field around; and write \"Honi soit qui mal y pense\", the Garter's motto, in emerald tufts and purple, blue and white flowers, like sapphires, pearls and rich embroidery fastened below the bending knee of noble knighthood. Fairies use flowers for their writing."],[19,21,"Away, scatter! But until one o'clock let us not forget our customary dance around the oak of Herne the hunter."]],"1205":[[0,5,"Shame on sinful fancies! Shame on lust and lechery! Lust is just a bloody fire, kindled by unchaste desire, fed in the heart, whose flames rise as thoughts blow on them, higher and higher."],[6,9,"Pinch him, fairies, all together; pinch him for his villainy. Pinch him and burn him and turn him about, till candles and starlight and moonlight are out."]],"1257":[[0,4,"You are bewildering her. Hear the truth of it. You would have married her most shamefully, to men she could never match in love. The truth is that she and I, engaged long ago, are now so firmly bound that nothing can separate us."],[5,10,"The offence she has committed is a holy one, and this deceit no longer deserves to be called cunning, disobedience or lack of duty, since by it she avoids and escapes a thousand unholy, cursed hours that a forced marriage would have brought her."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"honesty":["Merry and honest wives","The wives' fun, their faithfulness, and the proof that wives may be merry and yet honest too."],"jealousy":["Jealousy","Ford's suspicion, his searches and disguise, and Page's trust set against it."],"money":["Money and greed","Falstaff's hunt for the husbands' purses, Anne's dowry, and money as the measure of marriage."],"desire":["Desire and the body","Lust, fat, age and the punishment of the flesh, from the letters to the fairies' pinches."],"trickery":["Tricks and disguise","Letters, baskets, dresses, false names, fake Germans and fairy costumes: everyone plays a trick on someone."],"marriage":["Love and marriage","Anne's three suitors, her parents' plans and the match she makes for herself."],"language":["Language and class","Welsh, French, Pistol's rant, Nym's humours, Quickly's muddles and the mocking of 'fritters of English'."],"shame":["Shame and forgiveness","Public humiliation, apology and the final invitation to laugh it all off by the fire."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"SHALLOW","text":"If he were twenty Sir John Falstaffs, he shall not abuse Robert Shallow, esquire.","themes":["language","shame"],"note":"Shallow's pride in his title opens the play: Falstaff is already a man who offends the respectable."},{"id":"21","who":"EVANS","text":"seven hundred pounds of moneys, and gold, and silver, is her grandsire upon his death's-bed","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Anne is introduced by her fortune before her face: marriage in Windsor begins with money."},{"id":"25","who":"EVANS","text":"Seven hundred pounds, and possibilities, is goot gifts.","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Evans turns 'good gifts' (good qualities) into cash, a joke that sums up how the suitors see Anne."},{"id":"55","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I will answer it straight: I have done all this. That is now answered.","themes":["shame"],"note":"Falstaff's cheek: he admits everything and feels no shame at all, at least for now."},{"id":"119","who":"SLENDER","text":"I hope upon familiarity will grow more contempt.","themes":["marriage","language"],"note":"Slender turns the proverb upside down without noticing: a perfect picture of a loveless match."},{"id":"119","who":"SLENDER","text":"That I am freely dissolved, and dissolutely.","themes":["language","marriage"],"note":"He means 'resolved, and resolutely'. His words dissolve the very marriage he agrees to."},{"id":"143","who":"SLENDER","text":"I have seen Sackerson loose twenty times, and have taken him by the chain.","themes":["language","marriage"],"note":"Slender's idea of wooing is boasting about bears, which shows he has no idea how to talk to Anne."},{"id":"178","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"His filching was like an unskilful singer, he kept not time.","themes":["trickery","language"],"note":"Falstaff judges theft as an art: being caught is the only real crime."},{"id":"180","who":"PISTOL","text":"\"Convey,\" the wise it call. \"Steal?\" Foh! A fico for the phrase!","themes":["language","trickery"],"note":"Pistol prefers a polite word for stealing: language covers up what people really do."},{"id":"183","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"There is no remedy, I must cony-catch, I must shift.","themes":["money","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff is broke, and his whole plot comes from need, not love."},{"id":"189","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Indeed, I am in the waist two yards about, but I am now about no waste; I am about thrift.","themes":["money","desire","language"],"note":"His size and his money troubles are joined in a pun; his body is the joke of the play."},{"id":"189","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"the hardest voice of her behaviour, to be Englished rightly, is \"I am Sir John Falstaff's.\"","themes":["desire","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff 'translates' ordinary friendliness into a sexual invitation: his vanity misreads everyone."},{"id":"192","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"the report goes she has all the rule of her husband's purse.","themes":["money"],"note":"The real attraction is plain: Mistress Ford controls the money."},{"id":"198","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"they shall be my East and West Indies, and I will trade to them both.","themes":["money","desire"],"note":"The wives become colonies to be exploited: love talk turns into trade talk."},{"id":"200","who":"NYM","text":"I will keep the 'haviour of reputation.","themes":["honesty","language"],"note":"Even Falstaff's thieving follower will not carry the letters: the plot is doomed from the start."},{"id":"210","who":"NYM","text":"I will possess him with yellowness, for the revolt of mine is dangerous.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Yellow was the colour of jealousy. Nym plants the seed that grows in Ford."},{"id":"221","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","text":"His worst fault is that he is given to prayer; he is something peevish that way","themes":["language"],"note":"Quickly's comic logic praises Rugby by naming a virtue as his fault."},{"id":"277","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","text":"Never a woman in Windsor knows more of Anne's mind than I do","themes":["marriage","trickery"],"note":"Quickly promises every suitor she knows Anne's mind, but nobody, it turns out, really does."},{"id":"298","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Why, I'll exhibit a bill in the parliament for the putting down of men.","themes":["honesty","shame"],"note":"Mistress Page's comic fury: one man's insult becomes a war on all men."},{"id":"298","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"For revenged I will be, as sure as his guts are made of puddings.","themes":["shame","desire"],"note":"Revenge starts at once, and it is aimed at Falstaff's body."},{"id":"310","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"I shall think the worse of fat men as long as I have an eye to make difference of men's liking.","themes":["desire"],"note":"Falstaff's fat becomes a moral sign: his greed is written on his body."},{"id":"310","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"till the wicked fire of lust have melted him in his own grease.","themes":["desire","shame"],"note":"The punishment fits the fat: the wives plan to melt him down."},{"id":"311","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Letter for letter, but that the name of Page and Ford differs!","themes":["trickery","honesty"],"note":"The identical letters expose Falstaff's laziness and make the two wives allies."},{"id":"311","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"I will find you twenty lascivious turtles ere one chaste man.","themes":["honesty","desire"],"note":"Turtle doves were symbols of faithfulness; Mistress Page turns the old joke about women around on men."},{"id":"315","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Let's be revenged on him. Let's appoint him a meeting, give him a show of comfort in his suit","themes":["trickery","shame"],"note":"The wives' plan: lead him on until he is ruined."},{"id":"316","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"I will consent to act any villainy against him that may not sully the chariness of our honesty.","themes":["honesty","trickery"],"note":"Mistress Ford sets the rule for the whole plot: tricks are fine, unfaithfulness is not."},{"id":"316","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"O, that my husband saw this letter! It would give eternal food to his jealousy.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Ford's jealousy is known before we meet him."},{"id":"317","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"He's as far from jealousy as I am from giving him cause","themes":["jealousy","honesty"],"note":"Page's trust and his wife's honesty match: a picture of a healthy marriage."},{"id":"323","who":"PISTOL","text":"Hope is a curtal dog in some affairs. Sir John affects thy wife.","themes":["jealousy","language"],"note":"Pistol's ranting warning lands in Ford's ear."},{"id":"327","who":"PISTOL","text":"Prevent, or go thou like Sir Actaeon he, With Ringwood at thy heels.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Actaeon, the man turned stag, introduces the cuckold's horns, which return at Herne's oak."},{"id":"361","who":"PAGE","text":"If he should intend this voyage toward my wife, I would turn her loose to him","themes":["jealousy","honesty"],"note":"Page trusts his wife completely: the opposite of Ford."},{"id":"362","who":"FORD","text":"I do not misdoubt my wife, but I would be loath to turn them together. A man may be too confident.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Ford says he trusts her and then shows he does not."},{"id":"384","who":"FORD","text":"If I find her honest, I lose not my labour. If she be otherwise, 'tis labour well bestowed.","themes":["jealousy","trickery"],"note":"Ford cannot lose: his spying will satisfy him either way."},{"id":"389","who":"PISTOL","text":"Why then, the world's mine oyster, Which I with sword will open.","themes":["money","language"],"note":"The play's most famous line: Pistol will take by force what he cannot borrow."},{"id":"392","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I myself sometimes, leaving the fear of God on the left hand, and hiding mine honour in my necessity, am fain to shuffle, to hedge, and to lurch","themes":["money","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff admits his honour gives way to his needs, while scolding Pistol for having any."},{"id":"414","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","text":"you have brought her into such a canaries as 'tis wonderful.","themes":["language","trickery"],"note":"Quickly's muddled words make her messages funnier and more flattering."},{"id":"424","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","text":"Never a wife in Windsor leads a better life than she does.","themes":["marriage","honesty"],"note":"Quickly's description of the Pages' marriage, invented to tempt Falstaff, is also true."},{"id":"431","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Let them say 'tis grossly done; so it be fairly done, no matter.","themes":["desire","money"],"note":"Falstaff's motto: crude methods are fine if they pay."},{"id":"438","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Such Brooks are welcome to me, that o'erflow such liquor.","themes":["trickery","money"],"note":"Falstaff welcomes 'Brook' because he brings drink and money; the name is part of the trick."},{"id":"447","who":"FORD","text":"for they say, if money go before, all ways do lie open.","themes":["money","trickery"],"note":"Ford uses money to open Falstaff, just as Falstaff hopes to use women to open purses."},{"id":"448","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Money is a good soldier, sir, and will on.","themes":["money"],"note":"Falstaff's quick reply shows what really moves him."},{"id":"457","who":"FORD","text":"Love like a shadow flies when substance love pursues, Pursuing that that flies, and flying what pursues.","themes":["desire","language"],"note":"Ford's invented story of unrequited love is told in a neat rhyming couplet: a lie in polished form."},{"id":"463","who":"FORD","text":"Like a fair house built on another man's ground","themes":["marriage","trickery"],"note":"Ford's image of building on another man's land is the play's picture of adultery."},{"id":"467","who":"FORD","text":"Use your art of wooing, win her to consent to you.","themes":["jealousy","trickery"],"note":"The jealous husband pays a man to seduce his own wife, to test her."},{"id":"476","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I will use her as the key of the cuckoldly rogue's coffer, and there's my harvest-home.","themes":["money","desire"],"note":"Falstaff tells the husband himself that he wants the wife only for the money."},{"id":"480","who":"FORD","text":"See the hell of having a false woman","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Ford's soliloquy is full of torment, though his wife has done nothing."},{"id":"480","who":"FORD","text":"God be praised for my jealousy!","themes":["jealousy","shame"],"note":"The jealous man thanks God for his sickness: a line that is both funny and painful."},{"id":"480","who":"FORD","text":"Better three hours too soon than a minute too late.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Ford's haste becomes a proverb."},{"id":"480","who":"FORD","text":"Fie, fie, fie! Cuckold, cuckold, cuckold!","themes":["jealousy","shame"],"note":"The word that Ford fears most is the one he cannot stop saying."},{"id":"500","who":"HOST","text":"Is he dead, bully stale? Is he dead?","themes":["language","trickery"],"note":"The Host mocks Caius with nicknames he does not understand."},{"id":"585","who":"HOST","text":"Am I politic? Am I subtle? Am I a Machiavel?","themes":["trickery"],"note":"The Host is proud of his own trick, and is tricked in turn."},{"id":"585","who":"HOST","text":"Shall I lose my parson, my priest, my Sir Hugh? No, he gives me the proverbs and the no-verbs.","themes":["language"],"note":"The Host values the foreigners for their entertaining English."},{"id":"591","who":"EVANS","text":"let us knog our prains together to be revenge on this same scall, scurvy, cogging companion, the host of the Garter.","themes":["trickery","shame","language"],"note":"The rivals make peace by uniting against the man who laughed at them."},{"id":"613","who":"FORD","text":"Good plots they are laid, and our revolted wives share damnation together.","themes":["jealousy","honesty"],"note":"Ford is right that the wives are plotting, and wrong about what."},{"id":"623","who":"HOST","text":"He capers, he dances, he has eyes of youth, he writes verses, he speaks holiday, he smells April and May.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The Host's picture of Fenton is all youth and spring, unlike the other suitors."},{"id":"624","who":"PAGE","text":"he shall not knit a knot in his fortunes with the finger of my substance.","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Page will not let Fenton mend his fortunes with Page's wealth."},{"id":"624","who":"PAGE","text":"The wealth I have waits on my consent, and my consent goes not that way.","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Money is power in this marriage market, and Page holds it."},{"id":"660","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"we'll teach him to know turtles from jays.","themes":["honesty"],"note":"Turtle doves were faithful; jays were flashy and loose. The wives will prove which they are."},{"id":"662","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Why, now let me die, for I have lived long enough. This is the period of my ambition.","themes":["desire"],"note":"Falstaff's grand language for a sordid meeting."},{"id":"664","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I would thy husband were dead. I'll speak it before the best lord: I would make thee my lady.","themes":["desire","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff's empty promises."},{"id":"670","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I cannot cog and say thou art this and that, like a many of these lisping hawthorn buds","themes":["desire","language"],"note":"He claims to be too honest to flatter while flattering."},{"id":"693","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Look, here is a basket. If he be of any reasonable stature, he may creep in here","themes":["trickery"],"note":"The basket: the wives' prepared trick, presented as a sudden idea."},{"id":"695","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Let me see 't, let me see 't! O, let me see 't! I'll in, I'll in.","themes":["shame","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff's panic makes him walk into the trap himself."},{"id":"708","who":"FORD","text":"Buck? I would I could wash myself of the buck!","themes":["jealousy","language"],"note":"Ford's mind turns 'buck-washing' into the horns of a cuckold."},{"id":"719","who":"MISTRESS FORD","text":"I know not which pleases me better, that my husband is deceived, or Sir John.","themes":["jealousy","trickery","honesty"],"note":"The trick punishes two men at once: the lecher and the jealous husband."},{"id":"724","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"His dissolute disease will scarce obey this medicine.","themes":["desire","shame"],"note":"Lust is a disease, and the wives are the doctors."},{"id":"738","who":"PAGE","text":"What spirit, what devil suggests this imagination?","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"To Page, Ford's jealousy looks like possession."},{"id":"757","who":"FENTON","text":"He doth object I am too great of birth, And that my state being galled with my expense, I seek to heal it only by his wealth.","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Fenton states Page's objections fairly before answering them."},{"id":"759","who":"FENTON","text":"Yet, wooing thee, I found thee of more value Than stamps in gold or sums in sealed bags.","themes":["marriage","money"],"note":"Fenton admits money first drew him, and says love replaced it: the play's one honest suitor."},{"id":"768","who":"ANNE","text":"O, what a world of vile ill-favoured faults Looks handsome in three hundred pounds a year!","themes":["money","marriage"],"note":"Anne sees exactly why her father likes Slender."},{"id":"783","who":"SLENDER","text":"My will? 'Od's heartlings, that's a pretty jest indeed! I ne'er made my will yet","themes":["language","marriage"],"note":"Slender hears 'will' as a legal will, not desire: he cannot even understand a courtship question."},{"id":"785","who":"SLENDER","text":"Truly, for mine own part I would little or nothing with you.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The most honest and least romantic proposal in the play."},{"id":"799","who":"ANNE","text":"Alas, I had rather be set quick i' th' earth, And bowled to death with turnips.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Anne's comic horror at marrying Caius shows her spirit."},{"id":"816","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Have I lived to be carried in a basket like a barrow of butcher's offal, and to be thrown in the Thames?","themes":["shame","desire"],"note":"Falstaff's outrage is funny because he describes himself as meat."},{"id":"816","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"you may know by my size that I have a kind of alacrity in sinking","themes":["shame","desire","language"],"note":"He turns his own humiliation into a joke about his weight."},{"id":"857","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Think of that—hissing hot—think of that, Master Brook.","themes":["shame","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff tells his torture to the very man who wants him caught."},{"id":"863","who":"FORD","text":"If I have horns to make one mad, let the proverb go with me: I'll be horn-mad.","themes":["jealousy","language"],"note":"Ford embraces the cuckold's horns and the madness together."},{"id":"907","who":"MISTRESS QUICKLY","text":"He teaches him to hick and to hack, which they'll do fast enough of themselves","themes":["language"],"note":"Quickly turns Latin grammar into bawdy, the play's comedy of mishearing at its height."},{"id":"933","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"your husband is in his old lunes again.","themes":["jealousy"],"note":"Ford's jealousy is a recurring madness, and the wives can predict it."},{"id":"942","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"No, I'll come no more i' the basket.","themes":["shame"],"note":"The basket has taught Falstaff one lesson."},{"id":"953","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Good hearts, devise something. Any extremity rather than a mischief.","themes":["shame","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff will do anything, even dress as a woman, to escape."},{"id":"967","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Wives may be merry and yet honest too.","themes":["honesty"],"note":"The line that gives the play its meaning: fun and faithfulness go together."},{"id":"967","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"'Tis old but true: \"Still swine eats all the draff.\"","themes":["honesty"],"note":"The quiet ones are the ones to watch; the merry wives are honest."},{"id":"976","who":"FORD","text":"Youth in a basket! O you panderly rascals!","themes":["jealousy","shame"],"note":"Ford's second search, in front of witnesses, shames him."},{"id":"999","who":"FORD","text":"\"As jealous as Ford, that searched a hollow walnut for his wife's leman.\"","themes":["jealousy","shame"],"note":"Ford imagines himself as a proverb, and almost becomes one."},{"id":"1008","who":"FORD","text":"Out of my door, you witch, you rag, you baggage, you polecat, you runnion!","themes":["jealousy","shame","language"],"note":"Ford's anger finds a target at last, and it is Falstaff in a dress."},{"id":"1013","who":"EVANS","text":"I like not when a 'oman has a great peard.","themes":["trickery","language"],"note":"Evans sees through the disguise, though nobody listens."},{"id":"1019","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"I'll have the cudgel hallowed and hung o'er the altar. It hath done meritorious service.","themes":["shame","honesty"],"note":"The wives enjoy the beating as a holy act."},{"id":"1023","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"if it be but to scrape the figures out of your husband's brains.","themes":["jealousy","honesty"],"note":"Telling the husbands is meant to cure Ford."},{"id":"1039","who":"FORD","text":"I rather will suspect the sun with cold Than thee with wantonness.","themes":["jealousy","honesty","shame"],"note":"Ford's apology, in verse: he turns his jealousy into a vow of trust."},{"id":"1040","who":"PAGE","text":"Be not as extreme in submission as in offence.","themes":["shame","jealousy"],"note":"Page's sensible advice: don't swing from one extreme to the other."},{"id":"1046","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"There is an old tale goes that Herne the hunter","themes":["trickery"],"note":"A local ghost story becomes the frame for the final trick."},{"id":"1057","who":"PAGE","text":"And in that time Shall Master Slender steal my Nan away","themes":["marriage","trickery"],"note":"While planning one trick, Page plots another against his own wife."},{"id":"1064","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"He, none but he, shall have her, Though twenty thousand worthier come to crave her.","themes":["marriage","money"],"note":"Mistress Page admits she prefers money and influence to worth."},{"id":"1097","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"one that hath taught me more wit than ever I learned before in my life","themes":["shame"],"note":"Falstaff jokes that his beating was an education."},{"id":"1115","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I would all the world might be cozened, for I have been cozened and beaten too.","themes":["shame","trickery"],"note":"The swindled swindler wants company."},{"id":"1115","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"they would melt me out of my fat drop by drop, and liquor fishermen's boots with me.","themes":["shame","desire"],"note":"He fears public mockery more than beating."},{"id":"1121","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I was beaten myself into all the colours of the rainbow","themes":["shame"],"note":"His comic self-pity."},{"id":"1130","who":"FENTON","text":"Tonight at Herne's oak, just 'twixt twelve and one, Must my sweet Nan present the Fairy Queen","themes":["marriage","trickery"],"note":"Anne's own plan hides inside the adults' plans."},{"id":"1138","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"They say there is divinity in odd numbers, either in nativity, chance, or death.","themes":["trickery"],"note":"The third time will be the last, but not as Falstaff hopes."},{"id":"1152","who":"PAGE","text":"No man means evil but the devil, and we shall know him by his horns.","themes":["trickery","shame"],"note":"The horns, a sign of the cuckold, now mark the would-be adulterer."},{"id":"1160","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Better a little chiding than a great deal of heartbreak.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Mistress Page thinks she is saving Anne from a bad match; Anne will save herself."},{"id":"1166","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Against such lewdsters and their lechery, Those that betray them do no treachery.","themes":["honesty","trickery"],"note":"The wives' couplet justifies every trick."},{"id":"1175","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"O powerful love, that in some respects, makes a beast a man, in some other a man a beast!","themes":["desire"],"note":"Falstaff in a buck's head describes himself exactly."},{"id":"1175","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"For me, I am here a Windsor stag, and the fattest, I think, i' the forest.","themes":["desire","shame"],"note":"Horned and fat, he has become the very image of the play's jokes."},{"id":"1205","who":"FAIRIES","text":"Fie on lust and luxury!","themes":["desire","shame"],"note":"The fairies' song turns punishment into a ritual against lust."},{"id":"1209","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"Now, good Sir John, how like you Windsor wives?","themes":["honesty","shame"],"note":"Mistress Page's triumph: the town has beaten the courtier."},{"id":"1214","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass.","themes":["shame"],"note":"Falstaff's admission is the comic climax."},{"id":"1216","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"See now how wit may be made a Jack-a-Lent when 'tis upon ill employment!","themes":["shame","trickery"],"note":"Falstaff learns that cleverness used for bad ends makes a fool of you."},{"id":"1220","who":"FORD","text":"I will never mistrust my wife again, till thou art able to woo her in good English.","themes":["jealousy","language"],"note":"Ford's promise is also a joke at Evans's expense."},{"id":"1223","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Have I lived to stand at the taunt of one that makes fritters of English?","themes":["language","shame"],"note":"The worst humiliation for Falstaff, master of words, is to be mocked by Evans."},{"id":"1224","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"do you think, though we would have thrust virtue out of our hearts by the head and shoulders","themes":["honesty","desire"],"note":"Even if they had wanted to sin, it would never have been with Falstaff."},{"id":"1232","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Well, I am your theme. You have the start of me.","themes":["shame"],"note":"Falstaff gives in with good grace."},{"id":"1234","who":"PAGE","text":"Yet be cheerful, knight. Thou shalt eat a posset tonight at my house","themes":["shame"],"note":"Page offers forgiveness and food: the community takes Falstaff back."},{"id":"1241","who":"SLENDER","text":"I came yonder at Eton to marry Mistress Anne Page, and she's a great lubberly boy.","themes":["marriage","trickery"],"note":"The trickers are tricked: Page's plan ends in farce."},{"id":"1257","who":"FENTON","text":"Th' offence is holy that she hath committed","themes":["marriage","honesty"],"note":"Fenton defends Anne's disobedience as a holy act: a forced marriage would have been the real sin."},{"id":"1257","who":"FENTON","text":"A thousand irreligious cursed hours, Which forced marriage would have brought upon her.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The play's argument against marriage for money."},{"id":"1258","who":"FORD","text":"In love, the heavens themselves do guide the state. Money buys lands, and wives are sold by fate.","themes":["marriage","money"],"note":"Ford, who once paid to test his wife, now says love cannot be bought."},{"id":"1259","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I am glad, though you have ta'en a special stand to strike at me, that your arrow hath glanced.","themes":["shame"],"note":"Falstaff enjoys seeing the parents fooled too."},{"id":"1260","who":"PAGE","text":"What cannot be eschewed must be embraced.","themes":["marriage","shame"],"note":"Page accepts what he cannot change."},{"id":"1262","who":"MISTRESS PAGE","text":"let us every one go home, And laugh this sport o'er by a country fire, Sir John and all.","themes":["shame","honesty"],"note":"The ending includes everyone, even Falstaff, in laughter rather than punishment."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Sir John Falstaff, fat, old and out of money, is staying at the Garter Inn in Windsor. He decides to seduce two respectable wives, Mistress Ford and Mistress Page, because they control their husbands' money, and sends them identical love letters. The wives compare letters and plan revenge: they will lead him on and make a fool of him, without ever being unfaithful.","Ford, warned by Falstaff's sacked followers, is wildly jealous. Disguised as 'Master Brook', he pays Falstaff to seduce his wife and hears about every meeting in advance. Falstaff is tipped into the Thames in a laundry basket, then beaten while disguised as an old woman. Ford searches his house twice and finds nothing.","At the same time three suitors want Anne Page: foolish Slender, backed by her father; the French Doctor Caius, backed by her mother; and Fenton, whom she loves. A false duel, a stolen-horse trick and a Latin lesson add to the comedy.","When the wives reveal everything, Ford begs forgiveness and the whole town plans a final trick. At midnight Falstaff, wearing a buck's head as Herne the hunter, is pinched and burned by children dressed as fairies, and mocked by everyone. In the confusion Slender and Caius each run off with a boy in disguise, and Anne marries Fenton. Her parents accept the match, and everyone, Falstaff included, goes home to laugh by the fire."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Shallow accuses Falstaff of poaching and assault","id":52},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Evans proposes a match between Slender and Anne Page","id":21},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Falstaff reveals his plan to woo the two wives","id":189},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Pistol and Nym refuse to carry the letters","id":200},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Caius challenges Evans to a duel","id":270},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Quickly promises Fenton that Anne loves him","id":286},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The wives discover their letters are identical","id":311},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Pistol and Nym tell Ford and Page","id":327},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Ford arranges to meet Falstaff as Brook","id":376},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Quickly brings Mistress Ford's invitation","id":416},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Ford, as Brook, pays Falstaff to seduce his wife","id":467},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Ford's jealous soliloquy","id":480},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Caius waits in vain for Evans","id":488},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The Host reveals his trick and stops the duel","id":585},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Caius and Evans plot revenge on the Host","id":591},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Ford decides to search his house","id":613},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Falstaff woos Mistress Ford","id":664},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Falstaff hides in the laundry basket","id":695},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Ford searches and finds nothing","id":728},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Fenton tells Anne he loves her for herself","id":759},{"s":"a3s5","t":"Falstaff tells Brook about the basket","id":857},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Evans tests William's Latin","id":877},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Falstaff escapes as the old woman of Brentford","id":955},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Ford beats the 'witch' out of his house","id":1008},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Ford begs his wife's pardon","id":1039},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Mistress Page tells the tale of Herne the hunter","id":1046},{"s":"a4s5","t":"The Host loses his horses to the 'Germans'","id":1101},{"s":"a4s6","t":"Fenton plans to marry Anne during the masque","id":1132},{"s":"a5s5","t":"The fairies pinch and burn Falstaff","id":1199},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Falstaff admits he has been made an ass","id":1214},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Slender and Caius find they have married boys","id":1241},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Fenton and Anne return married","id":1257}],"irony":{"55":"Falstaff laughs off Shallow's complaint: by the end he will be the one shamed in public.","189":"Falstaff reads Mistress Ford's friendliness as desire; the audience will soon see her laugh at his letter.","317":"Mistress Page says she gives Page no cause for jealousy: true, but she is about to deceive him over Anne.","361":"Page trusts his wife in this, and is right; he trusts his plans for Anne, and is wrong.","447":"'Brook' says money opens all ways: Falstaff takes his money and opens his own trap.","476":"Falstaff calls Ford a cuckoldly rogue to Ford's face.","478":"Falstaff boasts he will 'awe' Ford with his cudgel; Ford will beat him with one in 4.2.","585":"The Host boasts of his cunning; Evans and Caius will make him their next victim.","624":"Page is sure his consent controls Anne's marriage; she marries without it.","712":"Ford promises 'sport anon' to his friends: the sport is at his expense.","742":"Ford promises to explain his behaviour later: it is his wife who will explain.","859":"Falstaff swears he will not give up 'Ford's wife' to the man who is Ford.","861":"'Master Brook, you shall cuckold Ford': he promises Ford he will cuckold himself.","1008":"Ford finally lands blows on Falstaff, without knowing it is him.","1013":"Evans spots the beard but nobody listens.","1057":"Page plots secretly while helping his wife's plot; she is doing the same to him.","1152":"'We shall know him by his horns': the horns are on Falstaff's head this time, not Ford's.","1263":"Ford's last joke: Brook will sleep with Mistress Ford, because Brook is Ford."},"devices":{"horns":"Horns, deer and cuckolds","food":"Food, fat and drink","sea":"Ships, sailing and trade","fire":"Fire, melting and burning","water":"Water, washing and drowning","beasts":"Beasts and birds"},"dev":{"189":[["food","His waist and his 'thrift' make his body a joke about consumption.","I am in the waist two yards about"]],"198":[["sea","The wives are colonies, to be traded with like the Indies.","they shall be my East and West Indies"]],"201":[["sea","His small page is a pinnace sailing to golden shores.","Sail like my pinnace to these golden shores"]],"298":[["food","Falstaff's guts 'made of puddings': fat as stuffing.","his guts are made of puddings"]],"310":[["fire","Lust will melt him in his own grease.","melted him in his own grease"],["sea","He is a whale washed ashore with tuns of oil.","threw this whale"]],"314":[["sea","'Boarding' a ship and accosting a woman: she will keep him above deck.","I'll be sure to keep him above deck"]],"315":[["sea","If he gets under her hatches she will never go to sea again.","If he come under my hatches"]],"327":[["horns","Actaeon, turned into a stag, wears the cuckold's horns.","like Sir Actaeon he"]],"329":[["horns","The horn and the cuckoo both mean a betrayed husband.","The horn, I say"]],"389":[["sea","The world as an oyster, prised open by force.","the world's mine oyster"]],"429":[["sea","Quickly is a ship to be captured, with fights and sails.","Clap on more sails"]],"480":[["food","He would sooner trust a Fleming with butter than his wife with herself.","trust a Fleming with my butter"]],"613":[["horns","Page as Actaeon, a willing cuckold.","secure and wilful Actaeon"]],"660":[["food","Falstaff as a watery pumpkin.","this gross watery pumpion"],["beasts","Turtle doves are faithful, jays are flashy.","turtles from jays"]],"708":[["horns","Buck-washing and the horned buck.","Buck, buck, buck!"]],"710":[["beasts","The hunt for the fox in his den.","we'll unkennel the fox"]],"816":[["water","Thrown into the Thames like a blind bitch's puppies.","to be thrown in the Thames"],["food","Carried like butcher's offal.","like a barrow of butcher's offal"]],"857":[["fire","Stewed in grease, then cooled like a horseshoe.","like a horseshoe"],["food","Half stewed like a Dutch dish.","like a Dutch dish"]],"863":[["horns","Horn-mad: angry and horned.","I'll be horn-mad"]],"1046":[["horns","Herne walks round the oak with great ragged horns.","with great ragged horns"]],"1115":[["fire","The court would melt him down drop by drop.","they would melt me out of my fat drop by drop"],["food","As crestfallen as a dried pear.","as crestfallen as a dried pear"]],"1175":[["horns","Jove the bull, Falstaff the Windsor stag.","I am here a Windsor stag"],["beasts","Love makes a beast a man and a man a beast.","makes a beast a man"]],"1197":[["food","Falstaff fears being turned into cheese.","transform me to a piece of cheese"]],"1205":[["fire","Lust is a bloody fire, punished by candles.","Lust is but a bloody fire"]],"1215":[["beasts","An ass and an ox: fool and horned beast.","and an ox too"]],"1225":[["food","A hodge-pudding, a bag of flax: Falstaff as stuffing.","a hodge-pudding"]],"1261":[["horns","Night dogs chase all kinds of deer.","all sorts of deer are chased"]]},"stage":{"street":"A street in Windsor","house":"Outside Page's house","inn":"The Garter Inn","room":"A room in a Windsor house","field":"Fields near Windsor","park":"Windsor Park","oak":"Herne's oak in Windsor Park, at midnight"},"why":["Shows Shallow's pride in rank, which Falstaff ignores.","Anne is first described by her money.","Turns 'good gifts' into cash.","Falstaff's shameless confidence at the start.","Slender's muddled words show a loveless match.","His slip undoes the marriage he agrees to.","Slender's idea of courtship is bear-baiting.","Falstaff treats theft as an art.","Polite words cover dishonest acts.","Falstaff's motive is need, not love.","His body and his debts become one joke.","Falstaff misreads women through vanity.","The money behind the wooing.","Love talk turns into trade talk.","Even Falstaff's men refuse him.","Plants Ford's jealousy.","Quickly's comic logic.","Quickly's promises to every suitor.","The wives' anger is comic and general.","Revenge aimed at his body.","Fat as a moral sign.","The punishment fits the fat.","The identical letters unite the wives.","Turns a sexist joke back on men.","Sets out the wives' plan.","Sets the rule: tricks, but no infidelity.","Ford's jealousy is known before he appears.","Page's trust as the healthy model.","Pistol's warning starts Ford's suspicion.","Introduces the cuckold's horns.","Page's trust contrasted with Ford.","Ford's suspicion in his own words.","Ford's spying satisfies him either way.","Shows a world where people take by force.","Falstaff admits his honour bends to need.","Quickly's muddled messages.","Quickly's praise of the Pages' marriage.","Falstaff's motto of profit.","The trick of the false name.","Money as a key that opens people.","What really moves Falstaff.","A polished lie about love.","The play's image of adultery.","The husband pays to test his wife.","Falstaff confesses his motive to Ford himself.","The start of Ford's rage.","Thanks God for his own sickness.","Ford's haste becomes a proverb.","The word he fears most.","The Host's mocking nicknames.","The trickster's pride before his fall.","The foreigners as entertainment.","Rivals united against a mocker.","Ford right about plotting, wrong about its aim.","Fenton as youth and spring.","Page's control of Anne's fortune.","Money as power in marriage.","The wives will prove they are faithful.","Falstaff's grand words for a sordid aim.","Falstaff's empty promises.","Flattery dressed as plain speech.","The basket trick begins.","Falstaff walks into the trap.","Ford's obsession with horns.","Two men fooled at once.","Lust as a disease to be cured.","Ford's jealousy as possession.","Fenton states the case against himself.","Fenton's honest love.","Anne sees the price of her father's choice.","Slender misunderstands courtship.","The least romantic proposal.","Anne's spirit against her mother's choice.","Falstaff's humiliation as comedy.","Turns shame into a joke about weight.","Falstaff tells his torture to Ford.","Ford embraces the horns.","Bawdy mishearing in the Latin lesson.","Ford's jealousy is predictable.","Falstaff has learned one lesson.","Falstaff will do anything to escape.","The play's central idea.","Quiet people are the ones to watch.","Ford shamed in public.","Ford imagines himself as a byword.","Ford's rage hits the disguised Falstaff.","Evans sees through the disguise.","The beating as a holy act.","Telling the truth to cure Ford.","Ford's apology closes his plot.","Page warns against extremes.","The ghost story frames the last trick.","Page plots behind his wife's back.","Mistress Page prefers money and influence.","Falstaff's wit survives the beating.","The swindled swindler.","Fear of public mockery.","Comic self-pity.","Anne's plan inside the adults' plans.","The rule of three.","The horns now mark the lecher.","Mistress Page thinks she is protecting Anne.","The wives justify their tricks.","Falstaff describes himself as a beast.","Horned and fat: the image of the play.","The punishment of lust as ritual.","The town beats the courtier.","The comic climax.","Wit used for bad ends makes a fool.","Ford's promise and a joke on Evans.","The master of words mocked by Evans.","The wives would never have chosen him.","Falstaff gives in with good grace.","Forgiveness and food.","The trickers tricked.","Disobedience as a holy act.","The case against forced marriage.","Love cannot be bought.","Falstaff enjoys the parents' defeat.","Page accepts what he cannot change.","Everyone goes home together."],"arc":{"Falstaff's confidence":[5,5,9,9,9,10,10,10,10,4,4,8,8,3,3,3,2,4,7,7,7,8,1],"Ford's jealousy":[0,0,0,0,7,10,0,0,9,10,0,9,0,10,0,1,0,0,1,0,0,0,0]},"arcnote":"Falstaff's confidence climbs as he writes his letters and peaks when Quickly brings the first invitation and 'Brook' pays him (2.2). It dips after the basket, rises again when he is invited back (3.5), falls after the beating (4.5), and soars one last time at midnight in his buck's head before collapsing at Herne's oak. Ford's jealousy runs the other way: it flares when Pistol warns him (2.1), boils over in his soliloquy, drives two searches of his own house (3.3 and 4.2), and then drops to nothing when his wife tells him the truth in 4.4. The two lines cross because the wives use each man's folly to cure the other. Scores are judgements from the text, for discussion.","context":[["Date","Probably written in 1597, perhaps for the Garter feast held that April, when Shakespeare's patron Lord Hunsdon was made a Knight of the Garter. A shorter, garbled version was printed in 1602; the fuller text appeared in the First Folio of 1623."],["Falstaff","Falstaff first appears in Henry IV, Part 1 as the drinking companion of Prince Hal. This play brings him, with Pistol, Nym, Bardolph and Mistress Quickly, into a separate comic world. Fenton's past with 'the wild Prince and Poins' links the two."],["The Queen's command","A story first recorded in 1702 says Queen Elizabeth wanted to see Falstaff in love, and that Shakespeare wrote the play in fourteen days. No evidence from the 1590s supports it, but it has been repeated ever since."],["The Order of the Garter","England's highest order of knighthood, centred on St George's Chapel at Windsor Castle. The Fairy Queen's speech in 5.5 praises the castle, the knights' stalls and the motto 'Honi soit qui mal y pense'."],["Windsor","The play is Shakespeare's only comedy set in the England of his own day. It names real places: the Garter Inn, Frogmore, Datchet Mead, Eton, Brentford and the roads to Reading and Maidenhead."],["Class and outsiders","Windsor is a town of prosperous householders. Everyone from outside is mocked: the Welsh parson and the French doctor for their English, Shallow and Slender for their airs, the courtly Falstaff for his debts and lechery, and the high-born Fenton for his spending. Yet the ending gathers them all at one fireside."],["Citizen comedy","The Fords and Pages are prosperous townsfolk, not courtiers. Comedies about London and town life, with clever wives and foolish husbands, became popular around 1600."],["Cuckolds and horns","A cuckold, a man whose wife was unfaithful, was said to grow horns. The joke was everywhere in Elizabethan comedy, and this play builds its last scene round a man wearing horns."],["Brook and Broome","In the Folio, Ford calls himself Brook; in the 1602 quarto he is Broome. The change may have been made because Brooke was the family name of Lord Cobham, a powerful nobleman, whose ancestor Oldcastle had already been mocked as the first version of Falstaff."],["The German duke","In 1592 a German count, Frederick of Mompelgard, visited Windsor with special permission to take post horses free. The 1602 text has a joke about 'cosen garmombles', so the stolen horses may glance at him."],["Herne the hunter","The legend of a ghostly keeper with horns who haunts an oak in Windsor Forest is first recorded in this play. Later writers treated Herne's oak as a real tree in the Little Park."],["Grammar school","William's Latin lesson in 4.1 follows Lily's Latin Grammar, the official textbook in every English grammar school, including the one in Stratford."],["Foreigners and accents","Evans is Welsh and Caius is French. Audiences enjoyed jokes about accents, but in the play the two foreigners get their revenge on the Englishman who mocks them."],["Marriage and dowries","Fathers arranged marriages for property, and a daughter's fortune made her valuable. Parents could not legally force a marriage, but most daughters obeyed. Anne's secret wedding argues for marriage based on love."],["Sources","There is no single source. Tales of a lover who confides in a jealous husband, hidden in laundry, appear in Italian story collections such as Ser Giovanni's Il Pecorone, and in English versions such as Tarlton's News out of Purgatory (1590)."]],"critics":{"desire":[["Samuel Johnson, notes to his edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson observed that Falstaff could not truly love without ceasing to be Falstaff; his wooing is a pretence driven by money, not pleasure, which is why it suits his character."]],"shame":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt enjoyed the play but felt that the Falstaff of Windsor is not the man of the Henry IV plays; here he is the butt of the jokes rather than their master."],["Northrop Frye, Anatomy of Criticism (1957)","Paraphrased: Frye saw Falstaff's punishment at Herne's oak as an old comic ritual, in which a scapegoat figure is driven out so that the community can be renewed."]],"trickery":[["John Dennis, The Comical Gallant (1702)","Paraphrased: Dennis, who rewrote the play, recorded the tradition that the Queen commanded it and wanted it finished in fourteen days, and admired the variety of its plot."]],"honesty":[["Peter Erickson, 'The Order of the Garter, the Cult of Elizabeth, and Class-Gender Tension in The Merry Wives of Windsor' (1987)","Paraphrased: Erickson argued that the wives' victory is real but limited: their wit protects their husbands' property and the town's order rather than changing women's place in it."]],"money":[["Natasha Korda, Shakespeare's Domestic Economies (2002)","Paraphrased: Korda reads the wives as managers of household goods; the laundry basket and the linen show their control of the home's property, which Falstaff wants to raid."]],"marriage":[["Leslie Hotson, Shakespeare versus Shallow (1931)","Paraphrased: Hotson argued that the play was written for the Garter feast of 1597, and that Shallow mocks a real Surrey justice, William Gardiner."]]},"insults":[{"id":"61","f":"You Banbury cheese!","who":"Bardolph","n":"Banbury cheeses were famously thin: a jibe at Slender's thinness."},{"id":"63","f":"How now, Mephostophilus?","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol calls Slender a devil, in his usual inflated style."},{"id":"77","f":"thou mountain-foreigner","who":"Pistol","n":"A sneer at Evans as a Welshman from the hills."},{"id":"81","f":"Scarlet and John","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff jokes about Bardolph's red face, naming two of Robin Hood's men."},{"id":"175","f":"O base Hungarian wight","who":"Pistol","n":"'Hungarian' sounds like 'hungry': Pistol mocks Bardolph's new job."},{"id":"203","f":"Base Phrygian Turk!","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol's grand insults sound like lines from a bad tragedy."},{"id":"270","f":"scurvy jackanape priest","who":"Caius","n":"Caius's rage against Evans, in broken English."},{"id":"298","f":"Flemish drunkard","who":"Mistress Page","n":"The Dutch were stereotyped as heavy drinkers."},{"id":"310","f":"this whale, with so many tuns of oil in his belly","who":"Mistress Ford","n":"The wives' insults always aim at Falstaff's size."},{"id":"319","f":"this greasy knight","who":"Mistress Page","n":"Grease and fat: a knight without honour."},{"id":"338","f":"such a Cataian","who":"Page","n":"Page dismisses Nym as a liar and scoundrel."},{"id":"478","f":"mechanical salt-butter rogue","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff mocks Ford as a lowly tradesman, unaware he is speaking to him."},{"id":"502","f":"Castalion King Urinal","who":"Host","n":"The Host's mock titles turn the doctor into a urine-pot."},{"id":"579","f":"de Jack dog, John ape","who":"Caius","n":"Caius's insults are as broken as his grammar."},{"id":"591","f":"scall, scurvy, cogging companion","who":"Evans","n":"Evans's alliterating list of insults for the Host."},{"id":"660","f":"this gross watery pumpion","who":"Mistress Ford","n":"Falstaff as a huge, wet pumpkin."},{"id":"976","f":"O you panderly rascals!","who":"Ford","n":"Ford accuses his own servants of being pimps."},{"id":"1008","f":"you polecat, you runnion","who":"Ford","n":"Ford's abuse of the 'witch' is the most violent language in the play."},{"id":"1223","f":"one that makes fritters of English","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff's last insult is about language: Evans chops English to bits."},{"id":"1225","f":"a hodge-pudding? A bag of flax?","who":"Ford","n":"The final pile-up of insults, as the whole town mocks Falstaff."},{"id":"1227","f":"Old, cold, withered, and of intolerable entrails","who":"Page","n":"Page lists everything wrong with Falstaff as a lover."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"198","f":"Go, bear thou this letter to Mistress Page","n":"Falstaff uses 'thou' to his followers: a master to servants."},{"id":"301","f":"trust me, I was coming to you","n":"The wives use 'you' to each other: polite equals and friends."},{"id":"343","f":"How now, sweet Frank, why art thou melancholy?","n":"Mistress Ford uses loving 'thou' to her husband."},{"id":"344","f":"Get you home, go.","n":"Ford answers coldly with 'you': his suspicion has entered the marriage."},{"id":"664","f":"I would thy husband were dead","n":"Falstaff uses intimate 'thou' to Mistress Ford, as a lover."},{"id":"665","f":"I your lady, Sir John?","n":"She keeps to 'your' and his title: she stays at a distance."},{"id":"1039","f":"Henceforth, do what thou wilt","n":"In his apology Ford returns to loving 'thou'."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Slender wins Anne at once","Others compete for Anne"],"a1s2":["Quickly helps Slender","Quickly refuses"],"a1s3":["Falstaff's men deliver the letters","Falstaff's men betray him"],"a1s4":["Caius and Evans fight","The duel goes wrong"],"a2s1":["The wives give in to Falstaff","The wives trick Falstaff"],"a2s2":["Falstaff learns who Brook is","Falstaff confides in Brook"],"a2s3":["Caius kills Evans","No one fights"],"a3s1":["The rivals become friends","The rivals keep fighting"],"a3s2":["Ford catches Falstaff","Falstaff escapes Ford"],"a3s3":["Falstaff gives up","Falstaff tries again"],"a3s4":["Anne obeys her parents","Anne makes her own choice"],"a3s5":["Falstaff refuses to go back","Falstaff goes back"],"a4s1":["William fails his test","The plot resumes at Ford's"],"a4s2":["Ford finds Falstaff in the basket","Falstaff escapes in disguise"],"a4s3":["The Germans pay","The Host is cheated"],"a4s4":["The husbands are told","The wives keep it secret"],"a4s5":["Falstaff stays away","Falstaff agrees to come"],"a4s6":["Anne goes with Slender","Anne goes with Fenton"],"a5s1":["Falstaff guesses the trick","Falstaff walks into the trap"],"a5s2":["Slender finds Anne","Slender finds the wrong person"],"a5s3":["Caius finds Anne","Caius is fooled"],"a5s4":["The fairies are ready","The trick is called off"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Ford and Page","pts":["Both are told by Falstaff's men that he is after their wives.","Page laughs it off and trusts his wife; Ford is sure he has been betrayed.","Both are fooled in the end: Ford by his jealousy, Page by his daughter."],"ids":[361,362]},{"t":"Mistress Ford and Mistress Page","pts":["Both get the same letter and plan the same revenge.","Mistress Ford has to manage a jealous husband; Mistress Page has a trusting one.","Mistress Page invents the tricks, but she also deceives her husband over Anne."],"ids":[316,315]},{"t":"Falstaff and Ford","pts":["Both chase Mistress Ford: one for money, one to test her.","Both are fooled by the wives, and both are shamed in public.","Falstaff uses a false message; Ford uses a false name."],"ids":[189,467]},{"t":"Fenton and Slender","pts":["Both want to marry Anne.","Slender has land but no love; Fenton has love but no money.","Fenton admits his first motive was money; Slender never pretends to love at all."],"ids":[759,785]},{"t":"Evans and Caius","pts":["Both are foreigners whose English is mocked.","Both are tricked by the Host and become friends.","Both get their revenge, through the stolen horses."],"ids":[580,592]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Merry Wives of Windsor"],["Written","Probably 1597; a short quarto printed in 1602; the full text in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy"],["Setting","Windsor and its neighbourhood: the Garter Inn, the houses of Ford, Page and Doctor Caius, the fields near Frogmore, and Windsor Park"],["Protagonists","Mistress Ford and Mistress Page"],["Antagonist","Sir John Falstaff (and Ford's jealousy)"],["Inciting incident","Falstaff sends identical love letters to the two wives (1.3, 2.1)"],["Turning point","Ford, as Brook, pays Falstaff to seduce his wife (2.2)"],["Climax","Falstaff is pinched and burned by the 'fairies' at Herne's oak and exposed (5.5)"],["Point of view","Shared, with soliloquies by Ford and Falstaff"],["Major conflicts","The wives against Falstaff; Ford's jealousy against his wife's honesty; Anne's choice against her parents' plans"],["Themes","Honesty and merriment, jealousy, money, desire, tricks and disguise, marriage, language, shame and forgiveness"]],"analysis":{"FALSTAFF":["Falstaff arrives in Windsor as a cheerful rogue who admits beating Shallow's men and killing his deer: 'I have done all this. That is now answered.' His plan to woo the wives comes from need ('I must cony-catch, I must shift') and from vanity, as he reads every look as desire.","His language is the richest in the play. He turns his own size into jokes ('I am in the waist two yards about') and makes his humiliations into comic performances, describing the basket and the Thames to 'Brook' as 'hissing hot'.","Unlike the Falstaff of the Henry IV plays, he is always the victim of the jokes here. The wives, the husbands, the Welsh parson and even the children outwit him. His admission 'I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass' is the comic climax.","The ending forgives him. Page invites him home, and he enjoys seeing the parents fooled too. Some readers find him a shadow of his earlier self; others see in his punishment an old ritual in which the town drives out its sins and then takes the sinner back."],"MISTRESS FORD":["Mistress Ford is the target of both men's plots: Falstaff's seduction and her husband's suspicion. She sets the rule for the wives: 'any villainy against him that may not sully the chariness of our honesty'.","She plays the willing lover with perfect timing, protesting that she fears Falstaff loves Mistress Page and leading him into each trap.","Her triumph is double: 'I know not which pleases me better, that my husband is deceived, or Sir John.' By proving her honesty in a way her husband can see, she cures his jealousy."],"MISTRESS PAGE":["Mistress Page is the chief inventor of the tricks: the alarm in 3.3, the witch of Brentford, and the story of Herne the hunter.","Her couplet 'Wives may be merry and yet honest too' gives the play its meaning: fun and faithfulness are not opposites.","Yet she is also a schemer at home. She secretly plans for Caius to carry Anne off, preferring his money and influence ('though twenty thousand worthier come to crave her'), and is outwitted by her own daughter."],"FORD":["Ford's jealousy is extreme from the start. He pays Falstaff, as Brook, to seduce his own wife, so that he can test her.","His soliloquies are the darkest speeches in the play ('See the hell of having a false woman'), but they are comic because the audience knows his wife is faithful. His obsession with horns turns every word, even 'buck', into a cuckold joke.","He is shamed twice in front of his friends. When the wives tell all, he apologises in verse ('I rather will suspect the sun with cold Than thee with wantonness') and joins the final trick. His last joke, that Brook will sleep with Mistress Ford, shows his cure."],"PAGE":["Page is Ford's opposite: he trusts his wife completely and mocks Ford's jealousy.","But he is a controlling father, set on Slender because of his land and refusing Fenton because 'the wealth I have waits on my consent'. He plots behind his wife's back to have Slender steal Anne away.","When Anne marries Fenton he accepts it gracefully: 'What cannot be eschewed must be embraced.' He also offers Falstaff supper, so he leads the play's forgiveness."],"MISTRESS QUICKLY":["Mistress Quickly is Windsor's go-between. She promises to help all three of Anne's suitors and says she knows Anne's mind better than anyone.","Her muddled words ('canaries', 'fartuous', 'erection' for 'direction') are a constant source of comedy, and she hears bawdy meanings even in William's Latin.","At Herne's oak she becomes the Queen of Fairies, speaking dignified verse in praise of Windsor Castle: a surprising change that shows how the masque transforms the town."],"ANNE":["Anne is the prize her parents and suitors argue over: her seven hundred pounds is mentioned before her name.","She speaks little but sharply: 'what a world of vile ill-favoured faults Looks handsome in three hundred pounds a year!' and she would rather be 'bowled to death with turnips' than marry Caius.","She agrees to both parents' plans and then follows her own, marrying Fenton during the masque. Her quiet trick is the last and best in the play."],"FENTON":["Fenton is the only suitor who speaks of love in verse. He admits Anne's money was his first motive but says he came to value her above 'stamps in gold'.","His defence of the secret marriage, that 'Th' offence is holy that she hath committed', turns disobedience into virtue, and wins over Ford and Page."]},"motifs":[["Horns and deer","Cuckolds' horns, Actaeon, the buck-basket, Herne's ragged horns and Falstaff as a Windsor stag.",[29,63,76,100,104]],["Fat and food","Falstaff's waist, his grease, puddings, pumpkins, butter and cheese.",[10,19,20,21,74,103]],["Water and washing","The buck-basket, Datchet Mead, the Thames and Falstaff's soaking.",[61,62,73,74,75]],["Letters and messages","The identical letters, Quickly's messages and Anne's letter to Fenton.",[11,22,35,98]],["Disguise","Brook, the witch of Brentford, Herne the hunter, the fairies and the boys dressed as Anne.",[38,80,86,91,114]],["Money and trade","The Indies, angels, bags of money and dowries.",[1,12,13,39,40,56]]],"symbols2":[["The buck-basket","The laundry basket that carries Falstaff to the Thames: dirty linen for a dirty plan, and the household the wives control."],["The horns","Worn by the cuckold in Ford's fears, by Herne in the legend, and finally by Falstaff: the shame he hoped to give is put on his own head."],["The letters","Two identical love letters prove Falstaff's love is a sham and turn two wives into allies."],["Herne's oak","An old haunted tree in Windsor Park: the place where folk belief, the town and the court meet for the final trick."],["White and green","The colours of Anne's costumes in her parents' plans. Both parents lose because their sign can be worn by anyone."],["The cudgel","Ford's stick, used on the 'witch of Brentford': the wives would hang it over the altar."]],"bigq":[["Is Falstaff a villain or a victim?",["He plans adultery for money and boasts about it","He is beaten, soaked and mocked three times","His wit makes even his shame entertaining","The town forgives him and takes him home"],[3,13,73,107,113]],["Are the wives too cruel?",["They act only to protect their honour","Ford and Falstaff both deserve a lesson","The beating and the burning are harsh","They enjoy the pain they cause"],[25,64,87,102]],["Does Ford really change?",["He apologises fully and joins the plan","His last joke shows he can laugh at himself","His jealousy was cured only by a public show","Mistress Page says his fits come back"],[45,78,89,109]],["Who is the real hero of the play?",["The wives, who outwit everyone","Anne, who outwits even them","Fenton, who marries for love","Windsor itself, which forgives everyone"],[81,115,117,120]]],"symbols":{"Love":"\\blov(e|es|ed|er|ing)\\b","Money":"\\bmoney\\b|\\bgold\\b|\\bangels?\\b|\\bpounds?\\b","Jealousy":"\\bjealous(y|ies)?\\b","Horns":"\\bhorns?\\b|\\bcuckold","Honest":"\\bhonest(y)?\\b","Basket":"\\bbasket\\b"},"stagerules":[["garter","inn"],["another part","oak"],["park","park"],["field","field"],["before","house"],["room","room"],["street","street"]],"solo":{"480":1,"613":1,"816":1,"863":1,"1115":1,"1175":1},"iago":[["a1s3","Falstaff sends identical love letters to Mistress Ford and Mistress Page."],["a2s1","The wives compare the letters and agree to lead him on without staining their honour."],["a2s2","They send Mistress Quickly to invite Falstaff to Ford's house between ten and eleven."],["a3s3","Mistress Ford receives Falstaff; Mistress Page raises the alarm that Ford is coming."],["a3s3","Falstaff is hidden in the buck-basket under dirty linen and carried out past Ford."],["a3s3","The basket is tipped into the muddy ditch by the Thames."],["a3s5","Quickly brings a second invitation, between eight and nine."],["a4s2","Mistress Page warns again; Falstaff refuses the basket."],["a4s2","The wives dress him as the fat woman of Brentford, and Ford beats him out of the house."],["a4s4","The wives tell their husbands, and everyone plans the Herne's oak trick."],["a4s5","Quickly carries the third invitation."],["a5s5","Falstaff waits at the oak in a buck's head; the wives run off at a noise of horns."],["a5s5","The 'fairies' test him with fire, pinch him and sing against lust."],["a5s5","The husbands and wives reveal the trick and mock him: 'how like you Windsor wives?'"]],"rel":{"1":[["SHALLOW","FALSTAFF","hate"],["EVANS","SLENDER","trust"],["SLENDER","ANNE","love"],["CAIUS","EVANS","hate"],["CAIUS","ANNE","love"],["FENTON","ANNE","love"],["FALSTAFF","MISTRESS FORD","deceive"],["PISTOL","FALSTAFF","hate"]],"2":[["MISTRESS FORD","MISTRESS PAGE","trust"],["MISTRESS FORD","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["FORD","MISTRESS FORD","hate"],["PAGE","MISTRESS PAGE","trust"],["FORD","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["HOST","CAIUS","deceive"]],"3":[["HOST","EVANS","deceive"],["CAIUS","EVANS","trust"],["MISTRESS PAGE","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["FENTON","ANNE","love"],["ANNE","FENTON","love"],["PAGE","FENTON","hate"],["MISTRESS QUICKLY","FENTON","trust"]],"4":[["FORD","FALSTAFF","hate"],["FORD","MISTRESS FORD","trust"],["MISTRESS PAGE","CAIUS","trust"],["PAGE","SLENDER","trust"],["ANNE","PAGE","deceive"],["ANNE","MISTRESS PAGE","deceive"],["FENTON","HOST","trust"]],"5":[["MISTRESS QUICKLY","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["EVANS","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["FENTON","ANNE","love"],["PAGE","FALSTAFF","trust"],["FORD","MISTRESS FORD","love"],["ANNE","SLENDER","deceive"],["ANNE","CAIUS","deceive"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Falstaff?",[3,10,11,73,107,112]],["How does Shakespeare present jealousy?",[26,31,45,46,76,89]],["How does Shakespeare present the merry wives?",[22,25,57,64,81,106]],["How does Shakespeare present marriage and money?",[1,55,68,69,93,117]],["How does Shakespeare present tricks and disguise?",[24,38,61,80,98,102]],["How does Shakespeare present language and foreigners?",[5,8,51,52,77,110]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, pun, prose, dramatic irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, for example of Falstaff's punishment","Referred to the whole play, including the ending","Checked spelling of names: Falstaff, Mistress Quickly, Caius, Evans"],"place":{"a1s1":"Outside Page's house","a1s2":"Outside Page's house","a1s3":"The Garter Inn","a1s4":"Doctor Caius's house","a2s1":"Outside Page's house","a2s2":"The Garter Inn","a2s3":"A field near Windsor","a3s1":"A field near Frogmore","a3s2":"A street in Windsor","a3s3":"Ford's house","a3s4":"Page's house","a3s5":"The Garter Inn","a4s1":"A street in Windsor","a4s2":"Ford's house","a4s3":"The Garter Inn","a4s4":"Ford's house","a4s5":"The Garter Inn","a4s6":"The Garter Inn","a5s1":"The Garter Inn","a5s2":"Windsor Park","a5s3":"A street in Windsor","a5s4":"Windsor Park","a5s5":"Herne's oak"},"models":[[81,"Shakespeare presents the wives' tricks as proof that a woman's sense of fun is no threat to her faithfulness.","The rhyme of 'merry' with 'honest too' is not a contrast but a balance: 'and yet' admits that people expect the two to clash.","The couplet closes a scene, so it sounds like a proverb, a moral the audience is meant to take home.","In a culture where a wife's jokes and freedom could be read as signs of looseness, the line defends middle-class women's reputation.","Some readers note that the wives' victory protects their husbands' honour and property rather than changing their own position."],[45,"Shakespeare presents Ford's jealousy as a torment that is real to him and comic to the audience.","'Hell' is the strongest word Ford could choose: he imagines himself damned by a betrayal that has not happened.","The soliloquy lets us hear his mind directly, while dramatic irony tells us his wife is innocent.","Elizabethan comedy made the jealous husband a stock figure of fun, and the cuckold's horns a universal joke.","An alternative view is that Ford's pain is close to Othello's, and that the comedy depends on the wives' cleverness to keep it from turning dark."],[107,"Shakespeare presents Falstaff's exposure as the moment when the great wit finally sees himself clearly.","'Begin to perceive' is slow and grudging: understanding comes late to a man who thought he saw through everyone.","'Ass' is both fool and a beast, completing the animal imagery of horns and stags.","The scene recalls folk rituals in which a horned figure is mocked and driven out before being welcomed back.","Some audiences feel sorry for him here; others feel he has earned every pinch."],[69,"Shakespeare presents Anne as a clear-eyed young woman who sees how money disguises a man's faults.","The contrast between 'vile ill-favoured faults' and 'handsome' shows money acting like make-up.","The exact figure, 'three hundred pounds a year', makes love sound like an account book.","Fathers arranged matches for property, so Anne's aside voices a criticism the audience may have shared.","Her later trick shows she does more than complain: she acts."],[13,"Shakespeare presents Falstaff's desire as greed disguised as love.","'East and West Indies' turns two Windsor housewives into foreign lands full of gold.","'Trade' is a merchant's word: love has become business.","In the 1590s explorers' reports of Guiana and the Indies made such wealth a popular dream.","Some critics see in this a satire on the age's colonial greed as well as on Falstaff."],[115,"Shakespeare presents Anne's secret marriage as a holy disobedience.","The paradox 'offence is holy' asks the audience to judge by love rather than by rules.","Fenton speaks in verse, raising the tone above the prose of the farce around him.","Church teaching held that marriage needed free consent, so a forced match could be called a sin.","Her parents accept it at once, which suggests the play wants the audience to agree."]],"lang":{"un":[["624","The wealth I have waits on my consent, and my consent goes not that way.","My wealth depends on my consent, and my consent does not go that way."],["1039","I rather will suspect the sun with cold Than thee with wantonness.","I would sooner suspect the sun of being cold than you of being unfaithful."],["1258","Money buys lands, and wives are sold by fate.","Money buys land, but fate decides who marries whom."],["1260","What cannot be eschewed must be embraced.","What cannot be avoided must be accepted."]],"pun":[["189","I am in the waist two yards about, but I am now about no waste","waist","pun","His waist (his size) and waste (spending money)."],["708","I would I could wash myself of the buck!","buck","pun","Buck-washing (laundry) and a buck (a horned male deer, so a cuckold)."],["829","I have had ford enough. I was thrown into the ford","ford","pun","Mistress Ford and a ford, a shallow river crossing."],["1175","think on't, Jove, a foul fault!","foul","pun","Foul (wicked) and fowl (a bird, the swan Jove became)."],["1177","my deer, my male deer?","deer","pun","Dear (darling) and deer, as he wears a buck's head."],["1213","I will always count you my deer.","deer","pun","Dear and deer: she mocks his horns."]],"st":[["967","Wives may be merry and yet honest too.",[["Wives","The play's subject: married women, not young lovers."],["merry","Fun, jokes and freedom."],["honest","Faithful: in this period the word meant sexual virtue."]]],["480","God be praised for my jealousy!",[["praised","Religious thanks for a sin."],["jealousy","The sickness he is proud of."]]],["1214","I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass.",[["begin","Understanding comes slowly."],["perceive","The great observer has failed to see."],["ass","Fool and beast, ending the animal imagery."]]]]},"dir":[["298","Ask me no reason why I love you","How should Mistress Page read Falstaff's letter aloud?",[["In a mock-romantic voice","She is already laughing at him."],["Slowly, with growing anger","The insult sinks in line by line."],["Flattered at first, then outraged","She is briefly tempted before she sees the trick."]]],["480","See the hell of having a false woman","How should Ford speak his soliloquy?",[["Raging, almost tragic","His pain is real, which makes the irony sharper."],["Comically, hopping with fury","He is a stock jealous husband for laughs."],["Quietly, coldly","His jealousy is a calculating obsession."]]],["695","I'll in, I'll in.","How should Falstaff get into the basket?",[["Scrambling and squeezing in","Physical farce: his size is the joke."],["With dignity, then collapsing","His pride is punctured."],["Desperately, terrified","He really fears Ford's violence."]]],["1008","Out of my door, you witch","How hard should Ford beat the 'old woman'?",[["Wildly, with real force","His rage finally bursts out."],["Comically, with slapstick blows","The audience should laugh, not wince."],["Hesitantly, then fully","He is not sure, then his anger takes over."]]],["1214","I do begin to perceive that I am made an ass.","How should Falstaff say this?",[["Slowly, with dignity","He accepts defeat like a gentleman."],["Ruefully, laughing at himself","He enjoys the joke even against himself."],["Bitterly","He is genuinely hurt and humiliated."]]],["1257","Th' offence is holy that she hath committed","How should Fenton face Anne's parents?",[["Boldly, holding Anne's hand","Love wins openly."],["Humbly, kneeling","He wants their blessing."],["Laughing","He enjoys outwitting them."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["1195","1056"],["624","389","1214"],["1214","1224"],["1195","1056"],["67","703","1149"],["2","1220","1223"],["311","316","1209"],["708","1220","1039"],["438","476"],["1029","1102"],["1046","1048","1143"],["874","880","907"],["369","583","1223"],["21","25","768"],["703","999","853"]],"cr":{"desire":[["183","192","431"]],"shame":[["1214","1097","1175"],["1214","1205","1234"]],"trickery":[["316","1040","1048"]],"honesty":[["316","1209","1224"]],"money":[["192","703","863"]],"marriage":[["1195","1257","21"]]}}};
